-- Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab) textbook MCQs, chapter 13 (Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation)) BIO-0001 to BIO-0042, chapter 14 (Human Urinary System) BIO-0043 to BIO-0092, chapter 15 (Human Nervous System) BIO-0093 to BIO-0186, chapter 16 (Human Endocrine System) BIO-0187 to BIO-0246, chapter 17 (Human Reproductive Systems) BIO-0247 to BIO-0281, chapter 18 (Inheritance) BIO-0282 to BIO-0358, chapter 19 (Chromosome and DNA) BIO-0359 to BIO-0439, chapter 20 (Biotechnology) BIO-0440 to BIO-0476, chapter 21 (Immunity) BIO-0477 to BIO-0530, chapter 22 (Biostatistics) BIO-0531 to BIO-0569, chapter 23 (Pharmacology) BIO-0570 to BIO-0593, chapter 24 (Evolution) BIO-0594 to BIO-0632, chapter 25 (Ecology) BIO-0633 to BIO-0678.
-- Original questions in the bank's template (stem, four options, key, why the key is right, why each other option is
-- wrong) with the source page in the textbook. Approved for the bank by the site owner, so they go straight to live.
-- Each question's own chapter is its MDCAT chapter (NUMS and AKU reuse it); FSc Part 2 lists them in bank_questions
-- under the textbook chapter. Not in the free set. INSERT OR IGNORE keeps the file safe to run twice; if the site
-- owner's admin account doesn't exist (a fresh local database) nothing is inserted.

-- Inserted in batches of 20 to stay under D1's statement size limit.

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-0001', 'Homeostasis', 'Elements of homeostasis', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 1 (§13.1.1)', 'Which three elements work together to maintain homeostasis?', '["Receptors, neurons and hormones", "Control centre, enzymes and blood", "Receptors, control centre and effectors", "Effectors, kidneys and skin"]', 2, 'The book names receptors (detect change), a control centre (processes it) and effectors (respond) as the three essential elements.', '{"0": "Neurons and hormones carry messages but are not the three named elements.", "1": "Enzymes and blood are not elements of the homeostatic loop.", "3": "Kidneys and skin are organs that may act as effectors, not the three elements."}'),
  ('BIO-0002', 'Homeostasis', 'Elements of homeostasis', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 1 (§13.1.1)', 'Structures that detect changes in the internal or external environment and pass the information to the control centre are called', '["receptors", "effectors", "set points", "control centres"]', 0, 'Receptors (sensors) detect changes and transmit the information to the control centre.', '{"1": "Effectors respond to messages from the control centre.", "2": "A set point is the optimal value being maintained, not a structure.", "3": "Control centres receive and process the information."}'),
  ('BIO-0003', 'Homeostasis', 'Elements of homeostasis', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 2 (§13.1.1)', 'According to the book, which of these acts as a control centre in homeostasis?', '["Skeletal muscles", "Endocrine glands", "Receptor cells in the skin", "Sweat produced on the skin"]', 1, 'The brain, spinal cord and endocrine glands act as control centres.', '{"0": "Muscles are effectors.", "2": "Skin receptor cells are receptors.", "3": "Sweat is a product of an effector response."}'),
  ('BIO-0004', 'Homeostasis', 'Elements of homeostasis', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 2 (§13.1.1)', 'In homeostasis, the effectors of the body are', '["the brain and spinal cord", "thermoreceptors in the skin", "blood vessel pressure receptors", "muscles and glands"]', 3, 'Muscles respond by contracting and glands by releasing secretions; both restore the set point.', '{"0": "The brain and spinal cord are control centres.", "1": "Thermoreceptors are receptors.", "2": "Pressure receptors in blood vessels are receptors."}'),
  ('BIO-0005', 'Homeostasis', 'Elements of homeostasis', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 2 (§13.1.1)', 'When body temperature drops below its set point, effectors in the skin and muscles respond by causing', '["sweating and vasodilation", "shivering and vasodilation", "shivering and vasoconstriction", "sweating and vasoconstriction"]', 2, 'Shivering raises heat production and vasoconstriction cuts heat loss from the skin, bringing temperature back up.', '{"0": "Sweating and vasodilation are responses to overheating.", "1": "Vasodilation would increase heat loss.", "3": "Sweating would cool the body further."}'),
  ('BIO-0006', 'Homeostasis', 'Feedback mechanisms', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 2 (§13.1.2)', 'A regulatory mechanism in which the output of a system reduces the activity of that system is called', '["positive feedback", "anhydrobiosis", "osmoconformity", "negative feedback"]', 3, 'In negative feedback the output reduces the activity of the system; it is the most common form of regulation.', '{"0": "Positive feedback increases the activity of the system.", "1": "Anhydrobiosis is a dormant state in dried-out invertebrates.", "2": "Osmoconformity is matching body fluid concentration to the environment."}'),
  ('BIO-0007', 'Homeostasis', 'Feedback mechanisms', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 2 (§13.1.2)', 'Which of the following is regulated by negative feedback, as given in the book?', '["Uterine contractions during labour", "Blood glucose level", "Blood clotting", "Cytokine release in severe inflammation"]', 1, 'Body temperature, blood glucose and hormone secretion are the book''s examples of negative feedback.', '{"0": "Labour contractions are driven by positive feedback through oxytocin.", "2": "Blood clotting is a positive feedback example.", "3": "Cytokine release that amplifies the immune response is (unregulated) positive feedback."}'),
  ('BIO-0008', 'Homeostasis', 'Feedback mechanisms', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 3 (§13.1.2)', 'If body temperature rises above 37 °C, the control centre that sends messages to the sweat glands is located in the', '["hypothalamus", "pituitary gland", "medulla oblongata", "skin dermis"]', 0, 'Thermoreceptors in the hypothalamus detect the rise and the thermoregulator in the hypothalamus signals the sweat glands.', '{"1": "The pituitary is the control link in the oxytocin loop, not thermoregulation.", "2": "The medulla is not named as the thermoregulatory centre.", "3": "The skin contains receptors and effectors, not the control centre."}'),
  ('BIO-0009', 'Homeostasis', 'Feedback mechanisms', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 3 (§13.1.2)', 'Once sweating has brought body temperature back to the set point, the thermoreceptors stop signalling the control centre. This shows that the output', '["amplifies the original stimulus", "has no effect on the receptors", "influences the receptors negatively", "switches on positive feedback"]', 2, 'In negative feedback, lowering temperature to the set point acts back on the thermoreceptors and switches the response off.', '{"0": "Amplifying the stimulus is positive feedback.", "1": "The output does act on the receptors; that is what stops the loop.", "3": "The loop described is negative feedback throughout."}'),
  ('BIO-0010', 'Homeostasis', 'Feedback mechanisms', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 3 (§13.1.2)', 'In homeostasis, positive feedback is generally used for processes that', '["need a constant value kept within narrow limits", "require rapid and significant changes", "regulate body temperature day to day", "must be stopped as soon as they start"]', 1, 'Positive feedback is less common and is seen where rapid, large changes are needed, such as blood clotting and childbirth.', '{"0": "Keeping a value within narrow limits is the job of negative feedback.", "2": "Day-to-day temperature control uses negative feedback.", "3": "Positive feedback amplifies a process until an end point (e.g. birth), rather than stopping it at once."}'),
  ('BIO-0011', 'Homeostasis', 'Feedback mechanisms', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 3 (§13.1.2)', 'During childbirth, which hormone is released from the pituitary gland in the positive feedback loop?', '["Oxytocin", "Thyroxine", "Insulin", "Antidiuretic hormone"]', 0, 'Uterine contractions signal the hypothalamus, which makes the pituitary release oxytocin; oxytocin causes further contractions.', '{"1": "Thyroxine is from the thyroid and affects metabolic rate.", "2": "Insulin is from the pancreas and lowers blood glucose.", "3": "ADH controls water reabsorption by the kidney, not labour."}'),
  ('BIO-0012', 'Homeostasis', 'Feedback mechanisms', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 3 (§13.1.2)', 'The positive feedback loop of labour comes to an end when', '["oxytocin secretion first begins", "the uterus stops sending nerve impulses at the first contraction", "body temperature returns to 37 °C", "the baby is born"]', 3, 'Each contraction triggers more oxytocin, which triggers stronger contractions; the loop continues until the baby is born.', '{"0": "Oxytocin release is what keeps the loop going.", "1": "The uterus keeps signalling with every contraction until birth.", "2": "Body temperature is not part of this loop."}'),
  ('BIO-0013', 'Homeostasis', 'Feedback mechanisms', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 3 (§13.1.2)', 'Why can a poorly regulated positive feedback of cytokines be harmful?', '["It stops the immune response completely", "It lowers body temperature below the set point", "It makes immune cells stop releasing proteins", "It can cause excessive inflammation and tissue damage"]', 3, 'Cytokines further stimulate the immune response; if this loop is not controlled, inflammation becomes excessive and damages tissue.', '{"0": "Positive feedback amplifies, not stops, the response.", "1": "The book links it to inflammation, not hypothermia.", "2": "The problem is that immune cells keep releasing more cytokines."}'),
  ('BIO-0014', 'Homeostasis', 'Osmoregulation', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 4 (§13.2)', 'The maintenance of the balance of water and solutes in the body fluids is called', '["thermoregulation", "osmoregulation", "excretion", "anhydrobiosis"]', 1, 'Osmoregulation keeps body fluids from becoming too dilute or too concentrated.', '{"0": "Thermoregulation controls body temperature.", "2": "Excretion is the removal of metabolic wastes.", "3": "Anhydrobiosis is a dormant state with almost no body water."}'),
  ('BIO-0015', 'Homeostasis', 'Osmoregulators and osmoconformers', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 4 (§13.2.1)', 'Animals whose body fluids remain isotonic to their surroundings because they do not regulate their internal solute concentration are', '["osmoregulators", "homeotherms", "osmoconformers", "uricotelic"]', 2, 'Osmoconformers let the osmotic concentration of their body fluids match the environment.', '{"0": "Osmoregulators keep internal concentrations constant despite the environment.", "1": "Homeotherms keep body temperature constant.", "3": "Uricotelic animals excrete uric acid."}'),
  ('BIO-0016', 'Homeostasis', 'Osmoregulators and osmoconformers', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 4 (§13.2.1)', 'Which group is given in the book as an example of osmoconformers?', '["Hagfishes", "Bony freshwater fishes", "Birds", "Kangaroo rats"]', 0, 'All marine invertebrates, some freshwater invertebrates and some marine vertebrates (hagfishes, sharks, rays) are osmoconformers.', '{"1": "Freshwater bony fishes are osmoregulators that remove excess water.", "2": "Birds are terrestrial osmoregulators.", "3": "The kangaroo rat is a desert mammal that conserves water, an osmoregulator."}'),
  ('BIO-0017', 'Homeostasis', 'Osmoregulators and osmoconformers', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 4 (§13.2.1)', 'Osmoconformers are usually found in environments that', '["change salinity rapidly, such as estuaries", "are completely dry, such as deserts", "are very hot, such as hot springs", "have relatively stable solute concentrations, such as sea water"]', 3, 'Because their internal concentration follows the surroundings, osmoconformers live where solute concentration stays stable, like the sea.', '{"0": "Rapid salinity changes would upset an animal that cannot regulate.", "1": "Deserts demand strong water conservation, i.e. osmoregulation.", "2": "Temperature is not the factor discussed for osmoconformers."}'),
  ('BIO-0018', 'Homeostasis', 'Osmoregulation in water', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 4 (§13.2.2)', 'Relative to the surrounding water, freshwater animals are', '["hypotonic, so water leaves their bodies by osmosis", "hypertonic, so water enters their bodies by osmosis", "isotonic, so no net water movement occurs", "hypertonic, so salts enter their bodies by diffusion"]', 1, 'Their body fluids have a higher solute concentration than fresh water, so water flows in (mainly across the gills) and salts diffuse out.', '{"0": "This describes marine animals.", "2": "Isotonic body fluids describe osmoconformers.", "3": "Salts diffuse out of freshwater animals, not in."}'),
  ('BIO-0019', 'Homeostasis', 'Osmoregulation in water', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 5 (§13.2.2)', 'To cope with the water that enters their bodies, freshwater fishes', '["drink large amounts of water", "produce small volumes of concentrated urine", "produce large volumes of dilute urine", "pump salts out through chloride cells"]', 2, 'They remove excess water as copious dilute urine and actively take salts back in; they rarely drink.', '{"0": "Freshwater animals rarely drink water.", "1": "Concentrated urine is produced by marine animals to save water.", "3": "Pumping salt out is what marine fishes do."}'),
  ('BIO-0020', 'Homeostasis', 'Osmoregulation in water', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 5 (§13.2.2)', 'In marine fishes, specialized chloride cells in the gills', '["use ATP to actively pump excess salts out of the blood", "absorb salts from sea water into the blood", "allow water to enter the body by osmosis", "convert ammonia into urea"]', 0, 'Marine fishes gain salt from the sea and from drinking it; chloride cells spend energy pumping it back out.', '{"1": "Taking salts in is the problem they face, not the job of chloride cells.", "2": "Water leaves, not enters, a marine fish by osmosis.", "3": "Urea formation happens in the liver."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-0021', 'Homeostasis', 'Osmoregulation in water', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 5 (§13.2.2)', 'Rectal glands found in some marine fishes', '["absorb water from the faeces into the blood", "remove salts from the blood into the digestive tract for elimination with faeces", "secrete dilute urine into the rectum", "store uric acid as crystals"]', 1, 'The book describes rectal glands as salt-excreting glands that move salt from blood into the gut, to leave with faeces.', '{"0": "Their role is salt removal, not water absorption.", "2": "Urine is made by the kidneys.", "3": "They are not storage organs for uric acid."}'),
  ('BIO-0022', 'Homeostasis', 'Osmoregulation on land', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 5 (§13.2.2)', 'Which pairing of animal and water-proof outer layer is correct according to the book?', '["Insects: scales made of keratin", "Mammals: waxy exoskeleton of chitin", "Birds: waxy exoskeleton of chitin", "Reptiles: scales made of keratin"]', 3, 'Insects have a waxy chitin exoskeleton, reptiles keratin scales, and mammals and birds dead keratinized skin cells with oil glands.', '{"0": "Insects have a chitin exoskeleton, not keratin scales.", "1": "Mammals have keratinized skin and oil glands.", "2": "Birds have keratinized skin, not an exoskeleton."}'),
  ('BIO-0023', 'Homeostasis', 'Osmoregulation on land', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 6 (§13.2.2)', 'The kangaroo rat is notable because it', '["can survive entirely on metabolic water", "drinks large amounts of water daily", "excretes ammonia directly into water", "becomes dormant for years until water returns"]', 0, 'Water made as a by-product of cellular respiration is enough for the kangaroo rat; it feeds on carbohydrate-rich seeds.', '{"1": "It does not depend on drinking.", "2": "Excreting ammonia needs plenty of water, which desert animals lack.", "3": "Years-long dormancy (anhydrobiosis) is described for tardigrades."}'),
  ('BIO-0024', 'Homeostasis', 'Osmoregulation on land', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 6 (§13.2.2)', 'Tardigrades (water bears) that lose almost all their body water and enter a dormant state show', '["hibernation due to cold", "osmoconformity", "anhydrobiosis", "thermoregulation"]', 2, 'Anhydrobiosis is a dormant state entered by certain invertebrates when they lose almost all their body water; they revive when water returns.', '{"0": "The trigger here is water loss, not cold.", "1": "Osmoconformers keep body fluids isotonic with the surrounding water.", "3": "Thermoregulation is control of body temperature."}'),
  ('BIO-0025', 'Homeostasis', 'Nitrogenous wastes', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 6 (§13.2.3)', 'One gram of ammonia needs about how much water to dilute it to a non-toxic level?', '["50 mL", "1 mL", "5 mL", "500 mL"]', 3, 'Ammonia is very toxic and needs about 500 mL of water per gram, which only aquatic, mainly freshwater, animals can afford.', '{"0": "50 mL per gram is the figure for urea.", "1": "1 mL per gram is the figure for uric acid.", "2": "This figure is not given; ammonia needs far more."}'),
  ('BIO-0026', 'Homeostasis', 'Nitrogenous wastes', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 6 (§13.2.3)', 'Animals that excrete mainly ammonia are called', '["ammonotelic", "ureotelic", "uricotelic", "osmoconformers"]', 0, 'Ammonotelic animals, such as most fishes, protozoans, sponges and coelenterates, excrete ammonia as their main nitrogenous waste.', '{"1": "Ureotelic animals excrete mainly urea.", "2": "Uricotelic animals excrete mainly uric acid.", "3": "Osmoconformers are defined by water balance, not by their waste."}'),
  ('BIO-0027', 'Homeostasis', 'Nitrogenous wastes', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 6 (§13.2.3)', 'Which group would be expected to be ammonotelic?', '["Birds", "Sponges", "Insects", "Most reptiles"]', 1, 'Freshwater animals such as most fishes, protozoans, sponges and coelenterates can afford the large volume of water ammonia needs.', '{"0": "Birds excrete uric acid.", "2": "Insects excrete uric acid.", "3": "Most reptiles excrete uric acid."}'),
  ('BIO-0028', 'Homeostasis', 'Nitrogenous wastes', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 6 (§13.2.3)', 'Excess uric acid in human blood can form crystals in the joints, causing', '["diabetes", "goitre", "gout", "anhydrobiosis"]', 2, 'Uric acid comes from purine breakdown; when it builds up in blood it crystallizes in joints and tissues, causing gout.', '{"0": "Diabetes concerns blood glucose.", "1": "Goitre is an enlarged thyroid.", "3": "Anhydrobiosis is a dormant dried state of some invertebrates."}'),
  ('BIO-0029', 'Homeostasis', 'Nitrogenous wastes', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 6 (§13.2.3)', 'In humans, the small amount of uric acid excreted in urine comes from the breakdown of', '["purines", "fatty acids", "glucose", "cholesterol"]', 0, 'Uric acid is a by-product of breaking down purines, which are found in many foods, especially meat.', '{"1": "Fatty acids do not contain nitrogen.", "2": "Glucose contains no nitrogen.", "3": "Cholesterol is a lipid, not a nitrogen source."}'),
  ('BIO-0030', 'Homeostasis', 'Nitrogenous wastes', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 7 (§13.2.3)', 'Compared with ammonia and urea, uric acid', '["is the most toxic and very soluble in water", "needs about 50 mL of water per gram", "is the least toxic and needs only about 1 mL of water per gram for excretion", "is made without spending any energy"]', 2, 'Uric acid is less toxic than urea, poorly soluble, forms precipitates and needs about 1 mL of water per gram, but its formation uses a lot of energy.', '{"0": "Ammonia is the most toxic and very soluble.", "1": "50 mL per gram is the urea figure.", "3": "Converting ammonia to uric acid uses a lot of energy."}'),
  ('BIO-0031', 'Homeostasis', 'Nitrogenous wastes', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 7 (§13.2.3)', 'Which statement about excretion of nitrogenous waste is correct?', '["Birds excrete urea because it is less toxic than uric acid", "Birds and insects convert ammonia to uric acid at a high energy cost to save water", "Freshwater fishes convert ammonia to uric acid", "Uric acid is more soluble in water than urea"]', 1, 'Animals in arid conditions, such as insects, birds and most reptiles, cannot afford even urea; they spend energy making uric acid, which needs very little water.', '{"0": "Uric acid is less toxic than urea, and birds are uricotelic.", "2": "Freshwater fishes are mostly ammonotelic.", "3": "Uric acid is less soluble and forms precipitates."}'),
  ('BIO-0032', 'Homeostasis', 'Thermoregulation', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 7 (§13.3)', 'Why is thermoregulation important for cell membranes?', '["Low temperatures make membranes more fluid and leaky", "Membranes contain no lipids that respond to temperature", "High temperatures make membranes rigid and brittle", "High temperatures can make membranes lose selective permeability"]', 3, 'The lipid bilayer is temperature-sensitive: high temperature can destroy selective permeability, low temperature makes the membrane rigid and brittle.', '{"0": "Low temperature makes membranes rigid, not more fluid.", "1": "Membranes are a lipid bilayer, which is temperature-sensitive.", "2": "Rigidity and brittleness come with low temperature."}'),
  ('BIO-0033', 'Homeostasis', 'Thermoregulation', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 7 (§13.3)', 'When body temperature falls well below the optimum for enzymes,', '["enzymes are permanently denatured", "enzymes work faster than at the optimum", "cell membranes lose their selective permeability", "enzyme-substrate collisions decrease and metabolic rate drops"]', 3, 'Low temperature slows molecules, so fewer enzyme-substrate collisions occur; denaturation is caused by temperatures well above the optimum.', '{"0": "Permanent denaturation follows a large rise, not a fall, in temperature.", "1": "Enzymes work best at the optimum.", "2": "Loss of selective permeability is linked to high temperature."}'),
  ('BIO-0034', 'Homeostasis', 'Heat source', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 7 (§13.3.1)', 'A lizard that presses its body against a warm rock to gain heat is an example of', '["an endotherm burning brown adipose tissue", "an ectotherm gaining heat by radiation from the rock", "an ectotherm using behavioural thermoregulation by conduction", "a homeotherm shivering"]', 2, 'Ectotherms rely on external heat; sitting on a warm rock gains heat by conduction, while basking in the sun gains heat by radiation.', '{"0": "Lizards are ectotherms; brown fat burning is an endotherm response.", "1": "Heat from a rock in contact with the body is conduction; radiation is from the sun.", "3": "Reptiles are poikilotherms and do not use shivering this way."}'),
  ('BIO-0035', 'Homeostasis', 'Heat source', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 7 (§13.3.1)', 'Ectotherms depend on heat from their environment mainly because', '["their metabolic rate is too low to produce enough body heat", "they lack a hypothalamus", "they have thick insulation of fat and feathers", "they cannot move to new places"]', 0, 'The book states that metabolism in ectotherms is very low, so they cannot raise and maintain body temperature internally.', '{"1": "The book gives low metabolic rate as the reason.", "2": "Insulation by fat, hair or feathers is a feature of endotherms.", "3": "Ectotherms move to shade or sun as behavioural thermoregulation."}'),
  ('BIO-0036', 'Homeostasis', 'Heat source', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 7 (§13.3.1)', 'Which response to cold is used by endotherms but not described for ectotherms?', '["Basking in the sun", "Burning brown adipose tissue", "Sitting on a warm rock", "Becoming dormant in the coldest months"]', 1, 'Endotherms produce heat internally, e.g. by shivering or burning brown adipose tissue.', '{"0": "Basking is ectotherm behaviour.", "2": "Gaining heat from a warm rock is ectotherm behaviour.", "3": "The book describes dormancy in cold months for ectotherms."}'),
  ('BIO-0037', 'Homeostasis', 'Thermoregulatory ability', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 8 (§13.3.2)', 'An advantage of being a poikilotherm is that the animal', '["keeps a constant body temperature in all climates", "has a very high metabolic rate", "needs much less food, so it can survive in nutrient-poor environments", "can live without a nervous system"]', 2, 'Poikilotherms do not burn large amounts of food to keep a constant temperature, so they survive where a homeotherm would starve.', '{"0": "A constant temperature is the feature of homeotherms.", "1": "Their metabolic rate is low.", "3": "This has nothing to do with poikilothermy."}'),
  ('BIO-0038', 'Homeostasis', 'Thermoregulatory ability', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 8 (§13.3.2)', 'Humans are described in the book as', '["ectothermic homeotherms", "endothermic homeotherms", "endothermic poikilotherms", "ectothermic poikilotherms"]', 1, 'Humans make heat internally (endothermic) and keep a constant temperature near 37 °C (homeothermic).', '{"0": "Humans do not rely on external heat.", "2": "Humans keep body temperature within narrow limits.", "3": "This describes fishes, amphibians and reptiles."}'),
  ('BIO-0039', 'Homeostasis', 'Thermoregulation in humans', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 8 (§13.3.3)', 'Which response occurs when human body temperature rises above 37 °C?', '["The thyroid may reduce thyroxine secretion, lowering the basal metabolic rate", "Skin capillaries constrict to reduce blood flow", "Skeletal muscles shiver to produce heat", "Hair erector muscles contract to trap air"]', 0, 'In hyperthermia, vasodilation, sweating and reduced thyroxine (lower BMR) all reduce heat gain or increase heat loss.', '{"1": "Vasoconstriction is a response to cooling.", "2": "Shivering is a response to cooling.", "3": "Goosebumps trap air to keep heat in during cooling."}'),
  ('BIO-0040', 'Homeostasis', 'Thermoregulation in humans', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 8 (§13.3.3)', 'Vasodilation helps cool the body because it', '["reduces blood flow to the skin", "causes shivering of skeletal muscles", "raises the basal metabolic rate", "increases blood flow in capillaries just beneath the epidermis, so more heat is lost"]', 3, 'Widening of skin capillaries brings more blood near the surface, where its heat is lost to the surroundings.', '{"0": "Reduced skin blood flow is vasoconstriction, which saves heat.", "1": "Shivering produces heat.", "2": "A higher metabolic rate produces more heat."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-0041', 'Homeostasis', 'Thermoregulation in humans', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 8 (§13.3.3)', 'We consciously "feel" warmth or cold and may switch on a fan or heater because thermoreceptors also stimulate', '["the sweat glands directly", "the thyroid gland", "the adrenal medulla", "the higher centres of the brain"]', 3, 'Besides the hypothalamus, thermoreceptors stimulate higher brain centres, giving the sensation that leads to voluntary actions.', '{"0": "Sweat glands are effectors, not where sensation arises.", "1": "The thyroid alters metabolic rate, not conscious feeling.", "2": "The adrenal medulla is not mentioned in this response."}'),
  ('BIO-0042', 'Homeostasis', 'Thermoregulation in humans', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 9 (§13.3.3)', '"Goosebumps" help to conserve heat because', '["they increase sweating", "they widen skin capillaries", "erect hairs trap a layer of air that acts as an insulator", "they lower the metabolic rate"]', 2, 'Tiny muscles at the base of hair follicles contract; air trapped by the raised hairs insulates the body.', '{"0": "Sweating cools the body.", "1": "Widening capillaries increases heat loss.", "3": "Metabolic rate rises, not falls, in cold."}'),
  ('BIO-0043', 'Homeostasis', 'Urinary system', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 12 (§14.1)', 'Besides removing nitrogenous waste and osmoregulation, the urinary system keeps the blood slightly basic at a pH of about', '["6.4", "8.4", "7.4", "7.0"]', 2, 'By balancing sodium, potassium and hydrogen ions, the urinary system keeps blood pH around 7.4.', '{"0": "6.4 would be acidic, not slightly basic.", "1": "8.4 is far more basic than normal blood.", "3": "7.0 is neutral, not slightly basic."}'),
  ('BIO-0044', 'Homeostasis', 'Urinary system', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 12 (§14.1)', 'The main nitrogenous waste filtered out of the blood by the human kidneys is', '["ammonia", "urea", "uric acid", "glucose"]', 1, 'Urea, formed in the liver from protein breakdown, is the nitrogenous waste the kidneys filter out.', '{"0": "Humans convert ammonia to urea in the liver.", "2": "Humans excrete only a small amount of uric acid.", "3": "Glucose is not a nitrogenous waste and is normally reabsorbed."}'),
  ('BIO-0045', 'Homeostasis', 'Urinary system', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 13 (§14.1)', 'Human kidneys lie on either side of the vertebral column between the', '["1st and 5th lumbar vertebrae", "7th cervical and 12th thoracic vertebrae", "1st and 5th sacral vertebrae", "12th thoracic and 3rd lumbar vertebrae"]', 3, 'The kidneys are high in the abdominal cavity between T12 and L3, partly protected by the 11th and 12th ribs.', '{"0": "This range is too low.", "1": "This range is in the chest region, too high.", "2": "The sacral region is in the pelvis."}'),
  ('BIO-0046', 'Homeostasis', 'Urinary system', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 13 (§14.1)', 'The right kidney is slightly lower than the left one because of the position of the', '["liver", "heart", "stomach", "spleen"]', 0, 'The liver lies on the right side above the kidney and pushes it slightly lower.', '{"1": "The heart is in the thorax and does not displace the kidney.", "2": "The stomach lies mainly on the left.", "3": "The spleen is on the left side."}'),
  ('BIO-0047', 'Homeostasis', 'Urinary system', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 13 (§14.1)', 'Each ureter, which carries urine from a kidney to the urinary bladder, is about', '["5 cm long", "50 cm long", "2.5 cm long", "25 cm long"]', 3, 'The ureters are about 25 cm long tubes that begin at the renal pelvis.', '{"0": "5 cm is far too short.", "1": "50 cm is twice the figure in the book.", "2": "2.5 cm is far too short."}'),
  ('BIO-0048', 'Homeostasis', 'Structure of kidney', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 13 (§14.1.1)', 'At the renal hilum,', '["the renal vein enters while the renal artery leaves", "the urethra leaves the kidney", "the renal artery enters while the renal vein and ureter leave", "the adrenal gland is attached"]', 2, 'The hilum is the depressed area on the concave side; the renal artery enters and the renal vein and ureter leave there.', '{"0": "The artery brings blood in; the vein takes it out.", "1": "The urethra leaves the urinary bladder, not the kidney.", "3": "The adrenal gland sits on top of the kidney."}'),
  ('BIO-0049', 'Homeostasis', 'Structure of kidney', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 13 (§14.1.1)', 'The renal cortex makes up about what fraction of the kidney''s mass?', '["One-half", "One-third", "Two-thirds", "One-fifth"]', 1, 'The outer cortex is about a third of the mass; the inner medulla makes up two-thirds.', '{"0": "The cortex and medulla are not equal.", "2": "Two-thirds is the medulla.", "3": "One-fifth is the fraction of plasma filtered, not the cortex mass."}'),
  ('BIO-0050', 'Homeostasis', 'Structure of kidney', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 13 (§14.1.1)', 'The cone-shaped renal pyramids are found in the', '["renal medulla", "renal cortex", "renal pelvis", "renal capsule"]', 0, 'The inner renal medulla is made of a number of cone-shaped renal pyramids.', '{"1": "The cortex is the outer portion.", "2": "The pelvis is the funnel-shaped collecting area.", "3": "The capsule is the fibrous outer covering."}'),
  ('BIO-0051', 'Homeostasis', 'Nephron', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 14 (§14.1.1)', 'Each human kidney contains about how many nephrons?', '["One million", "One thousand", "One hundred million", "Ten thousand"]', 0, 'Each kidney contains about one million microscopic nephrons, the functional units of the kidney.', '{"1": "Far too few.", "2": "Far too many.", "3": "Far too few."}'),
  ('BIO-0052', 'Homeostasis', 'Nephron', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 14 (§14.1.1)', 'The renal corpuscle of a nephron consists of', '["the loop of Henle and collecting duct", "the proximal and distal convoluted tubules", "the glomerulus and Bowman''s capsule", "the afferent and efferent arterioles only"]', 2, 'The glomerulus (capillary network) and the cup-shaped, double-walled Bowman''s capsule together form the renal corpuscle.', '{"0": "These are parts of the renal tubule and duct system.", "1": "These are parts of the renal tubule.", "3": "Arterioles supply and drain the glomerulus but are not the corpuscle itself."}'),
  ('BIO-0053', 'Homeostasis', 'Nephron', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 14 (§14.1.1)', 'The correct order of parts through which filtrate passes in a nephron is', '["Bowman''s capsule → loop of Henle → proximal convoluted tubule → distal convoluted tubule", "proximal convoluted tubule → Bowman''s capsule → loop of Henle → distal convoluted tubule", "Bowman''s capsule → distal convoluted tubule → loop of Henle → proximal convoluted tubule", "Bowman''s capsule → proximal convoluted tubule → loop of Henle → distal convoluted tubule"]', 3, 'The renal tubule leaves Bowman''s capsule as the PCT, turns as the U-shaped loop of Henle, and continues as the DCT.', '{"0": "The PCT comes before the loop.", "1": "Filtrate begins in Bowman''s capsule.", "2": "This is the reverse of the true order."}'),
  ('BIO-0054', 'Homeostasis', 'Nephron', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 14 (§14.1.1)', 'Collecting ducts merge as they descend through the medulla to form larger', '["ureters that run inside the cortex", "papillary ducts that discharge into the renal pelvis", "loops of Henle", "afferent arterioles"]', 1, 'Several DCTs drain into one collecting duct; collecting ducts merge into papillary ducts that empty into the renal pelvis.', '{"0": "Ureters start at the renal pelvis, outside the medulla.", "2": "Loops of Henle are part of the nephron, before the collecting duct.", "3": "Afferent arterioles are blood vessels."}'),
  ('BIO-0055', 'Homeostasis', 'Nephron', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 14 (§14.1.1)', 'Juxtamedullary nephrons differ from cortical nephrons in that they', '["have long loops of Henle reaching deep into the medulla", "make up about 85% of all nephrons", "lie entirely within the outer cortex", "have no loop of Henle"]', 0, 'Juxtamedullary nephrons start deep in the cortex near the medulla and have long loops; cortical nephrons (about 85%) have short loops.', '{"1": "About 85% are cortical nephrons.", "2": "Cortical nephrons lie mainly in the cortex.", "3": "Both types have loops of Henle."}'),
  ('BIO-0056', 'Homeostasis', 'Blood supply', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 15 (§14.1.2)', 'The kidneys receive about what share of the total cardiac output?', '["2–5%", "20–25%", "50–60%", "75%"]', 1, 'The kidneys are highly vascular organs and receive about 20–25% of the cardiac output.', '{"0": "Much too low.", "2": "Much too high.", "3": "75% is the share of filtrate water reabsorbed in the PCT, not cardiac output."}'),
  ('BIO-0057', 'Homeostasis', 'Blood supply', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 15 (§14.1.2)', 'What is unusual about the glomerular capillaries compared with most capillary beds?', '["They drain directly into the renal vein", "They carry deoxygenated blood from the heart", "They lie in the renal medulla around the loop of Henle", "They drain into an efferent arteriole instead of a venule"]', 3, 'Most capillary beds drain into venules, but the glomerulus drains into an efferent arteriole, which then forms the peritubular capillaries.', '{"0": "Blood reaches the renal vein only after the peritubular capillaries.", "1": "The renal artery brings oxygenated blood.", "2": "Glomeruli are inside Bowman''s capsules; vasa recta lie beside the loops."}'),
  ('BIO-0058', 'Homeostasis', 'Blood supply', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 15 (§14.1.2)', 'The vasa recta are', '["the capillaries inside Bowman''s capsule", "branches of the renal vein at the hilum", "long straight capillaries in the medulla that run parallel to the loops of Henle", "tubes that carry urine to the bladder"]', 2, 'In juxtamedullary nephrons, efferent arterioles give rise to vasa recta that run parallel to the loops of Henle.', '{"0": "Those are the glomerular capillaries.", "1": "The vasa recta arise from efferent arterioles.", "3": "Ureters carry urine to the bladder."}'),
  ('BIO-0059', 'Homeostasis', 'Blood supply', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 15 (§14.1.2)', 'The renal vein returns filtered blood to the heart by draining into the', '["superior vena cava", "inferior vena cava", "hepatic portal vein", "aorta"]', 1, 'The renal vein exits at the hilum and drains into the inferior vena cava.', '{"0": "The superior vena cava drains the upper body.", "2": "The hepatic portal vein carries blood from the gut to the liver.", "3": "The aorta is an artery carrying blood away from the heart."}'),
  ('BIO-0060', 'Homeostasis', 'Glomerular filtration', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 16 (§14.1.3)', 'Blood in the glomerulus stays under high pressure mainly because', '["the afferent arteriole is narrower than the efferent arteriole", "the glomerulus drains into a venule", "the efferent arteriole is narrower than the afferent arteriole", "the renal vein is blocked by the ureter"]', 2, 'Blood enters through a wider afferent arteriole and leaves through a narrower efferent one, keeping glomerular pressure high.', '{"0": "This would lower the pressure in the glomerulus.", "1": "The glomerulus drains into an arteriole.", "3": "There is no such blockage in a healthy kidney."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-0061', 'Homeostasis', 'Glomerular filtration', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 16 (§14.1.3)', 'Which of these normally remains in the blood and does not enter the glomerular filtrate?', '["Albumin", "Glucose", "Urea", "Amino acids"]', 0, 'Plasma proteins such as albumin and globulins, and blood cells, are too large to pass through the capillary walls.', '{"1": "Glucose is filtered and later reabsorbed.", "2": "Urea is filtered and excreted.", "3": "Amino acids are filtered and later reabsorbed."}'),
  ('BIO-0062', 'Homeostasis', 'Glomerular filtration', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 16 (§14.1.3)', 'About what fraction of the fluid portion of blood passing through the glomerulus filters into Bowman''s capsule?', '["One-half (50%)", "Three-quarters (75%)", "One-hundredth (1%)", "One-fifth (20%)"]', 3, 'About 20% of the plasma filters into Bowman''s capsule as glomerular filtrate.', '{"0": "Too high.", "1": "75% is the water reabsorbed in the PCT.", "2": "Too low."}'),
  ('BIO-0063', 'Homeostasis', 'Selective reabsorption', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 16 (§14.1.3)', 'Most selective reabsorption takes place in the', '["distal convoluted tubule", "collecting duct", "ascending limb of the loop of Henle", "proximal convoluted tubule"]', 3, 'In the PCT about 75% of the water returns by osmosis, and glucose, amino acids and salts are actively reabsorbed.', '{"0": "The DCT reabsorbs only some water and salts.", "1": "The collecting duct is the final site for water reabsorption, not the main one.", "2": "The ascending limb moves salts but is impermeable to water."}'),
  ('BIO-0064', 'Homeostasis', 'Selective reabsorption', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 16 (§14.1.3)', 'In the proximal convoluted tubule, glucose and amino acids return to the blood by', '["active transport", "osmosis", "filtration under pressure", "tubular secretion"]', 0, 'Glucose, amino acids and salts move back from the PCT into the blood by active transport, while water follows by osmosis.', '{"1": "Osmosis is the movement of water.", "2": "Filtration happens at the glomerulus.", "3": "Secretion moves substances into the filtrate, the opposite direction."}'),
  ('BIO-0065', 'Homeostasis', 'Hormonal control', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 18 (§14.2, Hormonal control)', 'After a person drinks a lot of water and blood salt concentration falls, which sequence follows?', '["ADH secretion rises, collecting ducts become more permeable, concentrated urine is excreted", "Aldosterone secretion rises, sodium is retained, urine volume falls", "ADH secretion stops, collecting ducts become less permeable, dilute urine is excreted", "ADH secretion stops, collecting ducts become more permeable, blood volume rises"]', 2, 'With low blood salt the hypothalamus stops ADH secretion; less water is reabsorbed, dilute urine is passed and blood volume and pressure fall.', '{"0": "This is the response to dehydration or salty food.", "1": "Aldosterone responds to low blood pressure or low sodium, and retaining sodium would not correct excess water.", "3": "Without ADH the ducts become less, not more, permeable, and blood volume falls."}'),
  ('BIO-0066', 'Homeostasis', 'Selective reabsorption', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 16 (§14.1.3)', 'Which part of the loop of Henle is impermeable to water but actively transports salts out of the tubule?', '["Descending limb", "Ascending limb", "Bowman''s capsule", "Proximal convoluted tubule"]', 1, 'The descending limb lets water out; the ascending limb is impermeable to water but pumps salts out.', '{"0": "The descending limb is permeable to water but not to salts.", "2": "Bowman''s capsule receives the filtrate; it is not part of the loop.", "3": "The PCT is not part of the loop."}'),
  ('BIO-0067', 'Homeostasis', 'Tubular secretion', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 16 (§14.1.3)', 'Through tubular secretion of which ions do the kidneys help to keep the pH of blood in balance?', '["Calcium ions", "Chloride ions", "Glucose molecules", "Hydrogen ions"]', 3, 'Cells of the tubule secrete hydrogen ions into the filtrate, which helps maintain blood acid-base balance.', '{"0": "Calcium is reabsorbed in the PCT.", "1": "Chloride ions follow sodium out of the ascending limb.", "2": "Glucose is not an ion and is reabsorbed."}'),
  ('BIO-0068', 'Homeostasis', 'Tubular secretion', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 16 (§14.1.3)', 'Penicillin residues reach the urine mainly by', '["tubular secretion", "selective reabsorption", "the counter-current mechanism", "diffusion through the urethra"]', 0, 'In tubular secretion, tubule cells take substances such as H⁺, NH₄⁺, K⁺, toxins and drug residues like penicillin from the blood into the filtrate.', '{"1": "Reabsorption returns materials to the blood.", "2": "The counter-current mechanism concentrates salts in the medulla.", "3": "The urethra only carries urine out."}'),
  ('BIO-0069', 'Homeostasis', 'Urine elimination', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 17 (§14.1.3)', 'A normal adult passes about 1–2 L of urine a day. Which change would decrease urine volume?', '["Drinking more fluids", "Low secretion of ADH", "Eating a diet high in salt", "Cool weather with little sweating"]', 2, 'A high-salt diet triggers water retention and decreases urine output; exercise and hot weather also reduce urine volume.', '{"0": "More fluid intake increases urine output.", "1": "Less ADH makes collecting ducts less permeable, giving more dilute urine.", "3": "With little sweating the kidneys do not need to conserve as much water."}'),
  ('BIO-0070', 'Homeostasis', 'Counter-current mechanism', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 17 (§14.2.1)', 'The counter-current mechanism allows water to be reabsorbed from the loop of Henle and collecting duct by', '["pumping water actively out of the tubule", "keeping a high salt (NaCl) concentration in the fluid around them", "making the ascending limb permeable to water", "lowering the salt concentration of the medulla"]', 1, 'The ascending limb pumps Na⁺ out (Cl⁻ follows), keeping the surrounding fluid salty; water then leaves the descending limb and collecting duct by osmosis.', '{"0": "Water moves by osmosis, not active pumping.", "2": "The ascending limb is impermeable to water.", "3": "A high, not low, salt concentration is needed."}'),
  ('BIO-0071', 'Homeostasis', 'Counter-current mechanism', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 17 (§14.2.1)', 'In the counter-current mechanism, the descending limb of the loop of Henle is', '["permeable to water but not to salts", "permeable to salts but not to water", "impermeable to both water and salts", "the site of active sodium pumping"]', 0, 'As filtrate moves down the descending limb, water leaves into the salty surrounding fluid; salts do not.', '{"1": "This describes the ascending limb more closely.", "2": "The descending limb does let water out.", "3": "Active sodium transport occurs in the ascending limb."}'),
  ('BIO-0072', 'Homeostasis', 'Counter-current mechanism', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 17 (§14.2.1)', 'If all loops of Henle failed to maintain the salty fluid around them, the most likely result would be', '["production of small volumes of concentrated urine", "production of large volumes of dilute urine", "complete stopping of glomerular filtration", "appearance of plasma proteins in urine"]', 1, 'Without the high salt concentration made by the loops, water cannot be drawn out of the collecting ducts, so urine stays dilute.', '{"0": "Concentrated urine depends on the salty medulla.", "2": "Filtration depends on glomerular pressure, not the loop.", "3": "Protein in urine would point to glomerular damage."}'),
  ('BIO-0073', 'Homeostasis', 'Hormonal control', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 17 (§14.2, Hormonal control)', 'Antidiuretic hormone (ADH) is produced by the', '["anterior pituitary and released by the hypothalamus", "adrenal cortex", "kidney itself", "hypothalamus and released by the posterior pituitary"]', 3, 'ADH is made in the hypothalamus and secreted from the posterior pituitary gland.', '{"0": "The origins are reversed and the wrong lobe is named.", "1": "The adrenal cortex makes aldosterone.", "2": "The kidney is the target, not the source."}'),
  ('BIO-0074', 'Homeostasis', 'Hormonal control', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 18 (§14.2, Hormonal control)', 'ADH conserves water by', '["increasing sodium reabsorption in the PCT", "making the ascending limb permeable to water", "making the walls of the collecting ducts more permeable to water", "increasing the glomerular filtration pressure"]', 2, 'ADH acts on collecting duct walls; more water moves out into the hypertonic fluid and then into blood, so urine becomes concentrated.', '{"0": "Sodium reabsorption is the action of aldosterone (on the DCT and collecting duct).", "1": "The ascending limb stays impermeable to water.", "3": "ADH does not act on the glomerulus."}'),
  ('BIO-0075', 'Homeostasis', 'Hormonal control', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 17 (§14.2, Hormonal control)', 'A rise in the salt concentration of the blood after eating salty food is detected by', '["thermoreceptors in the skin", "cells of the adrenal cortex", "pressure receptors in the bladder", "osmoreceptors in the hypothalamus"]', 3, 'Osmoreceptors in the hypothalamus detect the higher blood salt concentration and trigger ADH release.', '{"0": "Thermoreceptors detect temperature.", "1": "The adrenal cortex releases aldosterone when blood pressure or sodium falls.", "2": "Bladder receptors sense filling, not blood salt."}'),
  ('BIO-0076', 'Homeostasis', 'Hormonal control', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 18 (§14.2, Hormonal control)', 'Diabetes insipidus results from', '["too little insulin, so glucose appears in urine", "too much ADH, so very little urine is produced", "too little ADH, so large volumes of dilute urine are excreted", "too much aldosterone, so sodium is retained"]', 2, 'A person who cannot make enough ADH (e.g. pituitary damage) passes large volumes of dilute urine and risks severe dehydration.', '{"0": "That describes diabetes mellitus.", "1": "Excess ADH would give concentrated urine.", "3": "Diabetes insipidus is an ADH problem."}'),
  ('BIO-0077', 'Homeostasis', 'Hormonal control', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 18 (§14.2, Hormonal control)', 'Aldosterone is secreted when blood pressure drops or blood sodium is low. It acts on the', '["distal convoluted tubule and collecting duct to increase sodium reabsorption", "proximal tubule to increase glucose reabsorption", "glomerulus to reduce filtration", "collecting duct to reduce water reabsorption"]', 0, 'Aldosterone from the adrenal cortex increases Na⁺ reabsorption in the DCT and collecting duct; water follows the sodium into blood.', '{"1": "Glucose reabsorption in the PCT is not aldosterone''s role.", "2": "Aldosterone does not act on the glomerulus.", "3": "Water reabsorption increases as it follows sodium."}'),
  ('BIO-0078', 'Homeostasis', 'Hormonal control', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 18 (§14.2, Hormonal control)', 'Besides retaining sodium, aldosterone helps prevent hyperkalaemia by', '["increasing reabsorption of potassium into blood", "promoting the excretion of potassium ions in urine", "stopping the secretion of hydrogen ions", "converting potassium into urea"]', 1, 'Aldosterone promotes K⁺ excretion, preventing high blood potassium that can cause dangerous cardiac arrhythmias.', '{"0": "That would raise blood potassium.", "2": "This is not described as aldosterone''s action.", "3": "Potassium is an ion and is not converted into urea."}'),
  ('BIO-0079', 'Homeostasis', 'Capillary beds', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 18 (§14.2, Two capillary beds)', 'Compared with glomerular capillaries, peritubular capillaries', '["work under high pressure and are mainly for filtration", "lie inside Bowman''s capsule", "receive blood directly from the afferent arteriole", "work under low pressure and are mainly for reabsorption and secretion"]', 3, 'Glomerular capillaries filter under high pressure; peritubular capillaries around the tubules work at low pressure to take back nutrients and water and to secrete toxins.', '{"0": "This describes glomerular capillaries.", "1": "Glomerular capillaries lie in Bowman''s capsule.", "2": "Peritubular capillaries come from the efferent arteriole."}'),
  ('BIO-0080', 'Homeostasis', 'Disorders', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 19 (§14.3.1)', 'Urinary tract infections are most commonly caused by', '["Plasmodium", "Influenza virus", "Escherichia coli", "Candida"]', 2, 'UTIs are usually bacterial, most often E. coli, entering through the urethra.', '{"0": "Plasmodium is a protozoan causing malaria.", "1": "UTIs are usually bacterial, not viral.", "3": "The book names bacteria as the usual cause."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-0081', 'Homeostasis', 'Disorders', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 19 (§14.3.2)', 'About 70% of kidney stone patients have stones made of', '["uric acid", "calcium oxalate", "calcium phosphate", "cholesterol"]', 1, 'Calcium oxalate stones (about 70%) form with hypercalciuria and hyperoxaluria; about 10% are uric acid stones.', '{"0": "Uric acid stones are about 10%.", "2": "Phosphate stones are less common.", "3": "Cholesterol forms gallstones, not the common kidney stones."}'),
  ('BIO-0082', 'Homeostasis', 'Disorders', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 19 (§14.3.2)', 'Uric acid kidney stones are linked with', '["hyperuricemia, an increased amount of uric acid in blood", "hypercalciuria alone", "excess drinking of water", "low protein in the diet only"]', 0, 'About 10% of patients have uric acid stones, caused by hyperuricemia.', '{"1": "High calcium in urine leads to calcium oxalate stones.", "2": "Dehydration, not plenty of water, favours stones.", "3": "The book links them to high blood uric acid."}'),
  ('BIO-0083', 'Homeostasis', 'Disorders', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 19 (§14.3.2)', 'In extracorporeal shockwave lithotripsy (ECSWL), kidney stones are', '["broken by shockwaves generated outside the body", "removed through a tube inserted from the back", "dissolved by antibiotics", "filtered out by a dialyzer"]', 0, 'A lithotripter sends shockwaves from outside the body; the small pieces then pass out in urine.', '{"1": "That is percutaneous nephrolithotripsy (PNL).", "2": "Antibiotics treat infections, not stones.", "3": "A dialyzer filters blood in haemodialysis."}'),
  ('BIO-0084', 'Homeostasis', 'Disorders', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 20 (§14.3.2)', 'In percutaneous nephrolithotripsy (PNL), the stone is seen with a small camera called a', '["lithotripter", "dialyzer", "nephroscope", "stethoscope"]', 2, 'For larger stones a tube is inserted from the back; a nephroscope shows the stone, which is broken and pulled out.', '{"0": "A lithotripter makes shockwaves from outside the body.", "1": "A dialyzer is the filter in haemodialysis.", "3": "A stethoscope is used to listen to body sounds."}'),
  ('BIO-0085', 'Homeostasis', 'Kidney failure', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 20 (§14.3.3)', 'Which statement about kidney failure is correct?', '["Acute kidney failure is always irreversible", "Chronic kidney failure develops slowly and is irreversible", "Chronic kidney failure is mainly caused by NSAIDs taken once", "Acute kidney failure develops over many years"]', 1, 'Chronic failure develops over a long time (e.g. diabetes, high blood pressure) and is irreversible; acute failure is sudden and often resolves with treatment.', '{"0": "Acute failure often resolves with treatment.", "2": "NSAIDs are listed among causes of acute failure.", "3": "Acute failure occurs suddenly."}'),
  ('BIO-0086', 'Homeostasis', 'Kidney failure', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 20 (§14.3.3)', 'Which is listed as a cause of acute kidney failure?', '["Long-standing diabetes", "Long-term high blood pressure", "Ageing over decades", "Blockage of the urinary tract by an enlarged prostate"]', 3, 'Acute failure may follow infection, urinary tract blockage (stones, bladder cancer, enlarged prostate), NSAIDs or renal artery clots.', '{"0": "Diabetes is a cause of chronic failure.", "1": "High blood pressure is a cause of chronic failure.", "2": "This is a slow process, not a sudden one."}'),
  ('BIO-0087', 'Homeostasis', 'Dialysis', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 20 (§14.3.3)', 'In haemodialysis, the semipermeable membrane of the dialyzer is made of', '["plasma proteins", "the peritoneum", "cellulose", "keratin"]', 2, 'The dialyzer is a filter containing a cellulose semipermeable membrane; wastes pass from blood into the dialysate.', '{"0": "Plasma proteins are blood components.", "1": "The peritoneum is the natural membrane used in peritoneal dialysis.", "3": "Keratin is a skin protein."}'),
  ('BIO-0088', 'Homeostasis', 'Dialysis', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 21 (§14.3.3)', 'In peritoneal dialysis, about 1.5–2 L of dialysate is placed in the abdomen and left for', '["10 to 15 minutes", "4 to 6 hours", "2 to 3 days", "1 week"]', 1, 'The dialysate stays in the abdomen for 4–6 hours while wastes pass into it from peritoneal capillaries; it is then drained and the process repeated several times a day.', '{"0": "Too short for adequate clearance.", "2": "The process is repeated several times a day.", "3": "The process is repeated several times a day."}'),
  ('BIO-0089', 'Homeostasis', 'Dialysis', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 21 (§14.3.3)', 'Which is a common complication of haemodialysis named in the book?', '["Hypertension caused by the dialysate", "Polycythaemia (too many red cells)", "Formation of kidney stones", "Hypotension (low blood pressure)"]', 3, 'Hypotension is a common complication; others include infection, muscle cramps, anaemia and dehydration if fluid is removed too fast.', '{"0": "The book lists low, not high, blood pressure.", "1": "Dialysis can cause anaemia, not excess red cells.", "2": "Stones are not listed as a dialysis problem."}'),
  ('BIO-0090', 'Homeostasis', 'Transplant', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 22 (§14.3.4)', 'The best kidney donor, according to the book, is usually a', '["genetically related person such as a parent or sibling", "co-worker with a different blood group", "unrelated friend with no tissue matching", "person of any age without testing"]', 0, 'A related donor (parent, sibling or child) is described as the best donor; unrelated donation needs blood group and tissue type matching.', '{"1": "Blood groups must match.", "2": "Unrelated donors need matching of blood group and tissue type.", "3": "Careful evaluation and matching are always needed."}'),
  ('BIO-0091', 'Homeostasis', 'Transplant', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 22 (§14.3.4)', 'Pakistan''s first kidney transplant centre was established in 1985 at', '["PKLI, Lahore", "Mayo Hospital, Lahore", "PIMS, Islamabad", "SIUT, Karachi"]', 3, 'The Sindh Institute of Urology and Transplantation (SIUT) in Karachi began kidney transplants in 1985; PKLI in Lahore was set up in 2017.', '{"0": "PKLI was established in 2017.", "1": "Not named in the book as the first centre.", "2": "Not named in the book as the first centre."}'),
  ('BIO-0092', 'Homeostasis', 'Transplant', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 23 (§14.3.4)', 'Kidney transplant recipients take immunosuppressant drugs to', '["prevent the immune system from rejecting the donor kidney", "dissolve kidney stones", "increase the production of urine", "prevent all infections"]', 0, 'The recipient''s immune system may attack the transplanted kidney; immunosuppressants reduce this, though they raise the risk of infections and cancers.', '{"1": "They have nothing to do with stones.", "2": "They do not act as diuretics.", "3": "They actually increase the risk of infections."}'),
  ('BIO-0093', 'Coordination and control', 'Nervous coordination', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 26 (§15.1)', 'Nervous coordination is carried out by means of', '["hormones carried in blood", "enzymes secreted into the gut", "pigments in the skin", "electrical signals"]', 3, 'The nervous system coordinates the body by electrical signals (nerve impulses); chemical coordination by hormones is the job of the endocrine system.', '{"0": "Hormones are the basis of chemical (endocrine) coordination.", "1": "Digestive enzymes do not coordinate body activities.", "2": "Pigments have no coordinating role."}'),
  ('BIO-0094', 'Coordination and control', 'Nervous coordination', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 26 (§15.1.1)', 'Receptors are described as transducers because they', '["carry impulses from the CNS to muscles", "store neurotransmitters in vesicles", "convert one form of energy into another, such as light into nerve impulses", "connect sensory neurons to motor neurons"]', 2, 'Rods and cones, for example, convert light energy into the electrochemical energy of a nerve impulse.', '{"0": "That is the role of motor neurons.", "1": "Synaptic vesicles in axon terminals store neurotransmitters.", "3": "Inter-neurons in the CNS link sensory to motor neurons."}'),
  ('BIO-0095', 'Coordination and control', 'Nervous coordination', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 27 (§15.1.1)', 'The correct path of a message in nervous coordination is', '["receptor → motor neuron → inter-neuron → sensory neuron → effector", "receptor → sensory neuron → inter-neuron in CNS → motor neuron → effector", "effector → sensory neuron → CNS → motor neuron → receptor", "receptor → inter-neuron → sensory neuron → effector → motor neuron"]', 1, 'Receptors start impulses in sensory neurons; inter-neurons of the CNS process them and send impulses through motor neurons to effectors.', '{"0": "Motor neurons carry impulses away from the CNS, so they come after it.", "2": "The path starts at a receptor and ends at an effector.", "3": "The order of sensory neurons, inter-neurons and motor neurons is wrong."}'),
  ('BIO-0096', 'Coordination and control', 'Neuron structure', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 27 (§15.2.1)', 'Nissl''s granules in the cell body of a neuron are groups of', '["ribosomes and rough endoplasmic reticulum", "mitochondria", "synaptic vesicles", "myelin layers"]', 0, 'The cytoplasm of the cell body is characterized by Nissl''s granules, made of ribosomes and rough ER.', '{"1": "Mitochondria are not what Nissl''s granules are.", "2": "Synaptic vesicles are found in the synaptic knobs of axon terminals.", "3": "Myelin is made around axons by glial cells."}'),
  ('BIO-0097', 'Coordination and control', 'Neuron structure', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 27 (§15.2.1)', 'Which part of a neuron carries impulses toward the cell body?', '["Dendrite", "Axon", "Axon terminal", "Myelin sheath"]', 0, 'Dendrites receive information, convert it into impulses and carry it toward the cell body; the axon carries impulses away.', '{"1": "The axon carries impulses away from the cell body.", "2": "The axon terminal passes signals on to the next cell.", "3": "Myelin is an insulating covering, not a conducting part."}'),
  ('BIO-0098', 'Coordination and control', 'Neuron structure', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 27 (§15.2.1)', 'Motor neurons and inter-neurons have highly branched dendrites because they', '["need to conduct impulses over long distances", "are covered with myelin sheaths", "receive impulses from many different sources at the same time", "carry impulses away from the CNS only"]', 2, 'The book links heavy dendrite branching to receiving impulses from many sources simultaneously.', '{"0": "Long-distance conduction depends on the axon.", "1": "Myelin covers axons and is not related to dendrite branching.", "3": "This does not explain branching of the receiving ends."}'),
  ('BIO-0099', 'Coordination and control', 'Neuroglia', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 28 (§15.2.1)', 'Myelin sheaths around axons in the central nervous system are made by', '["Schwann cells", "oligodendrocytes", "Nissl''s granules", "basal cells"]', 1, 'Oligodendrocytes form myelin in the CNS; Schwann cells form it in the peripheral nervous system.', '{"0": "Schwann cells make myelin in the PNS.", "2": "Nissl''s granules are ribosomes and rough ER in the cell body.", "3": "Basal cells are stem cells in taste buds and nasal epithelium."}'),
  ('BIO-0100', 'Coordination and control', 'Neuroglia', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 28 (§15.2.1)', 'Which of these is a function of neuroglia given in the book?', '["Conducting nerve impulses to effectors", "Detecting stimuli as receptors", "Releasing acetylcholine at synapses", "Supplying nutrients to neurons and removing wastes"]', 3, 'Glial cells support neurons: they supply nutrients, remove wastes, provide immunity and make myelin.', '{"0": "Conduction is done by neurons.", "1": "Receptors detect stimuli.", "2": "Neurotransmitters are released by neurons."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-0101', 'Coordination and control', 'Neuroglia', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 28 (§15.2.1)', 'In the central nervous system, white matter is made mainly of', '["cell bodies and non-myelinated dendrites", "blood capillaries", "cerebrospinal fluid", "myelinated axons"]', 3, 'Myelinated axons form white matter; non-myelinated dendrites and cell bodies form grey matter.', '{"0": "These form grey matter.", "1": "Capillaries supply nervous tissue but do not make white matter.", "2": "CSF fills the ventricles and central canal."}'),
  ('BIO-0102', 'Coordination and control', 'Neuroglia', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 28 (§15.2.1)', 'Nodes of Ranvier are', '["small gaps in the myelin sheath at intervals of about 1–2 mm", "clusters of sensory cell bodies outside the CNS", "swellings at the end of the axon that store neurotransmitter", "the fluid-filled gaps between two neurons"]', 0, 'In myelinated neurons the sheath is interrupted every 1–2 mm by non-myelinated gaps, the nodes of Ranvier.', '{"1": "Those are ganglia.", "2": "Those are synaptic knobs.", "3": "That is the synaptic cleft."}'),
  ('BIO-0103', 'Coordination and control', 'Types of neurons', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 28 (§15.2.2)', 'Where are the cell bodies of sensory neurons located?', '["Inside the grey matter of the spinal cord", "Outside the CNS in clusters called ganglia", "In the white matter of the brain", "In the effector organs"]', 1, 'Sensory neuron cell bodies lie outside the CNS in ganglia, such as the dorsal root ganglia.', '{"0": "Motor neuron cell bodies are inside the CNS.", "2": "White matter is made of myelinated axons.", "3": "Effectors are muscles and glands."}'),
  ('BIO-0104', 'Coordination and control', 'Types of neurons', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 28 (§15.2.2)', 'Which statement about motor neurons is correct?', '["They carry impulses from receptors to the CNS", "Their cell bodies are in the dorsal root ganglia", "They are multipolar and their cell bodies lie in the CNS", "They occur entirely within the CNS"]', 2, 'Motor neurons conduct impulses away from the CNS, have many dendrites (multipolar) and have their cell bodies in the CNS.', '{"0": "That is the role of sensory neurons.", "1": "Dorsal root ganglia contain sensory cell bodies.", "3": "Inter-neurons occur entirely within the CNS; motor axons leave it."}'),
  ('BIO-0105', 'Coordination and control', 'Types of neurons', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 28 (§15.2.2)', 'Neurons that lie entirely within the CNS and link sensory neurons to motor neurons are', '["motor neurons", "sensory neurons", "Schwann cells", "inter-neurons (associative neurons)"]', 3, 'Inter-neurons occur only in the CNS; they receive impulses from sensory neurons or other inter-neurons and pass them to motor neurons or other inter-neurons.', '{"0": "Motor neuron axons extend out to effectors.", "1": "Sensory neurons bring impulses into the CNS from outside.", "2": "Schwann cells are glial cells of the PNS."}'),
  ('BIO-0106', 'Coordination and control', 'Reflex arc', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 29 (§15.2.3)', 'The reflex arc is best described as', '["a pathway that always involves the cerebrum", "a voluntary response under conscious control", "the simplest neural pathway, producing an immediate, automatic, involuntary response", "a hormonal pathway through the pituitary"]', 2, 'A reflex arc is the simplest neural pathway and controls reflex actions, which are immediate, automatic and involuntary.', '{"0": "Reflexes such as the knee-jerk are handled in the spinal cord.", "1": "Reflexes are involuntary.", "3": "A reflex arc is neural, not hormonal."}'),
  ('BIO-0107', 'Coordination and control', 'Reflex arc', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 29 (§15.2.3)', 'In the knee-jerk reflex, the stimulus of tapping the patellar tendon is detected by a receptor in the', '["hamstring muscle", "quadriceps muscle of the thigh", "skin of the knee", "spinal cord"]', 1, 'The receptor in the quadriceps generates an impulse in a sensory neuron that travels to the spinal cord.', '{"0": "The hamstrings are made to relax, not used as the receptor site.", "2": "The book places the receptor in the quadriceps, not the skin.", "3": "The spinal cord is where the impulse is passed to the motor neuron."}'),
  ('BIO-0108', 'Coordination and control', 'Reflex arc', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 29 (§15.2.3)', 'In the knee-jerk reflex, the sensory neuron passes the impulse to the motor neuron of the quadriceps', '["directly, without an inter-neuron", "through two inter-neurons in the brain", "through the cerebellum", "only after the hamstrings contract"]', 0, 'The sensory neuron synapses straight onto the motor neuron in the spinal cord; an inter-neuron is used only to inhibit the hamstrings.', '{"1": "The pathway does not go to the brain.", "2": "The cerebellum is not part of this reflex arc.", "3": "The hamstrings relax during the reflex."}'),
  ('BIO-0109', 'Coordination and control', 'Reflex arc', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 30 (§15.2.3)', 'During the knee-jerk, the hamstring muscles relax because', '["an inter-neuron in the spinal cord inhibits the motor neurons to the hamstrings", "the quadriceps motor neuron also stimulates the hamstrings", "the brain sends a voluntary command", "the patellar tendon is cut off from the spinal cord"]', 0, 'The sensory neuron also activates an inter-neuron, which releases inhibitory chemicals on the motor neurons serving the hamstrings.', '{"1": "Stimulating the hamstrings would oppose the kick.", "2": "The reflex is involuntary.", "3": "The tendon stays connected; its stretch starts the reflex."}'),
  ('BIO-0110', 'Coordination and control', 'Membrane potential', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 30 (§15.3.1)', 'The resting membrane potential of a neuron is about', '["+70 mV", "–70 mV", "–7 mV", "0 mV"]', 1, 'A resting (polarized) neuron has an inside about 70 mV negative relative to the outside.', '{"0": "The inside of a resting neuron is negative.", "2": "The value is ten times larger.", "3": "Zero would mean no potential difference."}'),
  ('BIO-0111', 'Coordination and control', 'Membrane potential', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 30 (§15.3.1)', 'Large negatively charged molecules such as proteins and nucleic acids that stay inside the neuron are called', '["neurotransmitters", "cations", "fixed anions", "cytokines"]', 2, 'These fixed anions cannot diffuse out and are a major reason the inside of the neuron is negative.', '{"0": "Neurotransmitters are released at synapses.", "1": "Cations are positive ions.", "3": "Cytokines are immune proteins."}'),
  ('BIO-0112', 'Coordination and control', 'Membrane potential', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 31 (§15.3.1)', 'In a resting neuron, how do the concentrations of K⁺ and Na⁺ compare inside and outside the cell?', '["K⁺ is about 10 times higher outside; Na⁺ is about 30 times higher inside", "Both ions are equal on the two sides", "Both ions are higher inside the cell", "K⁺ is about 30 times higher inside; Na⁺ is about 10 times higher outside"]', 3, 'The Na⁺–K⁺ pump keeps K⁺ concentrated inside (about 30×) and Na⁺ outside (about 10×).', '{"0": "This reverses both gradients.", "1": "The pump maintains unequal concentrations.", "2": "Na⁺ is higher outside."}'),
  ('BIO-0113', 'Coordination and control', 'Membrane potential', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 31 (§15.3.1)', 'The sodium–potassium pump makes the inside of the neuron more negative because for each cycle it', '["moves 2 Na⁺ out and 3 K⁺ in, using ATP", "moves 3 Na⁺ in and 2 K⁺ out, without ATP", "moves equal numbers of Na⁺ and K⁺ in opposite directions", "moves 3 Na⁺ out and 2 K⁺ in, using ATP"]', 3, 'Three positive charges leave for every two that enter, so there is a net loss of positive charge from inside.', '{"0": "This would add positive charge inside.", "1": "The pump works against the gradient and needs ATP.", "2": "Equal exchange would not change the charge balance."}'),
  ('BIO-0114', 'Coordination and control', 'Action potential', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 31 (§15.3.1)', 'Depolarization of a neuron membrane is caused by', '["opening of Na⁺ gates and rapid inward movement of Na⁺", "opening of K⁺ gates and outward movement of K⁺", "closing of all ion gates", "inward movement of Cl⁻ ions"]', 0, 'When Na⁺ gates open, Na⁺ rushes in and the inside becomes positive, reversing the polarity.', '{"1": "K⁺ outflow causes repolarization.", "2": "Ion movement is needed for an action potential.", "3": "Cl⁻ inflow makes the inside more negative (inhibition)."}'),
  ('BIO-0115', 'Coordination and control', 'Action potential', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 31 (§15.3.1)', 'Repolarization of the neuron membrane occurs when', '["Na⁺ gates open and Na⁺ diffuses in", "Ca²⁺ gates open in the axon membrane", "Na⁺ gates close and K⁺ gates open, so K⁺ diffuses out", "the myelin sheath is removed"]', 2, 'After depolarization, Na⁺ gates close and K⁺ leaves the cell, making the inside negative again.', '{"0": "This describes depolarization.", "1": "Ca²⁺ gates are involved at the synaptic knob.", "3": "Myelin is not involved in repolarization."}'),
  ('BIO-0116', 'Coordination and control', 'Action potential', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 31 (§15.3.1)', 'The brief hyperpolarization after an action potential is due to', '["extra Na⁺ entering the cell", "a slight delay in closing all the K⁺ gates", "Ca²⁺ leaving the axon", "the sodium–potassium pump stopping"]', 1, 'Because some K⁺ gates close late, extra K⁺ leaves and the potential briefly falls below the resting value.', '{"0": "Na⁺ entry would make the inside less negative.", "2": "Ca²⁺ movement is not the cause given.", "3": "The pump restores the resting distribution afterwards."}'),
  ('BIO-0117', 'Coordination and control', 'Action potential', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 31 (§15.3.1)', 'The period during which a neuron cannot generate another action potential is the', '["resting period", "refractory period", "synaptic delay", "depolarization phase"]', 1, 'During hyperpolarization the neuron cannot fire again; this is the refractory period.', '{"0": "At rest the neuron can be stimulated.", "2": "Synaptic delay is the time to cross a synapse.", "3": "Depolarization is part of the action potential itself."}'),
  ('BIO-0118', 'Coordination and control', 'Propagation', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 32 (§15.3.1)', 'An action potential moves along an axon because', '["the same ions travel all the way along the axon", "the action potential gets stronger as it moves", "neurotransmitter is released at every point of the axon", "each active region depolarizes the next region, which produces its own action potential"]', 3, 'Action potentials are reproduced at adjacent regions; the previous region meanwhile repolarizes.', '{"0": "Ions move across the membrane locally; they do not travel the whole length.", "1": "The amplitude stays constant along the axon.", "2": "Neurotransmitters are released at the synapse, not along the axon."}'),
  ('BIO-0119', 'Coordination and control', 'Propagation', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 32 (§15.3.2)', 'Which statement about the action potential along an axon is correct?', '["Its strength stays the same, but its speed depends on axon diameter and myelination", "It weakens as it travels along the axon", "Its speed is the same in all neurons", "It is faster in thin non-myelinated axons"]', 0, 'The amplitude at the end of the axon equals the first one; velocity is greater in thicker or myelinated axons.', '{"1": "The amplitude does not fall.", "2": "Speed varies with diameter and myelination.", "3": "Thin non-myelinated axons are the slowest."}'),
  ('BIO-0120', 'Coordination and control', 'Propagation', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 33 (§15.3.2)', 'In humans, myelinated neurons can conduct impulses at up to', '["3 m/s", "12 m/s", "120 m/s", "1200 m/s"]', 2, 'Non-myelinated neurons conduct at 1–3 m/s; myelinated ones at up to 120 m/s.', '{"0": "This is the upper speed of non-myelinated neurons.", "1": "Too slow for the fastest myelinated neurons.", "3": "Far too fast."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-0121', 'Coordination and control', 'Propagation', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 33 (§15.3.2)', 'Saltatory conduction is faster than conduction in non-myelinated axons because', '["myelin itself conducts electricity like a wire", "action potentials are produced only at the nodes of Ranvier, skipping the myelinated regions", "Na⁺ gates are spread evenly under the myelin", "neurotransmitters carry the impulse between nodes"]', 1, 'The impulse jumps from node to node; it can be up to 50 times faster than in the fastest non-myelinated axons.', '{"0": "Myelin is an insulator.", "2": "Action potentials are produced only at the nodes.", "3": "Neurotransmitters act at synapses only."}'),
  ('BIO-0122', 'Coordination and control', 'Synapse', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 33 (§15.4)', 'The fluid-filled gap between the presynaptic neuron and the postsynaptic cell is the', '["synaptic cleft", "node of Ranvier", "synaptic knob", "dorsal root"]', 0, 'At a synapse the two cells are not in direct contact; the gap between them is the synaptic cleft.', '{"1": "A node of Ranvier is a gap in the myelin sheath.", "2": "A synaptic knob is the swollen end of the axon terminal.", "3": "The dorsal root is a branch of a spinal nerve."}'),
  ('BIO-0123', 'Coordination and control', 'Synapse', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 33 (§15.4)', 'The synapse between a motor neuron and a muscle fibre is also called a', '["node of Ranvier", "ganglion", "neuromuscular junction", "gyrus"]', 2, 'A motor neuron–muscle fibre synapse is known as the neuromuscular junction.', '{"0": "A node is a gap in myelin.", "1": "A ganglion is a cluster of cell bodies.", "3": "A gyrus is a ridge on the cerebrum."}'),
  ('BIO-0124', 'Coordination and control', 'Synaptic transmission', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 33 (§15.4.1)', 'What is the first event when an action potential reaches the synaptic knob?', '["Neurotransmitter binds to the postsynaptic receptors", "Na⁺ gates open in the postsynaptic membrane", "Acetylcholinesterase breaks down the transmitter", "Calcium gates in the presynaptic membrane open"]', 3, 'Ca²⁺ gates open and Ca²⁺ diffuses in; this makes vesicles fuse with the membrane and release neurotransmitter.', '{"0": "Binding happens after the transmitter is released.", "1": "Postsynaptic Na⁺ gates open only after binding.", "2": "Breakdown happens last, after the transmitter has acted."}'),
  ('BIO-0125', 'Coordination and control', 'Synaptic transmission', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 33 (§15.4.1)', 'Neurotransmitters are released from synaptic vesicles into the cleft by', '["osmosis", "endocytosis", "active transport of single molecules through channels", "exocytosis"]', 3, 'The vesicles fuse with the presynaptic membrane and release their contents by exocytosis.', '{"0": "Osmosis is the movement of water.", "1": "Endocytosis takes material into a cell.", "2": "Release is by vesicle fusion, not single-molecule pumping."}'),
  ('BIO-0126', 'Coordination and control', 'Synaptic transmission', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 33 (§15.4.1)', 'A higher frequency of action potentials arriving at a presynaptic axon results in', '["larger action potentials in the axon", "closing of the calcium gates", "release of more neurotransmitter", "conversion of the synapse into an inhibitory one"]', 2, 'The book notes that the higher the frequency of presynaptic action potentials, the more neurotransmitter is released.', '{"0": "Action potential size stays constant.", "1": "Calcium gates open with each action potential.", "3": "Whether a synapse excites or inhibits depends on the transmitter and receptor."}'),
  ('BIO-0127', 'Coordination and control', 'Synaptic transmission', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 34 (§15.4.1)', 'After acting, neurotransmitters are removed from the synaptic cleft by', '["diffusion into the myelin sheath", "reabsorption into the presynaptic neuron or breakdown by enzymes", "staying in the cleft permanently", "being converted into calcium ions"]', 1, 'At many synapses the presynaptic neuron takes them back; at others enzymes break them down.', '{"0": "Myelin does not absorb neurotransmitters.", "2": "They do not remain indefinitely.", "3": "Neurotransmitters are not converted into calcium."}'),
  ('BIO-0128', 'Coordination and control', 'Neurotransmitters', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 34 (§15.4.2)', 'Excitatory neurotransmitters generate an action potential in the postsynaptic neuron by', '["increasing membrane permeability to Na⁺", "opening chloride channels", "making the inside more negative", "blocking all ion channels"]', 0, 'They open Na⁺ channels, so Na⁺ enters and the postsynaptic membrane depolarizes.', '{"1": "Opening Cl⁻ channels is the action of inhibitory transmitters.", "2": "A more negative inside is hyperpolarization (inhibition).", "3": "Blocking channels would prevent an action potential."}'),
  ('BIO-0129', 'Coordination and control', 'Neurotransmitters', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 34 (§15.4.2)', 'Which excitatory neurotransmitter acts in the CNS according to the book?', '["Glutamate", "GABA", "Glycine", "Endorphin"]', 0, 'Glutamate is given as an excitatory neurotransmitter of the CNS.', '{"1": "GABA is the chief inhibitory transmitter of the CNS.", "2": "Glycine is inhibitory, especially in the spinal cord.", "3": "Endorphins are inhibitory and reduce pain signals."}'),
  ('BIO-0130', 'Coordination and control', 'Neurotransmitters', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 34 (§15.4.2)', 'Many antidepressant drugs work by', '["destroying serotonin-releasing neurons", "blocking the removal of serotonin from the synaptic cleft", "blocking the release of dopamine", "increasing the breakdown of serotonin"]', 1, 'Low serotonin activity can cause depression; keeping serotonin in the cleft longer reduces depression.', '{"0": "Destroying those neurons would worsen the problem.", "2": "This is not how the book describes antidepressants.", "3": "Faster breakdown would lower serotonin activity."}'),
  ('BIO-0131', 'Coordination and control', 'Neurotransmitters', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 34 (§15.4.2)', 'Degeneration of dopamine-releasing neurons produces the muscle tremors of', '["multiple sclerosis", "meningitis", "diabetes insipidus", "Parkinson''s disease"]', 3, 'Loss of dopamine-releasing neurons in the brain leads to the tremors and movement problems of Parkinson''s disease.', '{"0": "Multiple sclerosis is damage to myelin sheaths.", "1": "Meningitis is inflammation of the meninges.", "2": "Diabetes insipidus is due to lack of ADH."}'),
  ('BIO-0132', 'Coordination and control', 'Neurotransmitters', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 35 (§15.4.2)', 'About 90% of the body''s serotonin is produced by neurons in the', '["brain", "spinal cord", "intestine", "adrenal glands"]', 2, 'Serotonin is made about 90% by intestinal neurons and about 10% in the CNS.', '{"0": "The CNS makes only about 10%.", "1": "The spinal cord is part of the CNS (the 10%).", "3": "Adrenal glands release epinephrine."}'),
  ('BIO-0133', 'Coordination and control', 'Neurotransmitters', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 35 (§15.4.2)', 'Inhibitory neurotransmitters reduce the chance of an impulse in the postsynaptic neuron by', '["opening sodium channels so Na⁺ enters", "opening calcium channels in the postsynaptic cell", "removing the myelin sheath", "opening chloride channels so Cl⁻ enters and hyperpolarizes the membrane"]', 3, 'Cl⁻ entering makes the inside more negative than the resting potential, so the neuron is harder to excite.', '{"0": "Opening Na⁺ channels would excite the cell.", "1": "This is not the mechanism given.", "2": "Myelin is not involved at synapses."}'),
  ('BIO-0134', 'Coordination and control', 'Neurotransmitters', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 35 (§15.4.2)', 'The chief inhibitory neurotransmitter of the CNS is', '["GABA (gamma-aminobutyric acid)", "acetylcholine", "glutamate", "norepinephrine"]', 0, 'GABA reduces neural excitability, lowers anxiety and promotes relaxation and sleep; many sedatives enhance its binding.', '{"1": "Acetylcholine is excitatory at neuromuscular junctions.", "2": "Glutamate is excitatory in the CNS.", "3": "Norepinephrine is listed among excitatory biogenic amines."}'),
  ('BIO-0135', 'Coordination and control', 'Neurotransmitters', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 35 (§15.4.2)', 'Morphine and heroin relieve pain because they', '["block the release of GABA", "resemble endorphins and bind to endorphin receptors", "stimulate nociceptors", "destroy acetylcholinesterase"]', 1, 'Endorphins reduce release of excitatory transmitters carrying pain messages; opium derivatives are chemically similar and bind the same receptors.', '{"0": "GABA is not the basis of their analgesic effect in the book.", "2": "Stimulating pain receptors would increase pain.", "3": "Inhibiting acetylcholinesterase is the action of nerve gases."}'),
  ('BIO-0136', 'Coordination and control', 'Neurotransmitters', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 35 (§15.4.2)', 'Nerve gases such as sarin can stop a person breathing because they', '["block the release of acetylcholine, so the diaphragm cannot contract", "destroy the myelin of the phrenic nerve", "inhibit acetylcholinesterase, so acetylcholine accumulates and the diaphragm stays contracted", "increase the breakdown of acetylcholine"]', 2, 'With the enzyme blocked, acetylcholine builds up and over-stimulates skeletal muscles; a diaphragm that stays contracted cannot work in breathing.', '{"0": "Nerve gases cause too much, not too little, acetylcholine.", "1": "They act on an enzyme, not on myelin.", "3": "They inhibit, not speed up, its breakdown."}'),
  ('BIO-0137', 'Coordination and control', 'Organization', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 36 (§15.5)', 'The peripheral nervous system consists of', '["the brain and spinal cord", "cranial and spinal nerves", "the brain and cranial nerves only", "the spinal cord and spinal nerves only"]', 1, 'The CNS is the brain and spinal cord; the PNS is the cranial and spinal nerves arising from them.', '{"0": "These form the CNS.", "2": "The brain is part of the CNS.", "3": "The spinal cord is part of the CNS."}'),
  ('BIO-0138', 'Coordination and control', 'Meninges', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 36 (§15.5.1)', 'Which meningeal layer is the outermost, lies just below the skull and contains larger blood vessels?', '["Arachnoid", "Pia mater", "Dura mater", "Corpus callosum"]', 2, 'The meninges from outside in are the dura mater, arachnoid and pia mater.', '{"0": "The arachnoid is the thin middle layer.", "1": "The pia mater is the innermost, delicate layer.", "3": "The corpus callosum is a band of axons joining the hemispheres."}'),
  ('BIO-0139', 'Coordination and control', 'Brain', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 37 (§15.5.1)', 'The average adult human brain weighs about', '["1.4 kg, about 2% of body weight", "4 kg, about 10% of body weight", "0.4 kg, about 0.5% of body weight", "14 kg, about 20% of body weight"]', 0, 'The brain weighs about 1.4 kg and contains about 100 billion inter-neurons.', '{"1": "Too heavy.", "2": "Too light.", "3": "Far too heavy."}'),
  ('BIO-0140', 'Coordination and control', 'Brain', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 37 (§15.5.1)', 'The two cerebral hemispheres are connected at the base of the deep groove by the', '["pons", "thalamus", "pia mater", "corpus callosum"]', 3, 'The corpus callosum is a band of axons joining the left and right hemispheres.', '{"0": "The pons links the cerebrum with the cerebellum via the brainstem.", "1": "The thalamus is a relay centre in the diencephalon.", "2": "The pia mater is a meningeal layer."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-0141', 'Coordination and control', 'Brain', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 37 (§15.5.1)', 'Damage to the left cerebral hemisphere is most likely to cause paralysis of the', '["left side of the body", "right side of the body", "both legs only", "face only on the left side"]', 1, 'Each hemisphere receives sensation from and sends motor commands to the opposite side of the body.', '{"0": "The control is crossed, so the left side would be spared.", "2": "There is no reason for both legs only to be affected.", "3": "Effects appear on the opposite side."}'),
  ('BIO-0142', 'Coordination and control', 'Brain', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 38 (§15.5.1)', 'Self-control, planning, reasoning and abstract thought are functions of the', '["occipital lobes", "temporal lobes", "frontal lobes", "parietal lobes"]', 2, 'The frontal lobes deal with executive functions.', '{"0": "The occipital lobes control vision.", "1": "The temporal lobes deal with visual memory, language comprehension and emotion.", "3": "The parietal lobes process sensory information and body position."}'),
  ('BIO-0143', 'Coordination and control', 'Brain', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 38 (§15.5.1)', 'The auditory cortex, which deals with different sound frequencies, lies in the', '["occipital lobe", "frontal lobe", "cerebellum", "temporal lobe"]', 3, 'The auditory cortex is within the temporal lobe; the visual cortex is in the occipital lobe.', '{"0": "The occipital lobe holds the visual cortex.", "1": "The frontal lobe is for executive functions.", "2": "The cerebellum coordinates muscle actions."}'),
  ('BIO-0144', 'Coordination and control', 'Brain', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 38 (§15.5.1)', 'A deep groove on the cerebral hemisphere is called a', '["fissure", "sulcus", "gyrus", "ganglion"]', 0, 'Ridges are gyri; shallow grooves are sulci; deep grooves are fissures.', '{"1": "A sulcus is a shallow groove.", "2": "A gyrus is a ridge.", "3": "A ganglion is a cluster of cell bodies outside the CNS."}'),
  ('BIO-0145', 'Coordination and control', 'Brain', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 38 (§15.5.1)', 'Higher mental activities, such as language on the left side and visual-spatial ability on the right, are sites of the', '["motor areas only", "medulla oblongata", "association areas of the cerebral cortex", "cerebellum"]', 2, 'Association areas are the sites of higher mental activity; some functions are localized to one side.', '{"0": "Motor areas send impulses to skeletal muscles.", "1": "The medulla controls heart rate and breathing.", "3": "The cerebellum coordinates movements."}'),
  ('BIO-0146', 'Coordination and control', 'Brain', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 39 (§15.5.1)', 'Which part of the brainstem is a control centre for heart rate and respiration rate?', '["Medulla oblongata", "Pons", "Midbrain", "Thalamus"]', 0, 'The medulla is both a relay centre and a control centre for heart rate, breathing and other homeostatic activities.', '{"1": "The pons relays impulses between the cerebrum and cerebellum.", "2": "The midbrain relays visual and auditory information.", "3": "The thalamus is in the diencephalon, not the brainstem."}'),
  ('BIO-0147', 'Coordination and control', 'Brain', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 39 (§15.5.1)', 'The pons transmits impulses between the', '["eyes and the occipital lobe only", "cerebral hemispheres and the cerebellum", "hypothalamus and the pituitary", "left and right kidneys"]', 1, 'The pons, part of the brainstem, carries impulses between the cerebrum and the cerebellum.', '{"0": "Visual relay is a midbrain role.", "2": "The hypothalamus controls the pituitary directly.", "3": "The pons has nothing to do with the kidneys."}'),
  ('BIO-0148', 'Coordination and control', 'Brain', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 39 (§15.5.1)', 'The thalamus acts as', '["the centre for hunger and thirst", "the coordinator of posture and balance", "the centre of long-term memory formation", "a relay centre between sensory information and the cerebrum"]', 3, 'The thalamus relays sensory information to the cerebrum; the hypothalamus regulates hunger and thirst.', '{"0": "Hunger and thirst are regulated by the hypothalamus.", "1": "Posture is controlled by the cerebellum.", "2": "Memory consolidation involves the hippocampus."}'),
  ('BIO-0149', 'Coordination and control', 'Brain', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 39 (§15.5.1)', 'Which structure regulates body temperature, hunger, thirst and the pituitary gland?', '["Thalamus", "Hypothalamus", "Amygdala", "Pons"]', 1, 'The hypothalamus integrates visceral activities and controls the pituitary, which regulates many other endocrine glands.', '{"0": "The thalamus is a sensory relay.", "2": "The amygdala deals with emotions such as fear and anger.", "3": "The pons relays impulses to the cerebellum."}'),
  ('BIO-0150', 'Coordination and control', 'Brain', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 39 (§15.5.1)', 'The hormone melatonin, which controls sleep patterns, is produced by the', '["hippocampus", "pituitary gland", "pineal gland", "medulla oblongata"]', 2, 'The pineal gland, where the two halves of the thalamus join, produces melatonin.', '{"0": "The hippocampus is involved in memory.", "1": "The pituitary is not named as the source of melatonin.", "3": "The medulla controls heart rate and breathing."}'),
  ('BIO-0151', 'Coordination and control', 'Brain', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 39 (§15.5.1)', 'The almond-shaped structures of the limbic system that control emotions such as fear, anger and rage are the', '["hippocampi", "thalami", "olfactory bulbs", "amygdalae"]', 3, 'The two amygdalae, deep in the temporal lobes, control feelings such as love, hate, anger, fear and rage.', '{"0": "The hippocampus consolidates memory.", "1": "The thalamus relays sensory information.", "2": "Olfactory bulbs receive smell impulses."}'),
  ('BIO-0152', 'Coordination and control', 'Brain', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 39 (§15.5.1)', 'Consolidation of information from short-term to long-term memory involves the', '["hippocampus", "amygdala", "cerebellum", "medulla oblongata"]', 0, 'The hippocampus, two "horns" curving back from the amygdala, helps convert short-term into long-term memory.', '{"1": "The amygdala deals with emotion.", "2": "The cerebellum coordinates movement.", "3": "The medulla controls vital reflexes."}'),
  ('BIO-0153', 'Coordination and control', 'Brain', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 39 (§15.5.1)', 'Which part of the brain coordinates rapid movements and controls posture using information from muscles, joints, eyes and ears?', '["Cerebrum''s occipital lobe", "Thalamus", "Cerebellum", "Hypothalamus"]', 2, 'The cerebellum, below and behind the cerebrum, processes body position and coordinates skeletal muscle actions.', '{"0": "The occipital lobe controls vision.", "1": "The thalamus is a sensory relay.", "3": "The hypothalamus regulates visceral activities."}'),
  ('BIO-0154', 'Coordination and control', 'Brain', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 40 (§15.5.1)', 'The fourth ventricle of the brain lies', '["between the two thalami", "inside each cerebral hemisphere", "in the occipital lobe only", "between the cerebellum and the brainstem and continues into the central canal of the spinal cord"]', 3, 'There are four ventricles: two lateral (cerebrum), the third (between the thalami) and the fourth (between cerebellum and brainstem).', '{"0": "That is the third ventricle.", "1": "Those are the lateral ventricles.", "2": "No ventricle is limited to the occipital lobe."}'),
  ('BIO-0155', 'Coordination and control', 'Spinal cord', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 40 (§15.5.1)', 'How does the arrangement of grey and white matter in the spinal cord compare with the brain?', '["It is the opposite: grey matter is inside (H-shaped) and white matter outside", "It is the same: grey matter outside and white matter inside", "The spinal cord has only white matter", "The spinal cord has only grey matter"]', 0, 'In the spinal cord the inner butterfly-shaped zone is grey matter and the outer zone is white matter, the reverse of the cerebrum.', '{"1": "The arrangement is reversed.", "2": "It has both grey and white matter.", "3": "It has both grey and white matter."}'),
  ('BIO-0156', 'Coordination and control', 'Cranial nerves', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 41 (§15.5.2)', 'How many pairs of cranial nerves arise from the human brain?', '["31", "12", "10", "8"]', 1, 'There are 12 pairs of cranial nerves; the first two arise from the cerebrum and the other 10 from the brainstem.', '{"0": "31 is the number of spinal nerve pairs.", "2": "10 pairs arise from the brainstem, but there are 12 in all.", "3": "8 is the number of cervical spinal nerve pairs."}'),
  ('BIO-0157', 'Coordination and control', 'Cranial nerves', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 41 (§15.5.2)', 'Which cranial nerve controls many organs including the heart, lungs and digestive system?', '["Cranial nerve I", "Cranial nerve VIII", "Cranial nerve X (vagus)", "Cranial nerve XII"]', 2, 'Cranial nerve X is a mixed nerve serving the heart, lungs and digestive system; many parasympathetic neurons run in it.', '{"0": "Nerve I carries smell.", "1": "Nerve VIII carries hearing and balance.", "3": "Nerve XII controls the tongue muscles."}'),
  ('BIO-0158', 'Coordination and control', 'Cranial nerves', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 41 (§15.5.2)', 'Which cranial nerve is purely sensory and serves hearing and balance?', '["V", "VII", "XI", "VIII"]', 3, 'Cranial nerve VIII is sensory for hearing and balance.', '{"0": "V is mixed (face sensation and chewing).", "1": "VII is mixed (facial expression and taste at the front of the tongue).", "2": "XI is motor to neck and shoulder muscles."}'),
  ('BIO-0159', 'Coordination and control', 'Spinal nerves', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 42 (§15.5.2)', 'The 31 pairs of human spinal nerves include', '["8 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar, 5 sacral and 1 coccygeal pair", "7 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar, 5 sacral and 2 coccygeal pairs", "8 cervical, 10 thoracic, 6 lumbar, 6 sacral and 1 coccygeal pair", "12 cervical, 8 thoracic, 5 lumbar, 5 sacral and 1 coccygeal pair"]', 0, '8 + 12 + 5 + 5 + 1 = 31 pairs, as listed in the book.', '{"1": "There are 8 cervical and 1 coccygeal pair.", "2": "There are 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar and 5 sacral pairs.", "3": "Cervical and thoracic numbers are swapped."}'),
  ('BIO-0160', 'Coordination and control', 'Spinal nerves', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 42 (§15.5.2)', 'The ventral root of a spinal nerve contains', '["cell bodies of sensory neurons", "axons of motor neurons", "only inter-neurons", "the dorsal root ganglion"]', 1, 'The ventral root carries motor axons; the dorsal root carries sensory fibres, whose cell bodies sit in the dorsal root ganglion.', '{"0": "Sensory cell bodies are in the dorsal root ganglion.", "2": "Inter-neurons lie entirely in the CNS.", "3": "The ganglion is on the dorsal root."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-0161', 'Coordination and control', 'Autonomic nervous system', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 42 (§15.5.3)', 'The somatic nervous system controls', '["skeletal muscles", "smooth muscles of the gut", "cardiac muscle", "glands"]', 0, 'Somatic motor neurons control skeletal muscles; the autonomic system controls smooth muscles and glands.', '{"1": "Smooth muscle is under autonomic control.", "2": "Heart muscle is regulated by the autonomic system.", "3": "Glands are controlled by the autonomic system."}'),
  ('BIO-0162', 'Coordination and control', 'Autonomic nervous system', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 42 (§15.5.3)', 'Motor neurons of the sympathetic nervous system originate from the', '["brain and sacral region of the spinal cord", "cervical region only", "thoracic and lumbar regions of the spinal cord", "cerebellum"]', 2, 'Sympathetic neurons arise from the thoracic and lumbar spinal cord; parasympathetic neurons arise from the brain and sacral cord.', '{"0": "This is the origin of the parasympathetic system.", "1": "The cervical region alone is not the origin.", "3": "The cerebellum coordinates movement."}'),
  ('BIO-0163', 'Coordination and control', 'Autonomic nervous system', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 43 (§15.5.3)', 'Which effect is produced by the sympathetic nervous system?', '["Increased secretion of saliva", "Increased motility of the digestive tract", "Contraction of the urinary bladder", "Dilation of the pupils and bronchioles"]', 3, 'Sympathetic activity prepares for fight or flight: pupils and bronchioles dilate, the heart speeds up and blood glucose rises.', '{"0": "Increased saliva is parasympathetic.", "1": "Increased gut motility is parasympathetic.", "2": "Bladder contraction is parasympathetic; sympathetic relaxes it."}'),
  ('BIO-0164', 'Coordination and control', 'Autonomic nervous system', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 43 (§15.5.3)', 'According to the table in the book, the sympathetic system causes the blood vessels in the skin to', '["dilate, while the parasympathetic system constricts them", "constrict, while the parasympathetic system has no effect", "constrict, while the parasympathetic system dilates them", "dilate, while the parasympathetic system has no effect"]', 1, 'Sympathetic stimulation constricts skin vessels and dilates muscle vessels; the parasympathetic has no effect on either.', '{"0": "Sympathetic constricts skin vessels.", "2": "The parasympathetic has no effect on them.", "3": "Dilation applies to vessels in muscles."}'),
  ('BIO-0165', 'Coordination and control', 'Autonomic nervous system', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 43 (§15.5.3)', 'Many parasympathetic motor neurons run in the', '["vagus nerve (cranial nerve X)", "optic nerve (cranial nerve II)", "thoracic spinal nerves", "lumbar spinal nerves"]', 0, 'Parasympathetic neurons arise from the brain and sacral cord; many travel in the vagus nerve.', '{"1": "The optic nerve carries vision.", "2": "Thoracic nerves carry sympathetic outflow.", "3": "Lumbar nerves carry sympathetic outflow."}'),
  ('BIO-0166', 'Coordination and control', 'Receptors', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 44 (§15.6.1)', 'A taste bud is made up of receptor cells with hair-like structures and', '["Schwann cells that make myelin", "basal cells that make new receptor cells", "Merkel cells that sense touch", "rods that sense light"]', 1, 'Taste buds are bulb-shaped, open by a taste pore and contain receptor cells plus basal cells that replace them.', '{"0": "Schwann cells are glial cells of the PNS.", "2": "Merkel cells are touch receptors in the epidermis.", "3": "Rods are in the retina."}'),
  ('BIO-0167', 'Coordination and control', 'Receptors', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 44 (§15.6.1)', 'Taste impulses travel to the brainstem along cranial nerves', '["I, II and III", "V, VIII and XI", "VII, IX and X", "III, IV and VI"]', 2, 'Taste is carried by cranial nerves VII, IX and X to the brainstem and then to the taste areas of the cortex.', '{"0": "I is smell, II vision, III eye movement.", "1": "VIII is hearing; V and XI are not taste nerves.", "3": "These move the eyes."}'),
  ('BIO-0168', 'Coordination and control', 'Receptors', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 45 (§15.6.2)', 'Odour molecules are detected after they', '["bind directly to the olfactory bulb", "enter the blood and reach the brain", "are trapped by taste pores", "dissolve in the mucus and bind to receptor sites on the cilia of olfactory neurons"]', 3, 'Airborne chemicals dissolve in the nasal mucus and bind to the cilia of sensory neurons, generating impulses in cranial nerve I.', '{"0": "The bulb receives impulses, not odour molecules.", "1": "Smell is detected in the nasal epithelium.", "2": "Taste pores are on taste buds."}'),
  ('BIO-0169', 'Coordination and control', 'Receptors', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 46 (§15.6.3)', 'Which skin receptors detect light touch and vibration and are found especially in lips, fingertips and palms?', '["Meissner''s corpuscles", "Ruffini endings", "Pacinian corpuscles", "Nociceptors"]', 0, 'Meissner''s corpuscles are encapsulated endings in the upper skin that detect light touch and vibration.', '{"1": "Ruffini endings detect sustained pressure and stretch.", "2": "Pacinian corpuscles, deeper in the skin, detect deep pressure and vibration.", "3": "Nociceptors detect pain."}'),
  ('BIO-0170', 'Coordination and control', 'Receptors', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 46 (§15.6.3)', 'Ruffini endings in the dermis detect', '["light touch on the fingertips", "pain from injured cells", "movement of hairs", "sustained pressure and stretching"]', 3, 'Ruffini endings are encapsulated nerve endings that respond to sustained pressure and stretch.', '{"0": "Light touch is detected by Meissner''s corpuscles and Merkel cells.", "1": "Pain is detected by nociceptors.", "2": "Hair follicle receptors detect hair movement."}'),
  ('BIO-0171', 'Coordination and control', 'Receptors', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 45 (§15.6.3)', 'Cold receptors differ from warm receptors in that they lie', '["in the hypothalamus only", "deeper in the dermis than warm receptors", "immediately below the epidermis, while warm receptors lie slightly deeper in the dermis", "only in the tongue"]', 2, 'Both are naked dendrite endings; cold receptors are just below the epidermis and warm ones a little deeper.', '{"0": "Thermoreceptors are also in the hypothalamus, but cold skin receptors lie under the epidermis.", "1": "The positions are reversed.", "3": "They are found in the skin throughout the body."}'),
  ('BIO-0172', 'Coordination and control', 'Drugs', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 46 (§15.7)', 'Which statement about narcotics is correct according to the book?', '["All psychoactive drugs are narcotics", "All narcotics are psychoactive, but not all psychoactive drugs are narcotics", "Caffeine is a narcotic opioid", "Narcotics have no potential for addiction"]', 1, 'Narcotics are opioids used for pain relief (e.g. morphine, codeine, oxycodone) with high addiction potential; caffeine and nicotine are psychoactive but not narcotics.', '{"0": "Alcohol, caffeine and nicotine are psychoactive but not narcotics.", "2": "Caffeine is a stimulant, not an opioid.", "3": "Narcotics have a high potential for addiction."}'),
  ('BIO-0173', 'Coordination and control', 'Drugs', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 47 (§15.7.1)', 'Drug tolerance means that', '["the person can stop the drug without any symptoms", "the drug becomes more effective with each dose", "the drug is no longer psychoactive", "the body becomes less sensitive to the drug, so higher doses are needed for the same effect"]', 3, 'With continued use the body adapts, so a larger dose is needed to get the same response.', '{"0": "Stopping after dependence causes withdrawal symptoms.", "1": "Tolerance is the opposite: the effect decreases.", "2": "The drug still acts on the brain."}'),
  ('BIO-0174', 'Coordination and control', 'Drugs', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 47 (§15.7.1)', 'According to the book, tolerance to addictive drugs develops at the synapse because', '["the presynaptic neuron stops releasing dopamine completely", "the brain reduces the number of dopamine receptors on the postsynaptic neuron", "the drug increases dopamine reabsorption", "new dopamine receptors are added to the postsynaptic neuron"]', 1, 'Drugs block dopamine reabsorption, dopamine builds up in the cleft, and the brain adapts by cutting postsynaptic receptor numbers.', '{"0": "Dopamine release continues.", "2": "The drug blocks, not increases, reabsorption.", "3": "Receptor numbers fall, not rise."}'),
  ('BIO-0175', 'Coordination and control', 'Drugs', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 48 (§15.7.1)', 'Regular caffeine users who suddenly stop may experience', '["increased alertness", "hallucinations of light only", "headaches and fatigue", "improved sleep at once with no symptoms"]', 2, 'Stopping caffeine abruptly after tolerance can cause withdrawal symptoms such as headache and fatigue.', '{"0": "Alertness falls without caffeine.", "1": "This is not the withdrawal described for caffeine.", "3": "Withdrawal symptoms do occur."}'),
  ('BIO-0176', 'Coordination and control', 'Drugs', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 48 (§15.7.2)', 'Cannabis drugs such as marijuana and hashish are made from', '["the dried flowering tops, leaves and stem of Cannabis sativa", "the opium poppy", "tobacco leaves", "coffee seeds"]', 0, 'Cannabis comes from the Indian hemp plant Cannabis sativa.', '{"1": "Heroin is derived from the opium poppy.", "2": "Tobacco is the source of nicotine.", "3": "Coffee is a source of caffeine."}'),
  ('BIO-0177', 'Coordination and control', 'Disorders', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 49 (§15.8.1)', 'A stroke is classified among nervous disorders as a', '["degenerative disorder", "functional disorder", "infectious disorder", "vascular disorder"]', 3, 'Strokes damage brain cells because blood supply to part of the brain is interrupted, so they are vascular disorders.', '{"0": "Degenerative disorders include Parkinson''s and Alzheimer''s.", "1": "Functional disorders, like headache, have no structural cause.", "2": "Infectious disorders include meningitis."}'),
  ('BIO-0178', 'Coordination and control', 'Disorders', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 50 (§15.8.1)', 'A haemorrhagic stroke occurs when', '["a brain blood vessel becomes narrowed or blocked", "the meninges become infected", "a blood vessel in the brain leaks or ruptures", "dopamine-releasing neurons die"]', 2, 'Haemorrhagic stroke is bleeding from a ruptured vessel; ischemic stroke is due to narrowing or blockage.', '{"0": "That is an ischemic stroke.", "1": "That is meningitis.", "3": "That is Parkinson''s disease."}'),
  ('BIO-0179', 'Coordination and control', 'Disorders', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 50 (§15.8.2)', 'Which type of meningitis is described as the most severe and life-threatening?', '["Bacterial meningitis", "Viral meningitis", "Fungal meningitis", "Parasitic meningitis in healthy adults"]', 0, 'Bacterial meningitis is most severe and needs immediate antibiotics; viral is usually milder and self-limiting.', '{"1": "Viral meningitis is usually less severe.", "2": "Fungal meningitis is rare, mostly in people with weak immunity.", "3": "The book does not describe parasitic meningitis as the most severe."}'),
  ('BIO-0180', 'Coordination and control', 'Disorders', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 51 (§15.8.4)', 'A secondary headache is one that', '["is due to the headache itself, such as migraine", "results from an underlying problem such as bleeding in the brain, a tumour or meningitis", "is always caused by stress", "never needs medical attention"]', 1, 'Primary headaches (migraine, tension headache) are not due to another cause; secondary ones are due to an underlying structural problem.', '{"0": "That is a primary headache.", "2": "Stress is linked to primary headaches.", "3": "An underlying cause such as a tumour needs attention."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-0181', 'Coordination and control', 'Disorders', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 51 (§15.8.5)', 'Multiple sclerosis is an autoimmune disease of the CNS in which', '["the meninges are infected by bacteria", "the myelin sheath is damaged", "blood vessels of the brain rupture", "cranial nerve VIII is cut"]', 1, 'In multiple sclerosis the myelin sheath is damaged, leading to vision loss, pain, fatigue and poor coordination.', '{"0": "That is bacterial meningitis.", "2": "That is a haemorrhagic stroke.", "3": "This is not what MS is."}'),
  ('BIO-0182', 'Coordination and control', 'Disorders', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 51 (§15.8.5)', 'Huntington''s disease and Alzheimer''s disease are both examples of', '["degenerative disorders", "vascular disorders", "infectious disorders", "functional disorders"]', 0, 'Degenerative disorders involve progressive deterioration of nerve cells; examples are Parkinson''s, Huntington''s, MS and Alzheimer''s.', '{"1": "Vascular disorders involve blood vessels, like stroke.", "2": "Infectious disorders include meningitis.", "3": "Functional disorders have no structural cause, like headache."}'),
  ('BIO-0183', 'Coordination and control', 'Diagnostic tests', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 52 (§15.9.1)', 'Electroencephalography (EEG) works by', '["passing X-rays through the head", "using a strong magnetic field and radio waves", "injecting dye into the spinal cord", "recording the electrical activity of the brain through electrodes on the scalp"]', 3, 'EEG electrodes pick up electrical impulses among neurons; an amplifier magnifies them and they are shown as waveforms.', '{"0": "X-rays are used in CT.", "1": "Magnets and radio waves are used in MRI.", "2": "This is not part of an EEG."}'),
  ('BIO-0184', 'Coordination and control', 'Diagnostic tests', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 52 (§15.9.1)', 'On an EEG, alpha waves indicate', '["active problem-solving and high perception", "normal alert state with active thinking", "a physically and mentally relaxed state", "seizure activity only"]', 2, 'Gamma waves are for high perception, beta for alert active thinking, alpha for a relaxed state.', '{"0": "That describes gamma waves.", "1": "That describes beta waves.", "3": "The book does not link alpha waves to seizures."}'),
  ('BIO-0185', 'Coordination and control', 'Diagnostic tests', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 52 (§15.9.2)', 'The principle of a CT scan is that', '["different tissues contain different amounts of hydrogen atoms", "neurons produce electrical waves", "different tissues absorb X-rays to different degrees", "sound waves echo from organs"]', 2, 'An X-ray tube rotates in a gantry; detectors record absorption through tissues and a computer builds cross-sectional images.', '{"0": "That is the principle of MRI.", "1": "That is the principle of EEG.", "3": "That describes ultrasound, not CT."}'),
  ('BIO-0186', 'Coordination and control', 'Diagnostic tests', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 53 (§15.9.3)', 'MRI produces images by using', '["a strong magnetic field and radio waves acting on hydrogen atoms in tissues", "X-rays rotating around the patient", "electrodes attached to the scalp", "radioactive dyes injected into the blood"]', 0, 'Tissues differ in hydrogen content; radio waves make hydrogen atoms in the magnetic field give signals that a computer turns into 3-D images.', '{"1": "X-rays are used in CT.", "2": "Electrodes are used in EEG.", "3": "This is not described for MRI."}'),
  ('BIO-0187', 'Coordination and control', 'Hormones', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 57 (§16.1)', 'Compared with nervous coordination, the endocrine system mainly controls', '["long-term processes such as growth, metabolism and reproduction", "high-speed reflex actions", "only voluntary muscle movements", "the conduction of impulses along axons"]', 0, 'The nervous system gives fast coordination; the endocrine system controls long-term processes by hormones (chemical coordination).', '{"1": "Reflexes are nervous responses.", "2": "Voluntary muscles are controlled by the somatic nervous system.", "3": "Impulse conduction is a nervous function."}'),
  ('BIO-0188', 'Coordination and control', 'Hormones', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 57 (§16.1)', 'Endocrine glands differ from exocrine glands because they', '["release secretions into ducts", "are ductless and release secretions directly into blood or tissue fluid", "secrete only digestive enzymes", "are found only in the brain"]', 1, 'Exocrine glands secrete into ducts; endocrine glands are ductless and secrete hormones straight into blood or tissue fluid.', '{"0": "That describes exocrine glands.", "2": "Digestive enzymes come from exocrine glands.", "3": "Endocrine glands are found throughout the body."}'),
  ('BIO-0189', 'Coordination and control', 'Hormones', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 57 (§16.1)', 'A hormone reaches every cell carried by the blood, yet affects only certain cells because', '["it is destroyed before reaching other cells", "other cells lack a nucleus", "it is released only near its target", "only its target cells have specific receptors for it"]', 3, 'Each receptor binds a specific hormone; only target cells with that receptor respond.', '{"0": "The hormone does reach all cells.", "1": "Most body cells have a nucleus.", "2": "Blood carries it to every cell in the body."}'),
  ('BIO-0190', 'Coordination and control', 'Chemical nature', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 58 (§16.1.1)', 'Peptide and protein hormones such as insulin act by binding to receptors on the cell surface because they', '["are lipid-soluble and pass easily into the nucleus", "are made from cholesterol", "are water-soluble and cannot pass through the cell membrane", "are too small to be detected inside cells"]', 2, 'Chains of amino acids dissolve easily in blood but cannot cross the lipid membrane, so their receptors are on the surface.', '{"0": "That describes steroid hormones.", "1": "Steroids, not peptides, are made from cholesterol.", "3": "Size alone is not the reason given."}'),
  ('BIO-0191', 'Coordination and control', 'Chemical nature', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 58 (§16.1.1)', 'Which hormone is a glycoprotein?', '["Insulin", "Thyroid-stimulating hormone", "Thyroxine", "Cortisol"]', 1, 'TSH, FSH and LH from the anterior pituitary are glycoproteins: proteins joined to a carbohydrate group.', '{"0": "Insulin is a peptide/protein hormone.", "2": "Thyroxine is an amino acid derivative.", "3": "Cortisol is a steroid."}'),
  ('BIO-0192', 'Coordination and control', 'Chemical nature', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 58 (§16.1.1)', 'Amino-acid-derivative hormones are usually derived from', '["tyrosine or tryptophan", "cholesterol", "glucose", "fatty acids"]', 0, 'These small hormones come from a single amino acid, usually tyrosine or tryptophan.', '{"1": "Cholesterol is the source of steroid hormones.", "2": "Glucose is not the source of these hormones.", "3": "Fatty acids are not the source named."}'),
  ('BIO-0193', 'Coordination and control', 'Chemical nature', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 58 (§16.1.1)', 'Among amino-acid-derivative hormones, which can cross the cell membrane?', '["Epinephrine and norepinephrine", "Insulin and glucagon", "FSH and LH", "Thyroxine and tri-iodothyronine"]', 3, 'Whether they cross depends on whether they behave like proteins or lipids; T4 and T3 cross, epinephrine and norepinephrine do not.', '{"0": "The book states these cannot cross membranes.", "1": "These are peptides, not amino acid derivatives.", "2": "These are glycoproteins."}'),
  ('BIO-0194', 'Coordination and control', 'Chemical nature', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 59 (§16.1.1)', 'Steroid hormones can pass through the cell membrane and act on the nucleus because they are', '["large polar glycoproteins", "water-soluble chains of amino acids", "derived from cholesterol and are lipid-soluble", "derived from tyrosine"]', 2, 'Steroids such as cortisol, aldosterone, testosterone and estrogen are lipid-soluble, so they cross the membrane.', '{"0": "Glycoproteins cannot cross the membrane.", "1": "Peptides cannot cross the membrane.", "3": "Tyrosine derivatives are amino-acid-derived hormones."}'),
  ('BIO-0195', 'Coordination and control', 'Chemical nature', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 59 (§16.1.1)', 'Which of these is a steroid hormone?', '["Glucagon", "Oxytocin", "Epinephrine", "Aldosterone"]', 3, 'Aldosterone and cortisol (adrenal cortex), testosterone and estrogen/progesterone are steroids.', '{"0": "Glucagon is a peptide.", "1": "Oxytocin is a peptide.", "2": "Epinephrine is an amino acid derivative."}'),
  ('BIO-0196', 'Coordination and control', 'Hormones', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 58 (§16.1.1)', 'Bayliss and Starling discovered the first hormone, secretin, in 1902. Secretin is released when', '["food enters the mouth, and it makes salivary glands secrete", "blood glucose rises, and it makes the liver store glycogen", "acidic gastric juice enters the small intestine, and it makes the pancreas secrete pancreatic juice", "blood calcium falls, and it makes bones release calcium"]', 2, 'Acid entering the small intestine causes secretin release into blood; secretin stimulates the pancreas to secrete pancreatic juice.', '{"0": "Secretin is not involved in salivation.", "1": "That describes insulin.", "3": "That describes parathormone."}'),
  ('BIO-0197', 'Coordination and control', 'Hormones', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 59 (§16.1.1)', 'Compared with neurotransmitters, hormones are generally', '["faster acting with shorter effects", "slower acting with longer-lasting effects", "released only into synaptic clefts", "not chemical messengers"]', 1, 'Both are chemical messengers, but hormones act more slowly and their effects last longer.', '{"0": "This is the reverse.", "2": "Neurotransmitters, not hormones, act in synaptic clefts.", "3": "Both are chemical messengers."}'),
  ('BIO-0198', 'Coordination and control', 'Hypothalamus', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 60 (§16.2.1)', 'Neurosecretory cells of the hypothalamus', '["conduct nerve impulses and also secrete hormones", "only store hormones made by the pituitary", "make myelin for the CNS", "secrete digestive enzymes"]', 0, 'These special cells both conduct impulses and secrete hormones, linking the nervous and endocrine systems.', '{"1": "They make, not just store, hormones.", "2": "Myelin is made by oligodendrocytes.", "3": "Digestive enzymes come from exocrine glands."}'),
  ('BIO-0199', 'Coordination and control', 'Hypothalamus', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 60 (§16.2.1)', 'Oxytocin and ADH reach the posterior pituitary by', '["travelling in blood vessels from the hypothalamus", "being made by the anterior pituitary", "diffusing through the cerebrospinal fluid", "being transported along the axons of hypothalamic neurosecretory cells"]', 3, 'They are made in the hypothalamus and carried down axons to the posterior pituitary, where they are stored and later released.', '{"0": "Blood vessels link the hypothalamus with the anterior, not posterior, pituitary.", "1": "They are made in the hypothalamus.", "2": "CSF is not the route."}'),
  ('BIO-0200', 'Coordination and control', 'Hypothalamus', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 60 (§16.2.1)', 'The hypothalamus controls the anterior pituitary through', '["axons that run directly into the anterior lobe", "oxytocin and ADH", "releasing and inhibiting hormones carried by connecting blood vessels", "the vagus nerve"]', 2, 'Hypothalamic releasing hormones stimulate, and inhibiting hormones suppress, anterior pituitary secretion; they travel in blood vessels.', '{"0": "Axons run to the posterior pituitary.", "1": "These are posterior pituitary hormones.", "3": "The vagus nerve serves visceral organs."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-0201', 'Coordination and control', 'Hypothalamus', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 61 (§16.2.1)', 'Somatostatin from the hypothalamus', '["stimulates the secretion of growth hormone", "inhibits the secretion of growth hormone", "stimulates the secretion of TSH", "inhibits the secretion of prolactin"]', 1, 'In the table of hypothalamic hormones, somatostatin inhibits growth hormone secretion.', '{"0": "That is growth hormone releasing hormone.", "2": "That is TRH.", "3": "That is prolactin inhibiting factor (dopamine)."}'),
  ('BIO-0202', 'Coordination and control', 'Hypothalamus', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 61 (§16.2.1)', 'According to the book, prolactin inhibiting factor of the hypothalamus is actually', '["dopamine", "oxytocin", "somatostatin", "serotonin"]', 0, 'The table notes that prolactin inhibiting factor is dopamine.', '{"1": "Oxytocin is a posterior pituitary hormone.", "2": "Somatostatin inhibits growth hormone.", "3": "Serotonin is not named as this factor."}'),
  ('BIO-0203', 'Coordination and control', 'Hypothalamus', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 61 (§16.2.1)', 'Gonadotropin releasing hormone (GnRH) from the hypothalamus causes the anterior pituitary to secrete', '["TSH", "FSH and LH", "ACTH", "growth hormone"]', 1, 'GnRH stimulates secretion of the gonadotropins FSH and LH.', '{"0": "TSH secretion is triggered by TRH.", "2": "ACTH secretion is triggered by its own releasing hormone.", "3": "GH secretion is triggered by GHRH."}'),
  ('BIO-0204', 'Coordination and control', 'Pituitary', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 61 (§16.2.2)', 'The pituitary gland is attached to the hypothalamus by a stalk called the', '["infundibulum", "corpus callosum", "hilum", "pons"]', 0, 'The infundibulum connects the pituitary to the hypothalamus.', '{"1": "The corpus callosum joins the cerebral hemispheres.", "2": "The hilum is on the kidney.", "3": "The pons is part of the brainstem."}'),
  ('BIO-0205', 'Coordination and control', 'Pituitary', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 61 (§16.2.2)', 'ACTH (corticotropin) from the anterior pituitary stimulates the', '["adrenal medulla to secrete epinephrine", "thyroid to secrete thyroxine", "adrenal cortex to secrete corticosteroids", "mammary glands to produce milk"]', 2, 'ACTH acts on the adrenal cortex; secretion from the adrenal medulla is controlled by the nervous system.', '{"0": "The adrenal medulla is controlled by nerves, not ACTH.", "1": "That is the role of TSH.", "3": "That is the role of prolactin."}'),
  ('BIO-0206', 'Coordination and control', 'Pituitary', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 62 (§16.2.2)', 'In males, luteinizing hormone (LH) stimulates the', '["development of ovarian follicles", "mammary glands to produce milk", "kidneys to retain water", "testes to produce testosterone"]', 3, 'In females LH stimulates estrogen and progesterone production and triggers ovulation; in males it stimulates testosterone production.', '{"0": "Follicle development is due to FSH in females.", "1": "Milk production is due to prolactin.", "2": "Water retention is due to ADH."}'),
  ('BIO-0207', 'Coordination and control', 'Pituitary', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 62 (§16.2.2)', 'FSH and LH are together called', '["corticosteroids", "gonadotropins", "catecholamines only", "neurosecretions"]', 1, 'Both act on the gonads and are referred to as gonadotropins.', '{"0": "Corticosteroids come from the adrenal cortex.", "2": "This is not the term used for FSH and LH.", "3": "They are made by the anterior pituitary, not by neurosecretory cells."}'),
  ('BIO-0208', 'Coordination and control', 'Pituitary', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 62 (§16.2.2)', 'Which statement about the posterior pituitary is correct?', '["It makes ADH and oxytocin itself", "It is connected to the hypothalamus only by blood vessels", "It does not synthesize hormones; it stores and releases hormones made by the hypothalamus", "It secretes growth hormone"]', 2, 'The posterior pituitary contains axons from the hypothalamus and only stores and releases ADH and oxytocin.', '{"0": "These are made in the hypothalamus.", "1": "It is linked by a tract of axons.", "3": "GH comes from the anterior pituitary."}'),
  ('BIO-0209', 'Coordination and control', 'Pituitary', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 62 (§16.2.2)', 'Oxytocin causes milk ejection by', '["stimulating contraction of smooth muscles of the mammary glands", "stimulating milk production in the mammary glands", "relaxing the uterus", "increasing water retention by the kidneys"]', 0, 'Prolactin makes milk; oxytocin ejects it by contracting smooth muscles of the mammary glands.', '{"1": "Milk production is due to prolactin.", "2": "Oxytocin contracts the uterus.", "3": "Water retention is due to ADH."}'),
  ('BIO-0210', 'Coordination and control', 'Pituitary', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 62 (§16.2.2)', 'The uterus of a breast-feeding mother returns to its normal size faster because', '["prolactin relaxes the uterus", "progesterone rises after delivery", "ADH secretion stops", "oxytocin secretion continues during breast-feeding"]', 3, 'Oxytocin released during nursing keeps causing uterine contractions.', '{"0": "Prolactin acts on mammary glands.", "1": "This is not the reason given.", "2": "ADH is not involved."}'),
  ('BIO-0211', 'Coordination and control', 'Pituitary', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 62 (§16.2.2)', 'Melanocyte stimulating hormone (MSH) is produced by the', '["intermediate pituitary", "posterior pituitary", "adrenal medulla", "pineal gland"]', 0, 'The thin intermediate lobe between the anterior and posterior lobes produces MSH, which stimulates melanin production.', '{"1": "The posterior lobe releases ADH and oxytocin.", "2": "The adrenal medulla releases epinephrine and norepinephrine.", "3": "The pineal gland secretes melatonin."}'),
  ('BIO-0212', 'Coordination and control', 'Pituitary', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 63 (§16.2.2)', 'Over-secretion of growth hormone in an adult causes', '["gigantism, with a great increase in height", "dwarfism with normal IQ", "cretinism", "acromegaly, with thickened bones of the hands, feet, skull and jaw"]', 3, 'In adults the long bones can no longer lengthen, so excess GH makes bones thicken (acromegaly).', '{"0": "Gigantism results from excess GH in a growing child.", "1": "Dwarfism results from GH deficiency.", "2": "Cretinism results from childhood hypothyroidism."}'),
  ('BIO-0213', 'Coordination and control', 'Pituitary', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 63 (§16.2.2)', 'Pituitary dwarfism due to GH deficiency is characterized by', '["short stature with mental retardation", "short stature with body parts in proportion and normal brain development", "enlarged hands and jaw", "excessive production of dilute urine"]', 1, 'GH deficiency slows growth, but proportions, brain development and IQ are unaffected.', '{"0": "Short stature with mental retardation is cretinism.", "2": "That is acromegaly.", "3": "That is diabetes insipidus."}'),
  ('BIO-0214', 'Coordination and control', 'Pituitary', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 63 (§16.2.2)', 'According to the book, under-secretion of oxytocin would', '["cause rupture of the uterine wall", "cause diabetes insipidus", "interfere with the normal process of labour", "cause gigantism"]', 2, 'Too little oxytocin inhibits normal labour; too much may rupture the uterine wall.', '{"0": "That is linked with over-secretion.", "1": "That is due to ADH deficiency.", "3": "That is due to GH excess."}'),
  ('BIO-0215', 'Coordination and control', 'Thyroid', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 63 (§16.2.3)', 'Which statement about thyroid hormones is correct?', '["The thyroid makes much more T4, which is converted into the more active T3", "The thyroid makes mostly T3, which is converted into T4", "T4 lowers the basal metabolic rate", "T3 and T4 slow down heart rate"]', 0, 'T4 (thyroxine) is the main product; T3 is the more active form. They raise heart rate, breathing rate and the BMR.', '{"1": "T4 is produced in larger amounts.", "2": "They set and raise the BMR.", "3": "They increase heart rate."}'),
  ('BIO-0216', 'Coordination and control', 'Thyroid', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 63 (§16.2.3)', 'When blood calcium rises too high, calcitonin', '["releases calcium from bones into blood", "activates vitamin D in the kidneys", "makes the kidneys reabsorb calcium", "stimulates absorption of calcium into bones, lowering blood calcium"]', 3, 'Calcitonin from the thyroid moves calcium into bone, reducing blood calcium.', '{"0": "Releasing calcium from bone is the action of parathormone.", "1": "Vitamin D activation is caused by parathormone.", "2": "Kidney reabsorption of calcium is caused by parathormone."}'),
  ('BIO-0217', 'Coordination and control', 'Thyroid', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 64 (§16.2.3)', 'Hypothyroidism during foetal life and childhood causes', '["acromegaly", "cretinism, with stunted growth and mental retardation", "Graves'' disease", "Cushing disease"]', 1, 'Lack of thyroid hormones during development leads to cretinism.', '{"0": "Acromegaly is due to excess GH in adults.", "2": "Graves'' disease causes hyperthyroidism.", "3": "Cushing disease is due to excess cortisol."}'),
  ('BIO-0218', 'Coordination and control', 'Thyroid', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 64 (§16.2.3)', 'Goitre, a swelling of the thyroid gland, is caused when hypothyroidism results from', '["excess growth hormone", "excess calcium in blood", "iodine deficiency", "lack of insulin"]', 2, 'If hypothyroidism is due to iodine deficiency, the thyroid swells, producing goitre.', '{"0": "Excess GH causes gigantism or acromegaly.", "1": "High calcium is linked to parathormone excess.", "3": "Insulin lack causes diabetes mellitus."}'),
  ('BIO-0219', 'Coordination and control', 'Thyroid', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 64 (§16.2.3)', 'Myxoedema, a rare and life-threatening complication of severe hypothyroidism, involves', '["protrusion of the eyeballs", "excessive loss of weight", "muscles remaining contracted due to low calcium", "build-up of mucopolysaccharides in connective tissue, causing swelling and thickening of skin"]', 3, 'In myxoedema mucopolysaccharides accumulate in connective tissues.', '{"0": "Exophthalmia is linked with Graves'' disease (hyperthyroidism).", "1": "Weight loss is a feature of hyperthyroidism.", "2": "That is tetany from low parathormone."}'),
  ('BIO-0220', 'Coordination and control', 'Thyroid', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 63 (§16.2.3)', 'Graves'' disease is an autoimmune disease that causes', '["hyperthyroidism, often with an enlarged thyroid and protruding eyeballs", "hypothyroidism with cretinism", "diabetes insipidus", "Addison''s disease"]', 0, 'Graves'' disease causes hyperthyroidism: fast heartbeat, heat intolerance, weight loss and sometimes exophthalmia.', '{"1": "Graves'' disease raises thyroid hormone levels.", "2": "Diabetes insipidus is due to lack of ADH.", "3": "Addison''s disease is due to lack of cortisol."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-0221', 'Coordination and control', 'Parathyroid', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 64 (§16.2.4)', 'The parathyroid glands are', '["two glands on top of the kidneys", "four small glands on the posterior surface of the thyroid", "one gland at the base of the brain", "patches of cells in the pancreas"]', 1, 'There are four small parathyroid glands attached to the back of the thyroid.', '{"0": "Those are the adrenal glands.", "2": "That describes the pituitary or pineal.", "3": "Those are the islets of Langerhans."}'),
  ('BIO-0222', 'Coordination and control', 'Parathyroid', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 64 (§16.2.4)', 'When blood calcium falls, parathormone raises it by', '["stimulating absorption of calcium into bones", "increasing calcium loss in urine", "stimulating osteoclasts to dissolve bone matrix calcium and the kidneys to reabsorb calcium", "blocking vitamin D activation"]', 2, 'Parathormone releases calcium from bone, increases calcium reabsorption by the kidneys and activates vitamin D for intestinal absorption.', '{"0": "That is the action of calcitonin.", "1": "It decreases, not increases, calcium loss.", "3": "It activates vitamin D."}'),
  ('BIO-0223', 'Coordination and control', 'Parathyroid', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 64 (§16.2.4)', 'Under-secretion of parathormone leads to hypocalcaemia, which can cause', '["soft and deformed bones", "depression of the nervous system and muscle weakness", "gigantism", "tetany, with muscles remaining contracted"]', 3, 'Low blood calcium increases neuron excitability and can cause tetany.', '{"0": "Soft bones result from over-secretion releasing too much calcium.", "1": "Depressed nerves and weak muscles result from high calcium.", "2": "Gigantism is due to excess GH."}'),
  ('BIO-0224', 'Coordination and control', 'Pancreas', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 64 (§16.2.5)', 'In the islets of Langerhans, insulin is secreted by', '["beta cells, which are more numerous", "alpha cells, which are more numerous", "beta cells, which are fewer than alpha cells", "exocrine cells of the pancreatic duct"]', 0, 'Beta cells, the larger group, secrete insulin; alpha cells secrete glucagon.', '{"1": "Alpha cells secrete glucagon.", "2": "Beta cells are the larger group.", "3": "Exocrine cells secrete pancreatic juice."}'),
  ('BIO-0225', 'Coordination and control', 'Pancreas', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 65 (§16.2.5)', 'Which is an action of insulin?', '["Promoting hydrolysis of liver glycogen", "Promoting glycogenesis in the liver", "Stimulating gluconeogenesis", "Raising blood glucose level"]', 1, 'Insulin increases glucose uptake, glycogen formation, glucose use and fat formation, and inhibits gluconeogenesis.', '{"0": "Glycogen breakdown is promoted by glucagon.", "2": "Insulin inhibits gluconeogenesis.", "3": "Insulin lowers blood glucose."}'),
  ('BIO-0226', 'Coordination and control', 'Pancreas', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 65 (§16.2.5)', 'Glucagon raises blood glucose mainly by', '["increasing glucose uptake by fat cells", "converting excess glucose to fat", "promoting the hydrolysis of stored glycogen in the liver", "promoting glycogenesis"]', 2, 'Glucagon is secreted when blood glucose is low and releases glucose from liver glycogen.', '{"0": "This lowers blood glucose (insulin action).", "1": "This is an insulin action.", "3": "Glycogenesis is promoted by insulin."}'),
  ('BIO-0227', 'Coordination and control', 'Pancreas', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 65 (§16.2.5)', 'In untreated diabetes mellitus, large volumes of urine are produced because', '["ADH secretion is completely absent", "excess glucose in blood inhibits water reabsorption by the kidneys", "the glomerulus stops filtering", "glucagon blocks the collecting ducts"]', 1, 'High blood glucose interferes with water reabsorption, causing large urine output and dehydration.', '{"0": "Lack of ADH causes diabetes insipidus.", "2": "Filtration continues.", "3": "Glucagon has no such action."}'),
  ('BIO-0228', 'Coordination and control', 'Pancreas', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 65 (§16.2.5)', 'Insulin for Type I diabetes must be injected rather than taken by mouth because', '["it is a steroid that dissolves in fat", "it is destroyed by light", "it is a peptide hormone and would be digested in the gut", "it is made only by bacteria"]', 2, 'Being a peptide, insulin would be broken down by digestive enzymes if swallowed.', '{"0": "Insulin is not a steroid.", "1": "This is not the reason given.", "3": "Injected insulin is made by engineered bacteria, but that is not why it is injected."}'),
  ('BIO-0229', 'Coordination and control', 'Pancreas', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 65 (§16.2.5)', 'People with Type II diabetes usually have', '["normal insulin levels but reduced sensitivity of cells to insulin", "no insulin-secreting beta cells", "too much ADH", "over-secretion of glucagon from tumours"]', 0, 'In Type II diabetes, insulin is present but cells respond poorly to it.', '{"1": "Lack of beta cells describes Type I diabetes.", "2": "ADH is not involved.", "3": "This is not how the book describes Type II."}'),
  ('BIO-0230', 'Coordination and control', 'Pancreas', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 65 (§16.2.5)', 'Over-secretion of insulin causes hypoglycaemia, with symptoms such as', '["excessive urination and thirst", "enlarged jaw and hands", "protruding eyeballs", "lethargy, dizziness, nervousness and, in extreme cases, unconsciousness"]', 3, 'Blood glucose falls below normal, depriving cells of fuel.', '{"0": "These are features of high blood glucose.", "1": "These are features of acromegaly.", "2": "This is a feature of Graves'' disease."}'),
  ('BIO-0231', 'Coordination and control', 'Adrenal glands', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 66 (§16.2.6)', 'Secretion of hormones from the adrenal medulla is controlled by', '["the nervous system", "ACTH from the anterior pituitary", "renin from the kidneys", "TSH from the anterior pituitary"]', 0, 'The adrenal medulla responds to nerve signals during stress, while the cortex is controlled by anterior pituitary hormones.', '{"1": "ACTH controls the adrenal cortex.", "2": "This is not given as the control of the medulla.", "3": "TSH controls the thyroid."}'),
  ('BIO-0232', 'Coordination and control', 'Adrenal glands', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 66 (§16.2.6)', 'In the "fight or flight" response caused by epinephrine and norepinephrine,', '["heart rate decreases", "bronchioles constrict", "blood flow to the skin and digestive organs is reduced", "blood glucose falls"]', 2, 'Heart rate, blood pressure and blood glucose rise and bronchioles dilate, while skin and gut receive less blood.', '{"0": "Heart rate increases.", "1": "Bronchioles dilate.", "3": "Blood glucose rises."}'),
  ('BIO-0233', 'Coordination and control', 'Adrenal glands', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 66 (§16.2.6)', 'Glucocorticoids such as cortisol maintain glucose homeostasis by', '["increasing sodium reabsorption by the kidneys", "promoting glycogenesis in muscles", "stimulating milk production", "breaking down muscle protein and stimulating gluconeogenesis in the liver"]', 3, 'Cortisol causes muscle protein breakdown; the amino acids are carried to the liver and converted into glucose.', '{"0": "That is the action of aldosterone (a mineralocorticoid).", "1": "This is not described for cortisol.", "2": "That is prolactin."}'),
  ('BIO-0234', 'Coordination and control', 'Adrenal glands', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 66 (§16.2.6)', 'Cushing disease occurs when', '["the adrenal cortex secretes too little cortisol", "the pituitary produces too much ACTH, so the adrenal cortex secretes excess cortisol", "the thyroid secretes too much thyroxine", "the pancreas secretes too little insulin"]', 1, 'Excess ACTH drives high cortisol, causing protein breakdown, high blood sugar and blood pressure, thin bones and fragile skin.', '{"0": "Too little cortisol causes Addison''s disease.", "2": "That is hyperthyroidism.", "3": "That is diabetes mellitus."}'),
  ('BIO-0235', 'Coordination and control', 'Adrenal glands', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 67 (§16.2.6)', 'Addison''s disease, with weakness of muscles and loss of salts, results from', '["over-secretion of cortisol", "over-secretion of epinephrine", "under-secretion of thyroxine", "under-secretion of cortisol"]', 3, 'Addison''s disease is due to too little cortisol; its symptoms develop slowly.', '{"0": "Excess cortisol causes Cushing disease.", "1": "Excess medullary hormones may cause hypertension and aggression.", "2": "Too little thyroxine causes hypothyroidism."}'),
  ('BIO-0236', 'Coordination and control', 'Gonads', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 67 (§16.2.7)', 'Estrogen is secreted by the Graafian follicle in response to', '["FSH", "LH alone", "ADH", "prolactin"]', 0, 'The developing (Graafian) follicle secretes estrogen under the influence of FSH.', '{"1": "LH stimulates progesterone from the corpus luteum.", "2": "ADH acts on kidneys.", "3": "Prolactin acts on mammary glands."}'),
  ('BIO-0237', 'Coordination and control', 'Gonads', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 67 (§16.2.7)', 'Progesterone is produced by the corpus luteum in response to LH. One of its effects is to', '["trigger ovulation", "inhibit further FSH secretion, preventing more follicles from ripening", "stimulate milk ejection", "break down the uterine lining"]', 1, 'Progesterone thickens and vascularizes the uterine wall, suppresses ovulation and inhibits FSH.', '{"0": "Progesterone suppresses ovulation.", "2": "Milk ejection is due to oxytocin.", "3": "It maintains, not breaks down, the lining."}'),
  ('BIO-0238', 'Coordination and control', 'Gonads', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 67 (§16.2.7)', 'Testosterone is secreted by the', '["Sertoli cells inside the tubules", "adrenal medulla", "interstitial cells between the seminiferous tubules", "corpus luteum"]', 2, 'LH stimulates the interstitial cells to secrete androgens such as testosterone.', '{"0": "Sertoli cells support sperm development, not named as the testosterone source.", "1": "The adrenal medulla secretes epinephrine.", "3": "The corpus luteum secretes progesterone."}'),
  ('BIO-0239', 'Coordination and control', 'Gonads', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 68 (§16.2.7)', 'Under-secretion of progesterone during pregnancy may lead to', '["miscarriage", "gigantism", "goitre", "tetany"]', 0, 'Progesterone maintains the thickened uterine wall; too little during pregnancy may cause miscarriage.', '{"1": "Gigantism is due to excess GH.", "2": "Goitre is due to iodine deficiency.", "3": "Tetany is due to low calcium."}'),
  ('BIO-0240', 'Coordination and control', 'Gonads', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 68 (§16.2.7)', 'Anabolic steroids used illegally by athletes are synthetic versions of', '["insulin", "thyroxine", "cortisol", "testosterone"]', 3, 'They increase muscle mass and strength but can damage the liver, raise heart disease risk and disrupt hormone balance.', '{"0": "Insulin is a peptide.", "1": "Thyroxine is an amino acid derivative.", "2": "Anabolic steroids mimic testosterone, not cortisol."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-0241', 'Coordination and control', 'Other glands', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 68 (§16.2.8)', 'The thymus gland secretes thymosin, which', '["regulates sleep patterns", "stimulates the maturation of T cells", "stimulates the release of bile", "raises blood calcium"]', 1, 'The thymus, beneath the sternum between the lungs, secretes thymosin for T-cell maturation.', '{"0": "Sleep is regulated by melatonin from the pineal gland.", "2": "Bile release is stimulated by cholecystokinin.", "3": "Blood calcium is raised by parathormone."}'),
  ('BIO-0242', 'Coordination and control', 'Other glands', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 69 (§16.2.8)', 'Which hormone from the small intestine wall stimulates the gallbladder to release bile?', '["Gastrin", "Secretin", "Cholecystokinin", "Erythropoietin"]', 2, 'Cholecystokinin causes bile release; secretin stimulates pancreatic juice and gastrin stimulates gastric juice.', '{"0": "Gastrin is from the stomach wall and acts on gastric glands.", "1": "Secretin acts on the pancreas.", "3": "Erythropoietin is from the kidneys."}'),
  ('BIO-0243', 'Coordination and control', 'Other glands', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 69 (§16.2.8)', 'Erythropoietin, produced by the kidneys,', '["stimulates the stomach to release gastric juice", "stimulates bone marrow to produce red blood cells", "lowers blood calcium", "controls sleep"]', 1, 'Erythropoietin acts on bone marrow to increase RBC production.', '{"0": "That is gastrin.", "2": "That is calcitonin.", "3": "That is melatonin."}'),
  ('BIO-0244', 'Coordination and control', 'Other glands', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 69 (§16.2.8)', 'Prostaglandins are hormone-like substances that', '["are made only by the pituitary and act on distant glands", "are steroids that act on the nucleus of gonad cells", "are made by almost all cells and act locally as vasodilators involved in inflammation", "stimulate milk production"]', 2, 'Prostaglandins are produced by almost all body cells, act locally and are involved in inflammation.', '{"0": "They are not limited to the pituitary.", "1": "This is not their described nature.", "3": "Milk production is due to prolactin."}'),
  ('BIO-0245', 'Coordination and control', 'Feedback', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 69 (§16.3)', 'Release of oxytocin during childbirth is an example of', '["positive feedback, in which the response speeds up the controlling system", "negative feedback, in which the product stops the process", "a reflex arc without hormones", "antagonism between two hormones"]', 0, 'Stronger contractions cause more oxytocin, which causes stronger contractions, until the baby is born.', '{"1": "Negative feedback is shown by insulin and glucagon.", "2": "Oxytocin is a hormone in the loop.", "3": "Only one hormone is driving this loop."}'),
  ('BIO-0246', 'Coordination and control', 'Feedback', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 70 (§16.3)', 'In the negative feedback control of insulin, what stops the beta cells releasing insulin?', '["A rise in blood glucose above normal", "Release of glucagon from beta cells", "Release of oxytocin from the pituitary", "The return of blood glucose to its normal level"]', 3, 'High glucose triggers insulin release; when insulin has brought glucose back to normal, this normal level switches the beta cells off.', '{"0": "High glucose is what starts insulin release.", "1": "Glucagon comes from alpha cells.", "2": "Oxytocin is not involved in glucose control."}'),
  ('BIO-0247', 'Reproduction', 'Reproductive systems', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Human Reproductive Systems, p. 73 (§17.1)', 'The human reproductive system differs from other organ systems because it', '["becomes functional only at puberty and differs greatly between males and females", "is fully functional from birth", "is the same in males and females", "is controlled only by the nervous system"]', 0, 'Other systems are nearly identical in both sexes and work from birth; the reproductive system differs between sexes and starts working at puberty.', '{"1": "It does not work until puberty.", "2": "It is quite different in males and females.", "3": "It is controlled largely by hormones."}'),
  ('BIO-0248', 'Reproduction', 'Male system', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Human Reproductive Systems, p. 73 (§17.1.1)', 'The testes lie in the scrotum, outside the abdomen. The scrotum keeps them at about', '["37 °C, the same as core body temperature", "34 °C, slightly below core body temperature, which sperm development needs", "40 °C, above core body temperature", "25 °C, well below body temperature"]', 1, 'Sperm development needs a temperature around 34 °C, lower than the core 37 °C.', '{"0": "Core temperature is too warm for sperm development.", "2": "Higher temperature harms sperm development.", "3": "The book gives about 34 °C."}'),
  ('BIO-0249', 'Reproduction', 'Male system', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Human Reproductive Systems, p. 74 (§17.1.1)', 'In the germinal epithelium of the seminiferous tubules, Sertoli cells', '["make sperms by meiosis", "secrete testosterone", "nourish the developing sperms", "produce fructose-rich fluid"]', 2, 'Germline cells make sperms by meiosis; Sertoli cells nourish them.', '{"0": "Germline cells make the sperms.", "1": "Leydig (interstitial) cells secrete testosterone.", "3": "Seminal vesicles produce fructose-rich fluid."}'),
  ('BIO-0250', 'Reproduction', 'Male system', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Human Reproductive Systems, p. 74 (§17.1.1)', 'From the seminiferous tubules, sperms pass first into the', '["vas deferens, then the rete testis", "epididymis, then the rete testis", "ejaculatory duct, then the epididymis", "rete testis, then the epididymis"]', 3, 'Sperms move from the seminiferous tubules into the rete testis, then the coiled epididymis, then the vas deferens.', '{"0": "The vas deferens comes after the epididymis.", "1": "The rete testis comes before the epididymis.", "2": "The ejaculatory duct comes near the end of the route."}'),
  ('BIO-0251', 'Reproduction', 'Male system', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Human Reproductive Systems, p. 74 (§17.1.1)', 'Sperms in the epididymis become motile after remaining there for at least', '["18 minutes", "18 days", "18 hours", "18 weeks"]', 2, 'Sperms entering the epididymis are non-motile; after at least 18 hours they become motile and move on.', '{"0": "Too short.", "1": "The book gives at least 18 hours.", "3": "Far too long."}'),
  ('BIO-0252', 'Reproduction', 'Male system', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Human Reproductive Systems, p. 74 (§17.1.1)', 'The ejaculatory duct is formed by the union of the', '["two vasa deferentia", "urethra and the prostate gland", "epididymis and rete testis", "vas deferens and the duct of a seminal vesicle"]', 3, 'Each vas deferens joins a duct from a seminal vesicle to form an ejaculatory duct, which passes through the prostate into the urethra.', '{"0": "Each side forms its own ejaculatory duct.", "1": "The ejaculatory ducts open into the urethra inside the prostate.", "2": "These are earlier parts of the route."}'),
  ('BIO-0253', 'Reproduction', 'Male system', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Human Reproductive Systems, p. 74 (§17.1.1)', 'The vas deferens passes from the scrotum into the abdominal cavity through the', '["inguinal canal", "urethra", "cervix", "ureter"]', 0, 'The vas deferens enters the abdomen via the inguinal canal.', '{"1": "The urethra carries urine and semen out of the body.", "2": "The cervix is part of the female uterus.", "3": "The ureter carries urine from kidney to bladder."}'),
  ('BIO-0254', 'Reproduction', 'Accessory glands', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Human Reproductive Systems, p. 75 (§17.1.1)', 'About 60% of the fluid of semen is produced by the', '["seminal vesicles", "prostate gland", "bulbourethral glands", "testes"]', 1, 'The soft, spongy prostate below the bladder produces about 60% of the semen fluid.', '{"0": "Seminal vesicles add fructose-rich fluid but the book gives 60% for the prostate.", "2": "Bulbourethral glands add a small amount of lubricating fluid.", "3": "The testes make sperms, not most of the fluid."}'),
  ('BIO-0255', 'Reproduction', 'Accessory glands', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Human Reproductive Systems, p. 75 (§17.1.1)', 'The bulbourethral glands secrete a clear fluid that', '["provides fructose as energy for sperms", "makes up most of the semen volume", "contains enzymes for penetrating the ovum", "lubricates the urethra and gives a protective neutral environment for sperms"]', 3, 'Bulbourethral fluid lubricates the urethra and keeps conditions neutral for sperm survival.', '{"0": "Fructose comes from the seminal vesicles.", "1": "The prostate makes most of the fluid.", "2": "Penetrating enzymes are in the acrosome."}'),
  ('BIO-0256', 'Reproduction', 'Spermatogenesis', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Human Reproductive Systems, p. 75 (§17.1.1)', 'In spermatogenesis, which cells are the first haploid (1n) cells formed?', '["Primary spermatocytes", "Secondary spermatocytes", "Spermatogonia", "Germline cells"]', 1, 'Primary spermatocytes (2n) undergo meiosis I to form haploid secondary spermatocytes.', '{"0": "Primary spermatocytes are 2n.", "2": "Spermatogonia are 2n.", "3": "Germline cells are 2n."}'),
  ('BIO-0257', 'Reproduction', 'Spermatogenesis', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Human Reproductive Systems, p. 75 (§17.1.1)', 'One spermatogonium (2n) finally produces how many spermatids?', '["Four", "One", "Two", "Eight"]', 0, 'Meiosis I gives two secondary spermatocytes and meiosis II gives four spermatids, each maturing into a sperm.', '{"1": "Oogenesis, not spermatogenesis, gives one functional cell.", "2": "Two cells come only after meiosis I.", "3": "Meiosis gives four cells, not eight."}'),
  ('BIO-0258', 'Reproduction', 'Sperm structure', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Human Reproductive Systems, p. 75 (§17.1.1)', 'The acrosome of a sperm is formed mainly from the', '["mitochondria, and supplies energy for swimming", "centriole, and forms the tail", "Golgi body, and contains enzymes that help in fertilization", "nucleus, and carries genetic material"]', 2, 'The acrosome cap covers about two-thirds of the head and holds enzymes for fertilizing the ovum.', '{"0": "Mitochondria are in the midpiece.", "1": "The centriole gives rise to the axial filament of the tail.", "3": "Genetic material is within the head, not the acrosome."}'),
  ('BIO-0259', 'Reproduction', 'Sperm structure', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Human Reproductive Systems, p. 75 (§17.1.1)', 'The midpiece of a sperm contains', '["the acrosome", "the nucleus", "Golgi enzymes only", "mitochondria and a centriole"]', 3, 'The midpiece holds mitochondria and a centriole; the tail''s microtubules arise from that centriole.', '{"0": "The acrosome caps the head.", "1": "The nucleus is in the head.", "2": "This is not what the midpiece holds."}'),
  ('BIO-0260', 'Reproduction', 'Hormonal control (male)', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Human Reproductive Systems, p. 76 (§17.1.1)', 'In males, LH stimulates', '["seminiferous tubules to start spermatogenesis", "Leydig cells to secrete testosterone", "Sertoli cells to secrete inhibin", "the hypothalamus to release GnRH"]', 1, 'LH acts on Leydig cells in the interstitial tissue; FSH acts on the seminiferous tubules.', '{"0": "That is the action of FSH.", "2": "Inhibin release follows FSH action on Sertoli cells.", "3": "GnRH comes from the hypothalamus and stimulates LH release, not the reverse."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-0261', 'Reproduction', 'Hormonal control (male)', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Human Reproductive Systems, p. 77 (§17.1.1)', 'Inhibin, released by Sertoli cells, acts to', '["inhibit further secretion of FSH", "stimulate more testosterone secretion", "stimulate the release of LH", "make sperms motile"]', 0, 'FSH causes Sertoli cells to release the peptide hormone inhibin, which feeds back to inhibit FSH secretion.', '{"1": "Inhibin does not stimulate testosterone.", "2": "Testosterone, not inhibin, regulates LH, by inhibiting it.", "3": "Motility is gained in the epididymis."}'),
  ('BIO-0262', 'Reproduction', 'Hormonal control (male)', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Human Reproductive Systems, p. 77 (§17.1.1)', 'High levels of testosterone in the blood', '["stimulate more LH release", "stimulate inhibin release", "inhibit the release of LH and GnRH", "have no effect on the hypothalamus"]', 2, 'Testosterone exerts negative feedback on the pituitary (LH) and the hypothalamus (GnRH).', '{"0": "Its feedback is negative.", "1": "Inhibin is released by Sertoli cells in response to FSH.", "3": "It inhibits GnRH from the hypothalamus."}'),
  ('BIO-0263', 'Reproduction', 'Female system', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Human Reproductive Systems, p. 77 (§17.1.2)', 'The functional units of the ovary, which nurture immature eggs, are the', '["ovarian follicles", "seminiferous tubules", "nephrons", "endometrial glands"]', 0, 'Tiny sac-like ovarian follicles are the functional units of the ovary.', '{"1": "Seminiferous tubules are in the testes.", "2": "Nephrons are in the kidneys.", "3": "The endometrium lines the uterus."}'),
  ('BIO-0264', 'Reproduction', 'Female system', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Human Reproductive Systems, p. 77 (§17.1.2)', 'After ovulation, the ruptured follicle changes into a glandular structure called the', '["Graafian follicle", "corpus luteum", "polar body", "placenta"]', 1, 'The ruptured follicle becomes the corpus luteum, which secretes progesterone (and estrogen).', '{"0": "The Graafian follicle is the mature follicle before ovulation.", "2": "A polar body is a small cell from oogenesis.", "3": "The placenta forms from the endometrium in pregnancy."}'),
  ('BIO-0265', 'Reproduction', 'Female system', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Human Reproductive Systems, p. 77 (§17.1.2)', 'The ovum moves along the oviduct toward the uterus mainly by', '["its own flagellum", "contraction of skeletal muscles", "the beating of cilia on the inner lining of the oviduct", "flow of menstrual blood"]', 2, 'Cilia lining the oviduct move the ovum toward the uterus; fertilization and early mitoses also happen there.', '{"0": "The ovum has no flagellum.", "1": "The oviduct does not contain skeletal muscle.", "3": "Menstrual flow moves outward through the vagina."}'),
  ('BIO-0266', 'Reproduction', 'Female system', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Human Reproductive Systems, p. 77 (§17.1.2)', 'The uppermost rounded part of the uterus, above the openings of the oviducts, is the', '["cervix", "body", "vagina", "fundus"]', 3, 'The uterus has a fundus (top), body (main part) and cervix (narrow lower part).', '{"0": "The cervix is the lower narrow part.", "1": "The body is the main portion below the fundus.", "2": "The vagina leads from the cervix to the outside."}'),
  ('BIO-0267', 'Reproduction', 'Female system', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Human Reproductive Systems, p. 77 (§17.1.2)', 'The middle, thick layer of the uterine wall, made of smooth muscle, is the', '["myometrium", "perimetrium", "endometrium", "germinal epithelium"]', 0, 'From outside in: perimetrium (thin), myometrium (smooth muscle), endometrium (spongy lining).', '{"1": "The perimetrium is the thin outer layer.", "2": "The endometrium is the inner spongy lining.", "3": "Germinal epithelium lines the seminiferous tubules."}'),
  ('BIO-0268', 'Reproduction', 'Female system', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Human Reproductive Systems, p. 77 (§17.1.2)', 'Which layer of the endometrium is shed during menstruation, and which regenerates it?', '["The basal layer is shed; the functional layer regenerates it", "The functional layer is shed; the basal layer with stem cells regenerates it", "The myometrium is shed; the perimetrium regenerates it", "Both endometrial layers are shed every month"]', 1, 'The basal layer holds stem cells that rebuild the functional layer after it is shed.', '{"0": "The roles of the two layers are reversed.", "2": "The muscular layers are not shed.", "3": "The basal layer stays to regenerate the lining."}'),
  ('BIO-0269', 'Reproduction', 'Female system', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Human Reproductive Systems, p. 78 (§17.1.2)', 'The vagina is a thin-walled tube about', '["25 cm long", "2–3 cm long", "8–10 cm long", "30–40 cm long"]', 2, 'The vagina, 8–10 cm long, runs from the outside to the cervix and serves as the birth canal and the route for menstrual flow.', '{"0": "25 cm is the length of a ureter.", "1": "2–3 cm is the width of an ovary or testis.", "3": "Far too long."}'),
  ('BIO-0270', 'Reproduction', 'Oogenesis', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Human Reproductive Systems, p. 78 (§17.1.2)', 'During embryonic development, primary oocytes begin meiosis I but stop at', '["metaphase II, until puberty", "anaphase I, until birth", "telophase II, until ovulation", "prophase I, and stay arrested through childhood"]', 3, 'Primary oocytes stay in prophase I through childhood; at puberty one at a time resumes meiosis I.', '{"0": "The secondary oocyte (not the primary) is held at metaphase II.", "1": "The arrest is in prophase I.", "2": "Meiosis II is completed only after a sperm enters."}'),
  ('BIO-0271', 'Reproduction', 'Oogenesis', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Human Reproductive Systems, p. 78 (§17.1.2)', 'When a primary oocyte completes meiosis I, it forms', '["a large secondary oocyte and a small first polar body, both haploid", "two equal secondary oocytes", "four ova", "one ovum and three polar bodies at once"]', 0, 'The division is unequal: the larger cell is the secondary oocyte (1n), the smaller is the first polar body (1n).', '{"1": "The two cells are dissimilar in size.", "2": "Oogenesis does not produce four ova.", "3": "The second polar body and ovum form only after fertilization."}'),
  ('BIO-0272', 'Reproduction', 'Oogenesis', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Human Reproductive Systems, p. 79 (§17.1.2)', 'Meiosis II of the secondary oocyte is completed', '["before ovulation, inside the Graafian follicle", "only if a sperm fuses with it, producing an ovum and a second polar body", "during embryonic life", "at puberty, in all oocytes together"]', 1, 'The secondary oocyte is arrested at metaphase II and is ovulated; meiosis II finishes in the oviduct only after sperm entry.', '{"0": "It is still at metaphase II when ovulated.", "2": "Only meiosis I begins in embryonic life.", "3": "Only one oocyte resumes at a time, and meiosis II needs fertilization."}'),
  ('BIO-0273', 'Reproduction', 'Oogenesis', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Human Reproductive Systems, p. 79 (§17.1.2)', 'At birth, a female''s ovaries contain about how many follicles, each with a primary oocyte?', '["400", "20 thousand", "200 million", "2 million"]', 3, 'The book states that the ovaries contain some 2 million follicles at birth.', '{"0": "Far too few.", "1": "Far too few.", "2": "Far too many."}'),
  ('BIO-0274', 'Reproduction', 'Menstrual cycle', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Human Reproductive Systems, p. 79 (§17.1.3)', 'The menstrual phase (days 1–5) of the cycle occurs because of', '["a sudden surge of LH", "high levels of progesterone", "low levels of estrogen and progesterone", "release of hCG by the embryo"]', 2, 'Low estrogen and progesterone cause the functional endometrium to be shed.', '{"0": "The LH surge causes ovulation around day 14.", "1": "High progesterone maintains the lining.", "3": "hCG prevents menstruation in pregnancy."}'),
  ('BIO-0275', 'Reproduction', 'Menstrual cycle', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Human Reproductive Systems, p. 79 (§17.1.3)', 'During the proliferative phase, the endometrium becomes thick and vascular again mainly due to', '["progesterone from the corpus luteum", "oxytocin from the posterior pituitary", "estrogen secreted by the growing Graafian follicle", "testosterone from the adrenal glands"]', 2, 'FSH stimulates the Graafian follicle to secrete estrogen, which rebuilds and vascularizes the endometrium.', '{"0": "The corpus luteum forms after ovulation, in the secretory phase.", "1": "Oxytocin is not involved in rebuilding the lining.", "3": "Testosterone is not the cause."}'),
  ('BIO-0276', 'Reproduction', 'Menstrual cycle', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Human Reproductive Systems, p. 79 (§17.1.3)', 'Of the several follicles that begin to grow in a cycle,', '["all of them release ova at ovulation", "two always mature and release twins", "none matures unless pregnancy occurs", "usually only one becomes a Graafian follicle and the others degenerate"]', 3, 'A rise in FSH starts several follicles growing, but only one becomes the mature Graafian follicle.', '{"0": "Only one usually ovulates.", "1": "This is not what the book describes.", "2": "A follicle matures in every cycle."}'),
  ('BIO-0277', 'Reproduction', 'Menstrual cycle', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Human Reproductive Systems, p. 79 (§17.1.3)', 'As estrogen rises in the proliferative phase, the FSH level', '["rises, because of positive feedback", "falls, because of negative feedback", "stays constant", "rises sharply to cause ovulation"]', 1, 'Rising estrogen lowers FSH by negative feedback.', '{"0": "The book describes negative feedback on FSH.", "2": "FSH falls.", "3": "Ovulation is triggered by the LH surge."}'),
  ('BIO-0278', 'Reproduction', 'Menstrual cycle', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Human Reproductive Systems, p. 80 (§17.1.3)', 'In the secretory phase (days 15–28), the corpus luteum secretes', '["estrogen and progesterone, making the endometrium more vascular and glandular", "FSH and LH", "only oxytocin", "hCG"]', 0, 'LH turns the ruptured follicle into the corpus luteum, whose estrogen and progesterone prepare the endometrium.', '{"1": "FSH and LH come from the anterior pituitary.", "2": "Oxytocin comes from the posterior pituitary.", "3": "hCG comes from the embryo."}'),
  ('BIO-0279', 'Reproduction', 'Menstrual cycle', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Human Reproductive Systems, p. 80 (§17.1.3)', 'If fertilization does not occur, menstruation begins around day 28 because', '["the corpus luteum degenerates and estrogen and progesterone fall sharply", "the corpus luteum grows larger and secretes more progesterone", "hCG levels rise", "FSH stops completely for the rest of life"]', 0, 'Without pregnancy the corpus luteum breaks down; the abrupt fall in its hormones causes the endometrium to be shed.', '{"1": "The corpus luteum degenerates.", "2": "hCG is made only by an embryo.", "3": "FSH rises again in the next cycle."}'),
  ('BIO-0280', 'Reproduction', 'Menstrual cycle', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Human Reproductive Systems, p. 80 (§17.1.3)', 'Pregnancy tests detect human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) because it', '["is produced by the mother''s pituitary every month", "is the same hormone as FSH", "is produced by the embryo''s chorion, not by the mother", "is released at every ovulation"]', 2, 'hCG from the embryonic chorion maintains the corpus luteum so menstruation is prevented; only a pregnancy produces it.', '{"0": "It is not made by the mother.", "1": "It is a distinct hormone from the embryo.", "3": "It appears only after fertilization."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-0281', 'Reproduction', 'Menstrual cycle', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Human Reproductive Systems, p. 80 (§17.1.3)', 'The stopping of the menstrual cycle at around 50 years of age is called', '["puberty", "menopause", "ovulation", "menstruation"]', 1, 'Menstrual cycles stop at about 50 years, which is termed menopause.', '{"0": "Puberty is when the cycle begins.", "2": "Ovulation is release of the egg.", "3": "Menstruation is monthly shedding of the lining."}'),
  ('BIO-0282', 'Inheritance', 'Law of segregation', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 84 (§18.1)', 'A true-breeding (pure-breeding) plant for a trait is one that', '["produces all offspring with the same trait on self-fertilization", "always shows the recessive trait", "produces offspring in a 3:1 ratio on selfing", "cannot be self-fertilized"]', 0, 'Mendel developed true-breeding pea varieties, which give only the same trait when self-fertilized.', '{"1": "A true-breeding plant may show the dominant trait (e.g. RR).", "2": "A 3:1 ratio comes from selfing a heterozygote.", "3": "Pea plants are normally self-fertilized."}'),
  ('BIO-0283', 'Inheritance', 'Law of segregation', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 84 (§18.1)', 'Mendel realized that the 3:1 ratio in the F2 of a monohybrid cross was really a disguised', '["9:3:3:1 ratio", "1:2:1 ratio of pure dominant : non-pure dominant : pure recessive", "1:1 ratio of dominant : recessive", "2:1 ratio of dominant : recessive"]', 1, 'In the F2, 1/4 were pure-breeding dominant, 2/4 non-pure-breeding dominant and 1/4 pure-breeding recessive.', '{"0": "9:3:3:1 is the dihybrid F2 ratio.", "2": "1:1 is the testcross ratio of a heterozygote.", "3": "Mendel found 3:1, which he broke down as 1:2:1."}'),
  ('BIO-0284', 'Inheritance', 'Law of segregation', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 86 (§18.1)', 'The particular location of a gene on a chromosome is called its', '["allele", "genotype", "locus", "karyotype"]', 2, 'A gene''s locus is its specific position on a chromosome.', '{"0": "An allele is an alternative form of a gene.", "1": "A genotype is the combination of alleles in an individual.", "3": "A karyotype is the whole set of chromosomes."}'),
  ('BIO-0285', 'Inheritance', 'Law of segregation', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 86 (§18.1)', 'An individual whose gene pair has two different alleles is', '["homozygous", "hemizygous", "true-breeding", "heterozygous"]', 3, 'When gametes with different alleles fuse, the offspring is heterozygous for that gene.', '{"0": "Homozygous means two identical alleles.", "1": "Hemizygous refers to genes present in only one copy, as on the Y.", "2": "True-breeding individuals are homozygous."}'),
  ('BIO-0286', 'Inheritance', 'Law of segregation', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 86 (§18.1)', 'The law of segregation states that', '["alleles of different genes always stay together in gametes", "each gamete receives only one allele of each gene, chosen at random", "each gamete receives both alleles of a gene", "dominant alleles are always passed on"]', 1, 'The two alleles of a gene separate during gamete formation, so each gamete carries one, selected randomly.', '{"0": "That would describe complete linkage.", "2": "Gametes are haploid and carry one allele per gene.", "3": "Either allele has an equal chance of passing on."}'),
  ('BIO-0287', 'Inheritance', 'Testcross', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 86 (§18.1)', 'In a testcross, a round-seeded pea plant is crossed with a wrinkled-seeded plant. All offspring are round. The round parent was', '["Rr", "rr", "either RR or Rr equally likely", "RR"]', 3, 'If the round parent were Rr, half the offspring would be wrinkled (1:1). All round offspring show it is RR.', '{"0": "Rr × rr would give round and wrinkled in a 1:1 ratio.", "1": "rr plants have wrinkled seeds.", "2": "The result clearly identifies RR."}'),
  ('BIO-0288', 'Inheritance', 'Independent assortment', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 86 (§18.2)', 'In Mendel''s dihybrid cross RRYY × rryy, all F1 seeds were', '["wrinkled and green, with genotype rryy", "round and green, with genotype RRyy", "round and yellow, with genotype RrYy", "in a 9:3:3:1 ratio"]', 2, 'The F1 showed both dominant traits and were heterozygous for both genes (dihybrids).', '{"0": "Wrinkled and green are both recessive.", "1": "Yellow is dominant, so F1 seeds are yellow.", "3": "9:3:3:1 appears in the F2."}'),
  ('BIO-0289', 'Inheritance', 'Independent assortment', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 87 (§18.2)', 'How many kinds of gametes can a dihybrid RrYy plant produce?', '["Four: RY, Ry, rY and ry", "Two: R and r", "Two: RY and ry", "Sixteen"]', 0, 'Independent assortment gives four combinations of alleles in the gametes.', '{"1": "Gametes carry one allele of each gene.", "2": "Ry and rY are also formed.", "3": "Sixteen is the number of combinations when 4 × 4 gametes fuse."}'),
  ('BIO-0290', 'Inheritance', 'Independent assortment', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 88 (§18.2)', 'In the F2 of Mendel''s dihybrid cross, the fraction of wrinkled yellow seeds was', '["9/16", "3/16", "1/16", "1/4"]', 1, 'The F2 ratio is 9 round yellow : 3 round green : 3 wrinkled yellow : 1 wrinkled green.', '{"0": "9/16 are round yellow.", "2": "1/16 are wrinkled green.", "3": "1/4 (4/16) is not the fraction of any single class."}'),
  ('BIO-0291', 'Inheritance', 'Independent assortment', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 87 (§18.2)', 'In the F2 of a dihybrid cross showing 9:3:3:1, what is the ratio of round to wrinkled seeds alone?', '["9:7", "1:1", "9:3", "3:1"]', 3, 'Independent assortment does not affect segregation of each pair: (9+3) round : (3+1) wrinkled = 12:4 = 3:1.', '{"0": "9:7 is not obtained from these classes.", "1": "Round is dominant, so round seeds outnumber wrinkled.", "2": "9:3 compares only two of the four classes."}'),
  ('BIO-0292', 'Inheritance', 'Independent assortment', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 88 (§18.2)', 'Independent assortment happens at the chromosome level because', '["homologous chromosome pairs line up randomly during meiosis", "all maternal chromosomes always go to the same gamete", "chromosomes are copied twice in meiosis", "genes on one chromosome always separate"]', 0, 'Random alignment of homologous pairs gives different mixes of maternal and paternal alleles in gametes.', '{"1": "Maternal and paternal chromosomes are mixed randomly.", "2": "This is not the cause of independent assortment.", "3": "Genes on the same chromosome tend to stay together (linkage)."}'),
  ('BIO-0293', 'Inheritance', 'Independent assortment', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 88 (§18.2)', 'In humans, random orientation of chromosome pairs can produce about how many different allele combinations in a gamete?', '["46 (2 × 23)", "23²", "Over 8 million (2²³)", "About 20,000"]', 2, 'With 23 pairs, each lining up randomly, the number of combinations is 2²³, about 8.4 million.', '{"0": "This is just the chromosome number.", "1": "23² = 529, far too low.", "3": "About 20,000 is the number of human genes."}'),
  ('BIO-0294', 'Inheritance', 'Independent assortment', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 88 (§18.2.1)', 'Which of the following is a limitation of the law of independent assortment?', '["Genes on different chromosomes assort independently", "Genes close together on the same chromosome tend to be inherited together", "Alleles of one gene segregate into different gametes", "Fertilization combines gametes at random"]', 1, 'Gene linkage, crossing over, epistasis, pleiotropy and environmental effects can make results differ from Mendel''s predictions.', '{"0": "This is what the law predicts.", "2": "This is the law of segregation.", "3": "Random fertilization is assumed by Mendel''s laws."}'),
  ('BIO-0295', 'Inheritance', 'Independent assortment', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 89 (§18.2.1)', 'The phenomenon in which one gene controls multiple characters is called', '["epistasis", "polygenic inheritance", "pleiotropy", "codominance"]', 2, 'In pleiotropy, a single gene affects several characters, which can distort expected ratios.', '{"0": "In epistasis one gene masks or modifies another gene.", "1": "In polygenic inheritance many genes control one trait.", "3": "In codominance both alleles are fully expressed."}'),
  ('BIO-0296', 'Inheritance', 'Probability', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 89 (§18.2.2)', 'The product rule of probability is used to find the chance that', '["two or more independent events happen together", "either of two mutually exclusive events happens", "an event does not happen at all", "a gene is dominant"]', 0, 'For independent events the probabilities are multiplied, e.g. two sixes on two dice = 1/6 × 1/6 = 1/36.', '{"1": "That uses the sum rule.", "2": "This is not the product rule.", "3": "Probability rules do not decide dominance."}'),
  ('BIO-0297', 'Inheritance', 'Probability', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 89 (§18.2.2)', 'Using the sum rule, the probability of rolling either a 2 or a 5 with one six-sided dice is', '["1/36", "1/6", "2/36", "1/3"]', 3, 'The events are mutually exclusive, so 1/6 + 1/6 = 1/3.', '{"0": "1/36 is the product of the two probabilities.", "1": "1/6 is the chance of one face only.", "2": "2/36 is not the correct sum."}'),
  ('BIO-0298', 'Inheritance', 'Probability', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 89 (§18.2.2)', 'Two parents are both heterozygous (Aa) for a recessive disorder and heterozygous (Bb) for a second, independently assorting recessive disorder. The chance of a child with both disorders (aabb) is', '["1/4", "1/16", "1/8", "3/16"]', 1, 'P(aa) = 1/4 and P(bb) = 1/4; by the product rule, 1/4 × 1/4 = 1/16.', '{"0": "1/4 is the chance of only one disorder.", "2": "1/8 is not obtained by the product rule here.", "3": "3/16 is the chance of a single dominant-recessive combination such as A_bb."}'),
  ('BIO-0299', 'Inheritance', 'Probability', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 89 (§18.2.2)', 'In a cross AaBbCc × AaBbCc, the probability of an offspring that is aabbcc is', '["1/64", "1/16", "1/8", "3/64"]', 0, 'Each recessive homozygote has probability 1/4, so (1/4)³ = 1/64.', '{"1": "(1/4)² = 1/16 would be for two genes.", "2": "1/8 = (1/2)³, the chance of a gamete abc, not of the offspring.", "3": "3/64 is not the product of the three probabilities."}'),
  ('BIO-0300', 'Inheritance', 'Dominance', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 90 (§18.3.1)', 'When a red-flowered four o''clock plant (RR) is crossed with a white-flowered one (WW), all offspring are pink. This shows', '["complete dominance", "codominance", "incomplete dominance", "epistasis"]', 2, 'Neither allele is fully dominant, so the heterozygote is intermediate between the parents.', '{"0": "With complete dominance the F1 would be red or white.", "1": "In codominance both parental phenotypes appear together, not a blend.", "3": "Epistasis involves alleles of different genes."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-0301', 'Inheritance', 'Dominance', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 91 (§18.3.1)', 'Crossing two pink four o''clock plants (RW × RW) gives red : pink : white in the ratio', '["3:1", "1:1", "9:3:4", "1:2:1"]', 3, 'With incomplete dominance the genotypic and phenotypic ratios are the same: 1 RR : 2 RW : 1 WW.', '{"0": "3:1 occurs with complete dominance.", "1": "1:1 comes from a cross such as RW × RR.", "2": "9:3:4 is a recessive epistasis ratio."}'),
  ('BIO-0302', 'Inheritance', 'Dominance', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 91 (§18.3.2)', 'In the MN blood group system, a person with genotype LᴹLᴺ has', '["both M and N antigens on the red cells", "an intermediate antigen that is neither M nor N", "only the M antigen", "no antigen"]', 0, 'Lᴹ and Lᴺ are codominant, so both antigens appear in the heterozygote.', '{"1": "There is no blending in codominance.", "2": "Lᴺ is also expressed.", "3": "Both alleles produce antigens."}'),
  ('BIO-0303', 'Inheritance', 'Dominance', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 91 (§18.3.2)', 'The key difference between incomplete dominance and codominance is that in codominance the heterozygote', '["shows a blend intermediate between the parents", "shows only the dominant phenotype", "always dies", "shows both parental phenotypes at once"]', 3, 'Codominant alleles are both fully expressed (e.g. MN, AB blood groups); incomplete dominance gives an intermediate blend.', '{"0": "A blend is incomplete dominance.", "1": "That is complete dominance.", "2": "This is not a feature of codominance."}'),
  ('BIO-0304', 'Inheritance', 'Multiple alleles', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 92 (§18.3.3)', 'A person with genotype Iᴬi has blood group', '["O", "AB", "A", "B"]', 2, 'Iᴬ is dominant to i, so Iᴬi produces antigen A and the blood group is A.', '{"0": "Group O needs ii.", "1": "Group AB needs IᴬIᴮ.", "3": "Group B needs Iᴮ."}'),
  ('BIO-0305', 'Inheritance', 'Blood groups', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 92 (§18.4)', 'According to the book, how many blood group systems in humans are recognized by the International Society of Blood Transfusion?', '["4", "43", "2", "23"]', 1, 'The ISBT recognizes 43 blood group systems; ABO and Rh are the most important for transfusion.', '{"0": "4 is the number of ABO blood groups.", "2": "Two systems (ABO and Rh) are the most important, but there are many more.", "3": "23 is the human haploid chromosome number."}'),
  ('BIO-0306', 'Inheritance', 'Blood groups', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 92 (§18.4)', 'Which pair of blood group systems must be matched between donor and recipient for a blood transfusion?', '["MN and Kell", "Lewis and Duffy", "Lutheran and MNS", "ABO and Rh"]', 3, 'The book states that ABO and Rh matching is necessary; other systems usually do not cause matching problems.', '{"0": "These usually do not cause transfusion problems.", "1": "These usually do not cause transfusion problems.", "2": "These usually do not cause transfusion problems."}'),
  ('BIO-0307', 'Inheritance', 'ABO system', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 92 (§18.4.1)', 'The ABO blood group system was discovered by Landsteiner in', '["1865", "1901", "1927", "1952"]', 1, 'Landsteiner discovered antigens A and B on RBCs in 1901.', '{"0": "1865 is the period of Mendel''s work.", "2": "1927 is when the MN system was discovered.", "3": "1952 is when the Bombay phenotype was discovered."}'),
  ('BIO-0308', 'Inheritance', 'ABO system', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 93 (§18.4.1)', 'The plasma of a person with blood group B contains', '["anti-B antibodies", "both anti-A and anti-B antibodies", "anti-A antibodies", "no anti-A or anti-B antibodies"]', 2, 'By Landsteiner''s rule, plasma has antibodies against the antigen the person lacks; group B lacks A.', '{"0": "Anti-B would attack the person''s own cells.", "1": "Both are found in group O.", "3": "Neither is found in group AB."}'),
  ('BIO-0309', 'Inheritance', 'ABO system', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 93 (§18.4.1)', 'The gene I that controls the ABO blood groups is located on', '["chromosome 9", "chromosome 19", "the X chromosome", "chromosome 7"]', 0, 'Gene I (iso-haemagglutinogen) is on chromosome 9; the secretor gene Se is on chromosome 19.', '{"1": "Chromosome 19 carries the secretor gene.", "2": "The ABO gene is autosomal.", "3": "Chromosome 7 carries the blue opsin gene."}'),
  ('BIO-0310', 'Inheritance', 'ABO system', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 93 (§18.4.1)', 'Unlike anti-Rh antibodies, anti-A and anti-B antibodies of the ABO system', '["form from embryonic life without any exposure to the antigens", "form only after a mismatched transfusion", "are found only in pregnant women", "are produced only by group AB"]', 0, 'ABO antibodies are naturally present; anti-Rh antibodies form only after an Rh-negative person is exposed to Rh antigen.', '{"1": "That is true of anti-Rh antibodies.", "2": "They are present in everyone lacking the antigen.", "3": "Group AB has neither antibody."}'),
  ('BIO-0311', 'Inheritance', 'ABO system', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 93 (§18.4.1)', 'A woman with blood group B has a child with blood group O. The genotypes of mother and child must be', '["mother IᴮIᴮ, child ii", "mother Iᴮi, child Iᴮi", "mother IᴮIᴮ, child Iᴮi", "mother Iᴮi, child ii"]', 3, 'A group O child is ii and must get an i from each parent, so the group B mother must be Iᴮi.', '{"0": "An IᴮIᴮ mother cannot pass on i.", "1": "A group O child must be ii.", "2": "Neither genotype fits a group O child."}'),
  ('BIO-0312', 'Inheritance', 'ABO system', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 94 (§18.4.1)', 'Agglutination in an incompatible transfusion happens because', '["the donor''s antibodies attack the recipient''s plasma", "the donor''s RBCs lack all antigens", "the recipient''s antibodies attack antigens on the donor''s RBCs", "the recipient''s RBCs are destroyed by the donor''s platelets"]', 2, 'Recipient antibodies bind donor RBC antigens, causing clumping (agglutination).', '{"0": "The book focuses on recipient antibodies against donor cells.", "1": "Cells without antigens (group O) are not attacked.", "3": "Platelets do not cause agglutination here."}'),
  ('BIO-0313', 'Inheritance', 'ABO system', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 94 (§18.4.1)', 'The book explains that allele i is frequent in the population partly because', '["i is dominant to Iᴬ and Iᴮ", "crosses such as Iᴬi × Iᴮi still give about 25% ii offspring", "ii individuals have more children", "i is located on the X chromosome"]', 1, 'Heterozygous carriers of i in all groups keep producing group O (ii) children, keeping i common.', '{"0": "i is recessive to both.", "2": "The book gives no such reason.", "3": "The ABO gene is on chromosome 9."}'),
  ('BIO-0314', 'Inheritance', 'Rh system', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 94 (§18.4.2)', 'The Rh blood group system gets its name from', '["the scientist Rhodes", "the Rhesus monkey, in which its antigen was first found", "the red colour of haemoglobin", "the Rhine river region"]', 1, 'Rh comes from the Rhesus monkey, in which Landsteiner found the antigen.', '{"0": "Not the source of the name.", "2": "Not the source of the name.", "3": "Not the source of the name."}'),
  ('BIO-0315', 'Inheritance', 'Rh system', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 95 (§18.4.2)', 'A person with Rh genotype Dd is', '["Rh-negative", "a universal donor", "without any Rh antigen", "Rh-positive"]', 3, 'The dominant D allele produces the Rh antigen, so DD and Dd are Rh-positive; only dd is Rh-negative.', '{"0": "Only dd is Rh-negative.", "1": "Universal donors are O-negative.", "2": "D produces the antigen."}'),
  ('BIO-0316', 'Inheritance', 'Rh system', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 95 (§18.4.2)', 'The true universal donor and universal recipient, considering both ABO and Rh, are respectively', '["O-negative and AB-positive", "AB-positive and O-negative", "O-positive and AB-negative", "A-negative and B-positive"]', 0, 'O-negative RBCs carry no A, B or Rh antigen; AB-positive plasma has no anti-A, anti-B or anti-Rh antibodies.', '{"1": "The two are reversed.", "2": "O-positive cells carry the Rh antigen; AB-negative can make anti-Rh antibodies.", "3": "These do not fit either definition."}'),
  ('BIO-0317', 'Inheritance', 'Rh system', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 95 (§18.4.2)', 'If an Rh-negative person receives Rh-positive blood,', '["nothing happens on any later transfusion", "the person becomes Rh-positive", "the person starts producing anti-Rh antibodies, which stay for life", "anti-A antibodies are produced"]', 2, 'Rh-negative people do not naturally have anti-Rh antibodies, but make them after exposure; these destroy Rh-positive cells later.', '{"0": "Later Rh-positive transfusions can be attacked.", "1": "The person''s genotype stays dd.", "3": "Anti-A depends on the ABO type."}'),
  ('BIO-0318', 'Inheritance', 'Erythroblastosis foetalis', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 95 (§18.4.3)', 'Erythroblastosis foetalis can occur when', '["an Rh-positive mother carries an Rh-negative foetus", "both mother and foetus are Rh-negative", "the mother is group AB and the foetus group O", "an Rh-negative mother carries an Rh-positive foetus"]', 3, 'The mother makes anti-Rh antibodies that cross the placenta and destroy the foetus''s RBCs.', '{"0": "An Rh-positive mother does not make anti-Rh antibodies.", "1": "There is no Rh incompatibility.", "2": "This is not the Rh situation described."}'),
  ('BIO-0319', 'Inheritance', 'Erythroblastosis foetalis', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 96 (§18.4.3)', 'Jaundice in a baby with erythroblastosis foetalis is caused by build-up of', '["excess oxygen in the blood", "fibrin clots in the liver", "bilirubin from the breakdown of red blood cells", "uric acid in the joints"]', 2, 'Destroyed foetal RBCs release bilirubin, which builds up and yellows the skin and eyes; high levels can damage brain cells.', '{"0": "Jaundice is not caused by oxygen.", "1": "Fibrin forms clots, not jaundice.", "3": "Uric acid in joints causes gout."}'),
  ('BIO-0320', 'Inheritance', 'Erythroblastosis foetalis', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 96 (§18.4.3)', 'To prevent Rh sensitization, a mother at risk is given Rh-antiserum during early pregnancy and just after birth. This works because it', '["changes the foetus into Rh-negative", "destroys any foetal RBCs in her blood before her immune system is sensitized", "adds Rh antigens to the mother''s RBCs", "stimulates her to make more anti-Rh antibodies"]', 1, 'The anti-Rh antibodies in the antiserum remove foetal Rh-positive cells before the mother forms her own lasting antibodies.', '{"0": "The foetus''s genotype cannot be changed.", "2": "It does not add antigens.", "3": "The aim is to stop her making antibodies."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-0321', 'Inheritance', 'Erythroblastosis foetalis', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 96 (§18.4.3)', 'ABO incompatibility can protect a baby from Rh disease when, for example, an O-negative mother carries an A-positive baby, because', '["her existing anti-A antibodies destroy foetal RBCs before she can form anti-Rh antibodies", "group O mothers cannot make any antibodies", "A-positive babies lack the Rh antigen", "anti-A antibodies cross the placenta to protect the baby"]', 0, 'Foetal cells entering her blood are quickly removed by anti-A antibodies, so Rh sensitization does not occur.', '{"1": "Group O people already have anti-A and anti-B.", "2": "A-positive means the Rh antigen is present.", "3": "The protection is in the mother''s blood, not the baby''s."}'),
  ('BIO-0322', 'Inheritance', 'Polygenic inheritance', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 96 (§18.5.1)', 'Polygenic traits such as human height and skin colour show', '["continuous variation, because many genes add their effects", "discontinuous variation with two distinct classes", "a strict 3:1 ratio", "expression in only one sex"]', 0, 'Many gene pairs contribute additively, giving a smooth range of phenotypes.', '{"1": "Distinct categories are typical of single genes or epistasis.", "2": "A 3:1 ratio is a single-gene result.", "3": "That describes sex-limited traits."}'),
  ('BIO-0323', 'Inheritance', 'Polygenic inheritance', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 97 (§18.5.1)', 'Using the book''s three-gene model of skin colour, children of two AaBbCc parents show phenotypes in the ratio', '["9:3:3:1", "1:2:1", "1:6:15:20:15:6:1", "27:9:9:9:3:3:3:1"]', 2, 'Seven classes appear, from 0 to 6 dominant alleles; the middle class (3 dominant alleles) is the most common.', '{"0": "9:3:3:1 is a dihybrid with complete dominance.", "1": "1:2:1 is a single gene with incomplete dominance.", "3": "That is a trihybrid ratio with complete dominance, not additive genes."}'),
  ('BIO-0324', 'Inheritance', 'Polygenic inheritance', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 97 (§18.5.1)', 'According to the book, human skin colour is controlled by about', '["3 genes only", "60 genes", "1 gene with 3 alleles", "23 genes"]', 1, 'Skin colour depends on melanin and is controlled by about 60 genes; three genes are used only to explain it simply.', '{"0": "Three genes are a teaching model only.", "2": "That describes the ABO system.", "3": "23 is the number of chromosome pairs."}'),
  ('BIO-0325', 'Inheritance', 'Polygenic inheritance', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 98 (§18.5.1)', 'In the F2 of dark red (AABBCC) × white (aabbcc) wheat, out of 64 plants, how many are expected to have white grains?', '["62", "16", "4", "1"]', 3, 'Only aabbcc is white (1/64) and only AABBCC is darkest red (1/64); the other 62 show shades of red.', '{"0": "62 show intermediate shades of red.", "1": "16 would be 1/4.", "2": "4 is not the expected number."}'),
  ('BIO-0326', 'Inheritance', 'Epistasis', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 98 (§18.5.2)', 'Incomplete dominance, codominance and multiple alleles are examples of', '["non-allelic interactions", "epistasis", "allelic interactions", "polygenic inheritance"]', 2, 'In allelic interactions, alleles of the same gene interact.', '{"0": "Non-allelic interactions involve different genes.", "1": "Epistasis is a non-allelic interaction.", "3": "Polygenic inheritance involves many genes."}'),
  ('BIO-0327', 'Inheritance', 'Epistasis', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 98 (§18.5.2)', 'In epistasis, the allele whose effect is masked by an allele of another gene is called', '["epistatic", "codominant", "pleiotropic", "hypostatic"]', 3, 'The masking allele is epistatic and the masked one is hypostatic.', '{"0": "The epistatic allele does the masking.", "1": "Codominant alleles are both expressed.", "2": "Pleiotropy is one gene affecting many traits."}'),
  ('BIO-0328', 'Inheritance', 'Epistasis', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 98 (§18.5.2)', 'Compared with polygenic inheritance, epistasis usually produces', '["continuous variation", "discontinuous variation in distinct categories", "only intermediate phenotypes", "the same 9:3:3:1 ratio"]', 1, 'Polygenic genes add small effects (continuous variation); in epistasis one gene masks another, giving distinct classes.', '{"0": "Continuous variation is typical of polygenic traits.", "2": "Intermediates are typical of incomplete dominance.", "3": "Epistasis modifies the 9:3:3:1 ratio."}'),
  ('BIO-0329', 'Inheritance', 'Epistasis', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 99 (§18.5.2)', 'In Labrador retrievers, a dog with genotype BBee has', '["a yellow coat", "a black coat", "a brown coat", "a white coat"]', 0, 'ee blocks pigment deposition in the fur whatever the B gene alleles, giving a yellow coat.', '{"1": "Black needs B_ with at least one E.", "2": "Brown needs bb with at least one E.", "3": "The three Labrador colours are black, brown and yellow."}'),
  ('BIO-0330', 'Inheritance', 'Epistasis', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 99 (§18.5.2)', 'Crossing two BbEe black Labradors gives black, brown and yellow puppies in the ratio', '["9:3:3:1", "12:3:1", "9:3:4", "9:7"]', 2, 'B_E_ black (9), bbE_ brown (3), and all __ee yellow (3 + 1 = 4).', '{"0": "ee masks the B/b difference, so two classes merge.", "1": "12:3:1 is the foxglove ratio.", "3": "9:7 is not the ratio given."}'),
  ('BIO-0331', 'Inheritance', 'Epistasis', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 99 (§18.5.2)', 'A person with Bombay phenotype has IᴬIᴮ alleles but blood group O phenotype because', '["Iᴬ and Iᴮ are recessive to i", "the person has no chromosome 9", "anti-A and anti-B destroy the antigens as soon as they form", "the genotype hh makes no H substance, the precursor of A and B antigens"]', 3, 'Iᴬ and Iᴮ enzymes modify H substance into A and B antigens; without H substance (hh), no antigens are made.', '{"0": "Iᴬ and Iᴮ are dominant to i.", "1": "This is not the cause.", "2": "The antigens are never formed in the first place."}'),
  ('BIO-0332', 'Inheritance', 'Epistasis', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 100 (§18.5.2)', 'In foxglove, selfing a DdWw plant gives white, dark red and light red flowers in the ratio', '["9:3:4", "12:3:1", "9:3:3:1", "15:1"]', 1, 'Any plant with W (12/16) looks white because W limits pigment to small spots; ddww is light red (1/16) and D_ww dark red (3/16).', '{"0": "9:3:4 is the Labrador ratio.", "2": "W masks the D/d difference, so classes merge.", "3": "15:1 is not the ratio given."}'),
  ('BIO-0333', 'Inheritance', 'Linkage', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 100 (§18.6)', 'The chromosomal theory of inheritance, stating that genes lie at specific places on chromosomes, was proposed in 1902 by', '["Walter Sutton", "Karl Correns", "Thomas Hunt Morgan", "Gregor Mendel"]', 0, 'Correns rediscovered Mendel''s work in 1900; Sutton proposed the chromosomal theory in 1902.', '{"1": "Correns rediscovered Mendel''s work.", "2": "Morgan later worked on linkage and sex-linkage in Drosophila.", "3": "Mendel described \"factors\", not chromosomes."}'),
  ('BIO-0334', 'Inheritance', 'Linkage', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 101 (§18.6.1)', 'A test cross gives mostly parental combinations with a few new combinations. This indicates', '["complete linkage", "independent assortment", "incomplete linkage, with some crossing over", "codominance"]', 2, 'Equal 1:1:1:1 classes show independent assortment; only parental types show complete linkage; mostly parental with few recombinants shows incomplete linkage.', '{"0": "Complete linkage gives no new combinations.", "1": "Independent assortment gives equal proportions.", "3": "Codominance is about expression of alleles of one gene."}'),
  ('BIO-0335', 'Inheritance', 'Linkage', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 101 (§18.6.1)', 'Gene linkage can be reliably observed only when', '["only a few offspring are studied", "the genes are on different chromosomes", "the organism reproduces asexually", "the cross produces a large number of offspring"]', 3, 'The larger the progeny, the more likely the results represent the true frequency of linkage.', '{"0": "Small samples can mislead.", "1": "Genes on different chromosomes are not linked.", "2": "Linkage is studied through sexual crosses."}'),
  ('BIO-0336', 'Inheritance', 'Linkage', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 102 (§18.6.1)', 'In Morgan''s cross of long-winged broad-abdomen with vestigial-winged narrow-abdomen Drosophila, the F2 showed only the two parental combinations in a 3:1 ratio. He concluded that', '["the two genes are on the same chromosome and did not assort independently", "the two genes are on different chromosomes", "crossing over happened between the genes in every fly", "both traits are sex-linked"]', 0, 'Without linkage a 9:3:3:1 ratio was expected; only parental types meant the genes were linked.', '{"1": "Genes on different chromosomes would give new combinations.", "2": "Crossing over would give new combinations.", "3": "Both sexes showed the traits; no sex difference is described."}'),
  ('BIO-0337', 'Inheritance', 'Crossing over', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 103 (§18.6.2)', 'Crossing over occurs during', '["mitosis, between sister chromatids", "meiosis I, between chromatids of homologous chromosomes", "fertilization, between sperm and egg", "meiosis II, between non-homologous chromosomes"]', 1, 'Homologous chromosomes pair in meiosis I and swap DNA segments, recombining linked genes.', '{"0": "Crossing over is described in meiosis I.", "2": "Fertilization combines gametes; it does not exchange segments.", "3": "Exchange is between homologues in meiosis I."}'),
  ('BIO-0338', 'Inheritance', 'Sex determination', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 103 (§18.7)', 'Drosophila has how many pairs of autosomes?', '["22", "4", "11", "3"]', 3, 'Drosophila has 3 pairs of autosomes and 1 pair of sex chromosomes (4 pairs in all).', '{"0": "22 pairs are in humans.", "1": "4 is the total number of chromosome pairs.", "2": "11 pairs of autosomes are in the grasshopper."}'),
  ('BIO-0339', 'Inheritance', 'Sex determination', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 103 (§18.7)', 'In the XX–XY system, males are described as heterogametic because they', '["produce only X-carrying sperm", "have two X chromosomes", "produce two types of sperm, half with X and half with Y", "produce eggs with Z or W"]', 2, 'Females (XX) make one type of egg; males (XY) make X- and Y-bearing sperm, so the sperm decides the sex.', '{"0": "Males produce Y-carrying sperm too.", "1": "Females have two X chromosomes.", "3": "That is the ZW system of birds."}'),
  ('BIO-0340', 'Inheritance', 'Sex determination', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 104 (§18.7)', 'A male grasshopper has 23 chromosomes because', '["it has 22 autosomes and only one X (XO)", "it has 22 autosomes and XY", "it has 21 autosomes and XX", "it is haploid"]', 0, 'In the XX–XO system the male has one X and no Y; it forms X sperm and nullo sperm.', '{"1": "XY would give 24 chromosomes.", "2": "Females have XX and 24 chromosomes.", "3": "Male grasshoppers are not haploid."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-0341', 'Inheritance', 'Sex determination', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 104 (§18.7)', 'In birds (ZW–ZZ system), the sex of the offspring is decided by', '["the type of sperm, because males are ZW", "the type of egg, because females are ZW and males ZZ", "temperature only", "the number of autosomes"]', 1, 'The female bird is heterogametic (Z and W eggs); all sperm carry Z.', '{"0": "Males are ZZ and make only Z sperm.", "2": "This is not the system described.", "3": "The sex chromosomes decide sex."}'),
  ('BIO-0342', 'Inheritance', 'Sex linkage', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 105 (§18.8)', 'Traits whose alleles are found on both the X and Y chromosomes and are equally common in both sexes are called', '["holandric traits", "sex-limited traits", "X–Y-linked or pseudo-autosomal traits", "X-linked recessive traits"]', 2, 'A few traits have alleles on both X and Y; they behave almost like autosomal traits.', '{"0": "Holandric genes are only on the Y.", "1": "Sex-limited genes are on autosomes but expressed in one sex.", "3": "X-linked recessive traits are commoner in males."}'),
  ('BIO-0343', 'Inheritance', 'Sex linkage', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 105 (§18.8)', 'In Morgan''s experiment, red-eyed F1 flies were crossed together. All the white-eyed flies in the F2 were', '["females", "equally males and females", "sterile", "males"]', 3, 'Of 4252 F2 flies, 782 were white-eyed and all of them were males.', '{"0": "No white-eyed females appeared in this cross.", "1": "The trait was not equally distributed.", "2": "Sterility was not reported."}'),
  ('BIO-0344', 'Inheritance', 'Sex linkage', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 106 (§18.8)', 'When Morgan crossed a white-eyed female with a red-eyed male Drosophila,', '["all daughters had red eyes and all sons had white eyes", "all offspring had red eyes", "all offspring had white eyes", "all daughters had white eyes and all sons red eyes"]', 0, 'Sons get their only X from the mother (white allele); daughters get the father''s X with the dominant red allele.', '{"1": "Sons received the mother''s X carrying the white allele.", "2": "Daughters received the red allele from the father.", "3": "This reverses the result."}'),
  ('BIO-0345', 'Inheritance', 'Embryonic screening', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 106 (§18.8)', 'Amniocentesis is usually performed between the', '["1st and 3rd weeks", "15th and 20th weeks of pregnancy", "36th and 40th weeks", "2nd and 4th days after fertilization"]', 1, 'A needle draws amniotic fluid containing foetal cells, which are cultured and checked for chromosome abnormalities.', '{"0": "Too early in pregnancy.", "2": "Too late; this is near term.", "3": "Far too early."}'),
  ('BIO-0346', 'Inheritance', 'X-linked traits', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 107 (§18.8.1)', 'A father has an X-linked recessive trait and the mother is normal (not a carrier). Their children will be', '["all sons affected and daughters normal", "half of sons affected", "all children affected", "all daughters carriers and no sons affected"]', 3, 'Daughters receive the father''s X (carrier); sons receive his Y, so they do not inherit the trait.', '{"0": "Sons do not inherit their father''s X.", "1": "The mother is not a carrier.", "2": "Daughters are carriers, not affected."}'),
  ('BIO-0347', 'Inheritance', 'X-linked traits', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 107 (§18.8.1)', 'Which of the following is an X-linked dominant disorder named in the book?', '["Haemophilia A", "Duchenne muscular dystrophy", "Vitamin D-resistant rickets", "Red-green colour blindness"]', 2, 'Vitamin D-resistant rickets, Rett syndrome and Aicardi syndrome are given as X-linked dominant.', '{"0": "Haemophilia A is X-linked recessive.", "1": "Duchenne muscular dystrophy is X-linked recessive.", "3": "Red-green colour blindness is X-linked recessive."}'),
  ('BIO-0348', 'Inheritance', 'X-linked traits', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 107 (§18.8.1)', 'A father has an X-linked dominant trait and the mother is normal. Their children will be', '["all daughters affected and no sons affected", "all sons affected and no daughters affected", "half of all children affected regardless of sex", "no children affected"]', 0, 'Every daughter gets the father''s X with the dominant allele; sons get his Y.', '{"1": "Sons get the Y, not the X, from their father.", "2": "That is the pattern when the mother is heterozygous.", "3": "The daughters are all affected."}'),
  ('BIO-0349', 'Inheritance', 'Y-linked traits', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 108 (§18.8.1)', 'Hypertrichosis of the pinna (hair on the rim of the ear) is inherited', '["from mother to son only", "from father to son only, because it is Y-linked", "equally to sons and daughters", "only by daughters"]', 1, 'Holandric genes are on the non-homologous part of the Y and appear only in males, passing from father to son.', '{"0": "That pattern fits X-linked recessive traits.", "2": "That fits autosomal or pseudo-autosomal traits.", "3": "Daughters do not inherit a Y."}'),
  ('BIO-0350', 'Inheritance', 'Haemophilia', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 108 (§18.8.2)', 'Haemophilia B is due to a defect in clotting factor', '["VIII", "IX", "XI", "I"]', 1, 'Haemophilia A involves factor VIII, B factor IX (both X-linked recessive), and C factor XI (autosomal recessive).', '{"0": "Factor VIII is haemophilia A.", "2": "Factor XI is haemophilia C.", "3": "Factor I is fibrinogen, not linked to these types."}'),
  ('BIO-0351', 'Inheritance', 'Haemophilia', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 108 (§18.8.2)', 'Why does haemophilia C occur equally in males and females?', '["It is an autosomal recessive trait", "It is X-linked recessive", "It is Y-linked", "It is sex-limited"]', 0, 'Haemophilia C (factor XI) is autosomal, so sex does not affect its frequency.', '{"1": "X-linked recessive traits are commoner in males.", "2": "Y-linked traits appear only in males.", "3": "Sex-limited traits are expressed in one sex."}'),
  ('BIO-0352', 'Inheritance', 'Haemophilia', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 108 (§18.8.2)', 'During blood clotting, the clotting cascade converts', '["insoluble fibrin into soluble fibrinogen", "platelets into red blood cells", "haemoglobin into bilirubin", "soluble fibrinogen into insoluble fibrin"]', 3, 'Fibrin forms a mesh that traps blood cells into a solid clot, later stabilized by cross-linking.', '{"0": "The conversion is the other way.", "1": "Platelets do not become RBCs.", "2": "Haemoglobin breakdown is not part of clotting."}'),
  ('BIO-0353', 'Inheritance', 'Colour blindness', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 109 (§18.8.2)', 'The genes for the red and green opsins of cone cells are located on', '["chromosome 7", "the Y chromosome", "the X chromosome", "chromosome 9"]', 2, 'Red and green opsin genes are on the X, which is why red–green defects are X-linked; the blue opsin gene is on chromosome 7.', '{"0": "Chromosome 7 carries the blue opsin gene.", "1": "They are not on the Y.", "3": "Chromosome 9 carries the ABO gene."}'),
  ('BIO-0354', 'Inheritance', 'Colour blindness', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 109 (§18.8.2)', 'Inability to perceive red because the red opsin is missing is called', '["deuteranopia", "tritanopia", "protanopia", "monochromacy"]', 2, 'Dichromacy can be protanopia (red), deuteranopia (green) or tritanopia (blue).', '{"0": "Deuteranopia is green blindness.", "1": "Tritanopia is blue blindness.", "3": "Monochromacy is perception of one colour only."}'),
  ('BIO-0355', 'Inheritance', 'Muscular dystrophy', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 109 (§18.8.2)', 'In Duchenne muscular dystrophy, boys cannot make the muscle protein', '["dystrophin", "myosin", "fibrinogen", "opsin"]', 0, 'This X-linked recessive disorder blocks dystrophin production, so muscle fibres die.', '{"1": "Myosin is not the protein named.", "2": "Fibrinogen is a clotting protein.", "3": "Opsins are cone pigments."}'),
  ('BIO-0356', 'Inheritance', 'Muscular dystrophy', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 110 (§18.8.2)', 'Which treatment can give short-term improvement in muscle strength in muscular dystrophy?', '["Rh-antiserum", "Insulin injections", "Colour-blind glasses", "Corticosteroid medicines"]', 3, 'There is no cure; physical therapy, corticosteroids, painkillers and surgery manage symptoms.', '{"0": "Rh-antiserum prevents Rh sensitization.", "1": "Insulin treats Type I diabetes.", "2": "These help colour-blind people."}'),
  ('BIO-0357', 'Inheritance', 'Sex-limited and sex-influenced', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 110 (§18.8.3)', 'Horn development in sheep, whose genes are present in both sexes but expressed only in males, is an example of a', '["sex-linked trait", "sex-limited trait", "sex-influenced trait", "Y-linked trait"]', 1, 'Sex-limited genes are on autosomes but expressed in only one sex because of anatomical or hormonal differences.', '{"0": "Sex-linked genes are on sex chromosomes.", "2": "Sex-influenced alleles are expressed in both sexes but differ in dominance.", "3": "Both sexes carry these genes, so they are not Y-linked."}'),
  ('BIO-0358', 'Inheritance', 'Sex-limited and sex-influenced', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 111 (§18.8.3)', 'Baldness is a sex-influenced trait because its allele', '["is located only on the Y chromosome", "is dominant in men but recessive in women", "is expressed only in women", "is located on the X chromosome"]', 1, 'Sex-influenced alleles differ in dominance between the sexes because of hormonal differences, so baldness is commoner in men.', '{"0": "It is not Y-linked.", "2": "It is expressed in both sexes.", "3": "It is not described as X-linked."}'),
  ('BIO-0359', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Chromosome structure', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 116 (§19.1)', 'Chromosomes were first discovered in 1882, in dividing cells of salamander larvae, by', '["Waldeyer-Hartz", "Wilhelm Johannsen", "Walther Flemming", "Walter Sutton"]', 2, 'Flemming discovered chromosomes in 1882; Waldeyer-Hartz named them in 1888.', '{"0": "Waldeyer-Hartz proposed the name \"chromosome\".", "1": "Johannsen coined the term \"gene\" in 1909.", "3": "Sutton proposed the chromosomal theory in 1902."}'),
  ('BIO-0360', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Chromosome structure', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 116 (§19.1)', 'In a non-dividing (interphase) cell, the uncoiled DNA and proteins form a fine network called', '["the kinetochore", "the spindle", "the karyotype", "chromatin"]', 3, 'Chromosomes are not visible in interphase; their material forms chromatin, which condenses when the cell prepares to divide.', '{"0": "The kinetochore is a protein disc at the centromere.", "1": "The spindle forms during division.", "2": "A karyotype is the complete set of chromosomes."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-0361', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Chromosome structure', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 116 (§19.1.1)', 'The disc-shaped protein structure on the centromere, where the chromosome attaches to the spindle, is the', '["telomere", "kinetochore", "satellite", "nucleosome"]', 1, 'The kinetochore is attached to the centromere and is the spindle attachment point during division.', '{"0": "Telomeres are the chromosome ends.", "2": "The satellite is the knob-like end beyond a secondary constriction.", "3": "A nucleosome is DNA wound on a histone octamer."}'),
  ('BIO-0362', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Chromosome structure', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 116 (§19.1.1)', 'The secondary constriction of some chromosomes, also called the nucleolar organizer region (NOR),', '["makes nucleoli during interphase", "attaches the chromosome to the spindle", "prevents chromosome ends from sticking together", "joins the two sister chromatids"]', 0, 'The NOR produces the nucleoli in interphase.', '{"1": "That is the kinetochore at the centromere.", "2": "That is the role of telomeres.", "3": "Sister chromatids are joined at the centromere."}'),
  ('BIO-0363', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Chromosome structure', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 117 (§19.1.1)', 'Telomeres are the terminal ends of chromosomes that', '["attach chromosomes to spindle fibres", "contain the genes for nucleoli", "prevent chromosomes from joining to each other at their ends", "join sister chromatids together"]', 2, 'The book states that telomeres prevent two chromosomes from attaching to each other by their ends.', '{"0": "Spindle attachment is at the kinetochore.", "1": "Nucleoli are made at the NOR.", "3": "Sister chromatids are held at the centromere."}'),
  ('BIO-0364', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Chromosome structure', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 117 (§19.1.1)', 'A chromosome with its centromere located near (but not at) one end is', '["metacentric", "acrocentric", "sub-metacentric", "telocentric"]', 1, 'Metacentric: centre; sub-metacentric: slightly off centre; acrocentric: near the end; telocentric: at the end.', '{"0": "Metacentric has the centromere in the middle.", "2": "Sub-metacentric has it slightly away from the centre.", "3": "Telocentric has it at the very end."}'),
  ('BIO-0365', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Chromosome composition', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 117 (§19.1.2)', 'According to the book, a chromosome is made of about', '["60% DNA and 40% proteins", "90% DNA and 10% RNA", "50% DNA and 50% lipids", "40% DNA and 60% histone proteins"]', 3, 'Chromosomes are about 40% DNA and 60% proteins (histones).', '{"0": "The proportions are reversed.", "1": "Chromosomes are mainly DNA and protein, not RNA.", "2": "Lipids are not a main component."}'),
  ('BIO-0366', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Chromosome composition', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 117 (§19.1.2)', 'DNA binds tightly to histones because', '["DNA is negatively charged and histones are positively charged", "both are negatively charged", "histones are rich in acidic amino acids", "DNA has no phosphate groups"]', 0, 'Phosphate groups make DNA negative; histones are rich in basic arginine and lysine, so they are positive.', '{"1": "Like charges would repel.", "2": "Histones are rich in basic amino acids.", "3": "Phosphate groups give DNA its negative charge."}'),
  ('BIO-0367', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Chromosome composition', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 117 (§19.1.2)', 'The histone octamer of a nucleosome consists of two copies each of', '["H2A, H2B, H3 and H4", "H1, H2A, H3 and H4", "H1, H2B, H3 and H4", "H1, H2A, H2B and H3"]', 0, 'The octamer is 2 × (H2A, H2B, H3, H4); H1 is the linker histone near the DNA entry and exit points.', '{"1": "H1 is not part of the octamer.", "2": "H1 is not part of the octamer.", "3": "H1 is not part of the octamer, and H4 is."}'),
  ('BIO-0368', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Chromosome composition', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 118 (§19.1.2)', 'Arrange the levels of chromosome packing from thinnest to thickest.', '["Chromatin fibre (30 nm) → nucleosome string (11 nm) → chromatid (700 nm) → supercoil (300 nm)", "Supercoil (300 nm) → chromatid (700 nm) → nucleosome string (11 nm) → chromatin fibre (30 nm)", "Nucleosome string (11 nm) → chromatin fibre (30 nm) → supercoil (300 nm) → chromatid (700 nm)", "Nucleosome string (11 nm) → supercoil (300 nm) → chromatin fibre (30 nm) → chromatid (700 nm)"]', 2, 'The 2 nm DNA forms an 11 nm nucleosome string, coiled to a 30 nm fibre, folded to a 300 nm supercoil and condensed into a 700 nm chromatid.', '{"0": "The order of the first two is wrong.", "1": "This is not in order of thickness.", "3": "The chromatin fibre comes before the supercoil."}'),
  ('BIO-0369', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Chromosome composition', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 118 (§19.1.2)', 'Inactive portions of chromatin that produce dark bands are called', '["euchromatin", "telomeres", "exons", "heterochromatin"]', 3, 'Inactive regions are heterochromatin; active regions are euchromatin.', '{"0": "Euchromatin is the active portion.", "1": "Telomeres are chromosome ends.", "2": "Exons are coding regions of genes."}'),
  ('BIO-0370', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Genes', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 118 (§19.1.3)', 'The term "gene" for the basic unit of heredity was introduced in 1909 by', '["Gregor Mendel", "Wilhelm Johannsen", "Walther Flemming", "Francis Crick"]', 1, 'The Danish botanist Johannsen introduced the word "gene".', '{"0": "Mendel called them \"factors\".", "2": "Flemming discovered chromosomes.", "3": "Crick proposed the central dogma."}'),
  ('BIO-0371', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'DNA as hereditary material', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 119 (§19.2)', 'Which is listed in the book as a requirement of a molecule that serves as genetic material?', '["It must be able to replicate itself before cell division", "It must never change over time", "It must be found only in the cytoplasm", "It must be made of amino acids"]', 0, 'Genetic material must code for amino acid sequences, replicate itself, be in the nucleus and be able to change over time for evolution.', '{"1": "It must be able to change to account for evolution.", "2": "It must be in the nucleus of eukaryotic cells.", "3": "Proteins are made of amino acids; DNA fulfils the requirements."}'),
  ('BIO-0372', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Griffith', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 120 (§19.2.1)', 'In Griffith''s experiment, the mice died when injected with', '["living R-type bacteria alone", "heat-killed S-type bacteria alone", "a mixture of heat-killed S-type and living R-type bacteria", "heat-killed R-type bacteria alone"]', 2, 'Information from the dead S-type passed to the living R-type, transforming them into virulent S-type bacteria.', '{"0": "R-type bacteria alone were harmless.", "1": "Heat-killed S-type alone did not cause disease.", "3": "Heat-killed R-type was not virulent."}'),
  ('BIO-0373', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Griffith', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 120 (§19.2.1)', 'The S-type strain of Streptococcus pneumoniae was virulent because it had', '["no cell wall", "a polysaccharide coat that protects it from the host''s immune system", "a protein coat injected into host cells", "a rough colony surface"]', 1, 'The S strain had a polysaccharide coat (smooth colonies); the R strain lacked it (rough colonies).', '{"0": "Bacteria have cell walls; the difference is the coat.", "2": "Protein coat injection describes bacteriophages.", "3": "Rough colonies belong to the non-virulent R strain."}'),
  ('BIO-0374', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Griffith', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 120 (§19.2.1)', 'Griffith called the change of R-type bacteria into S-type by material from dead S-type cells', '["transduction", "translation", "transcription", "transformation"]', 3, 'Transformation is the transfer of genetic material from one organism to another, changing the recipient''s genetic makeup.', '{"0": "Transduction is not the term Griffith used here.", "1": "Translation is protein synthesis on ribosomes.", "2": "Transcription is RNA synthesis from DNA."}'),
  ('BIO-0375', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Griffith', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 121 (§19.2.1)', 'Avery, MacLeod and McCarty (1944) found that no transformation occurred when they', '["removed all proteins from the dead S-type bacteria", "heated the R-type bacteria", "removed all DNA from the dead S-type bacteria before mixing them with live R-type", "added extra polysaccharide to the mixture"]', 2, 'Removing proteins did not stop transformation, but removing DNA did; so DNA is the transforming principle.', '{"0": "Transformation still occurred without proteins.", "1": "This was not their key test.", "3": "Their test was removal of proteins or of DNA."}'),
  ('BIO-0376', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Hershey–Chase', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 121 (§19.2.2)', 'In the Hershey–Chase experiment, the protein coats of bacteriophages were labelled with', '["³⁵S", "³²P", "¹⁵N", "¹⁴C"]', 0, 'Sulphur occurs in proteins (in amino acids) but not in DNA; phosphorus occurs in DNA phosphate groups.', '{"1": "³²P was used to label the DNA.", "2": "¹⁵N was used by Meselson and Stahl.", "3": "¹⁴C was not used in this experiment."}'),
  ('BIO-0377', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Hershey–Chase', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 122 (§19.2.2)', 'In Hershey and Chase''s setup with ³²P-labelled phages, after centrifugation the radioactivity was found', '["in the medium, not in the bacterial cells", "equally in cells and medium", "nowhere, because ³²P decays at once", "in the bacterial cells, not in the medium"]', 3, 'The labelled DNA entered the bacteria and directed new phage production; the ³⁵S-labelled coats stayed outside in the medium.', '{"0": "That was the result for ³⁵S-labelled protein.", "1": "The DNA went into the cells.", "2": "Radioactivity was detected."}'),
  ('BIO-0378', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Replication models', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 123 (§19.3.1)', 'According to the semi-conservative model of DNA replication, each daughter DNA molecule has', '["both parental strands", "one strand from the parent and one newly made strand", "both new strands", "strands that are mixtures of old and new nucleotides"]', 1, 'The parent strands separate and each serves as a template for a new strand.', '{"0": "One daughter would have both parental strands in the conservative model.", "2": "One daughter would have both new strands in the conservative model.", "3": "Mixed strands describe the dispersive model."}'),
  ('BIO-0379', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Meselson–Stahl', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 124 (§19.3.2)', 'Meselson and Stahl separated heavy and light DNA by ultracentrifugation in a solution of', '["sodium chloride", "sucrose", "glucose", "caesium chloride"]', 3, 'Ultracentrifugation of caesium chloride creates a density gradient; each DNA settles where its density matches the caesium.', '{"0": "Sodium chloride was not used.", "1": "Sucrose was not used.", "2": "Glucose was not used."}'),
  ('BIO-0380', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Meselson–Stahl', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 124 (§19.3.2)', 'After the first replication in ¹⁴N medium, Meselson and Stahl found a single band of intermediate density. This ruled out the', '["conservative model", "semi-conservative model", "dispersive model", "law of segregation"]', 0, 'The conservative model predicts one heavy and one light band after one round, not a single intermediate band.', '{"1": "The result fits semi-conservative replication.", "2": "A single intermediate band after one round fits the dispersive model too; the second round ruled it out.", "3": "This is a law of inheritance, not a replication model."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-0381', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Meselson–Stahl', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 124 (§19.3.2)', 'After the second round of replication in ¹⁴N medium, Meselson and Stahl observed', '["one band of intermediate density only", "two bands, one of intermediate density and one light", "one heavy and one light band", "one heavy band only"]', 1, 'Each hybrid molecule gives one hybrid and one fully light (¹⁴N/¹⁴N) daughter, exactly as semi-conservative replication predicts.', '{"0": "A single intermediate band was seen after the first round.", "2": "Heavy DNA was not present after replication in ¹⁴N.", "3": "All heavy DNA was seen only at 0 minutes."}'),
  ('BIO-0382', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Replication mechanism', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 126 (§19.3.3)', 'How do prokaryotic and eukaryotic DNA differ in replication origin sites?', '["Prokaryotic DNA has many origins; eukaryotic DNA has one", "Both have only one origin", "Prokaryotic DNA has one origin; eukaryotic DNA has many", "Neither needs an origin"]', 2, 'Prokaryotes have a single origin and termination site; eukaryotes have many origins along each chromosome, so each chromosome has several replicons.', '{"0": "This is reversed.", "1": "Eukaryotes have many origins.", "3": "Replication always starts at origin sites."}'),
  ('BIO-0383', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Replication mechanism', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 126 (§19.3.3)', 'At the replication origin, the enzyme that breaks hydrogen bonds between base pairs to separate the strands is', '["helicase", "primase", "ligase", "peptidyl transferase"]', 0, 'Helicases unwind the DNA, forming replication forks.', '{"1": "Primase lays down RNA primers.", "2": "Ligase joins Okazaki fragments.", "3": "Peptidyl transferase forms peptide bonds in translation."}'),
  ('BIO-0384', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Replication mechanism', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 126 (§19.3.3)', 'Single-strand binding proteins in DNA replication', '["unwind the double helix", "add RNA nucleotides as primers", "bind to separated strands and prevent them from re-joining", "join Okazaki fragments"]', 2, 'They keep the template strands apart so they can be copied.', '{"0": "Unwinding is done by helicase.", "1": "Primers are made by primase.", "3": "Fragments are joined by DNA ligase."}'),
  ('BIO-0385', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Replication mechanism', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 126 (§19.3.3)', 'An RNA primer in DNA replication is a short chain of about', '["100–200 DNA nucleotides laid down by DNA polymerase I", "250 adenine nucleotides", "1000–2000 RNA nucleotides laid down by helicase", "5–10 RNA nucleotides laid down by primase"]', 3, 'Primase attaches a short RNA primer of 5–10 nucleotides; DNA polymerase III then extends from its 3′ end.', '{"0": "DNA polymerase I later replaces primers; 100–200 is the eukaryotic Okazaki fragment length.", "1": "About 250 A nucleotides make the poly-A tail of mRNA.", "2": "Helicase does not make primers."}'),
  ('BIO-0386', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Replication mechanism', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 127 (§19.3.3)', 'The raw materials used by DNA polymerase III to build new strands are', '["ribonucleoside triphosphates (ATP, CTP, GTP, UTP)", "deoxyribonucleoside triphosphates (dATP, dCTP, dGTP, dTTP)", "amino acids", "deoxyribonucleoside monophosphates without energy"]', 1, 'As each nucleotide is added, two phosphates are removed, releasing energy for the building process.', '{"0": "rNTPs are used in transcription.", "2": "Amino acids are used in translation.", "3": "The triphosphate form supplies the energy."}'),
  ('BIO-0387', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Replication mechanism', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 127 (§19.3.3)', 'In prokaryotes, DNA polymerase I', '["replaces the RNA primers with DNA at the end of replication", "is the main enzyme that adds nucleotides to new strands", "unwinds the double helix", "only proofreads mRNA"]', 0, 'Polymerase III is the main builder; polymerase I removes primers and fills gaps; polymerase II repairs DNA damage.', '{"1": "That is polymerase III.", "2": "Unwinding is done by helicase.", "3": "This is not its function."}'),
  ('BIO-0388', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Replication mechanism', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 127 (§19.3.3)', 'The book explains that RNA, rather than DNA, is used as a primer because', '["RNA is more stable than DNA", "starting chains introduces many errors, and RNA marks this stretch as temporary so it is easily removed", "DNA polymerase III can only copy RNA", "RNA primers stay permanently in the new DNA"]', 1, 'The error-prone starting stretch is made of RNA so that it can be excised and replaced later.', '{"0": "Stability is not the reason given.", "2": "DNA polymerase III copies DNA templates.", "3": "Primers are removed and replaced by DNA."}'),
  ('BIO-0389', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Replication mechanism', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 128 (§19.3.3)', 'Okazaki fragments are formed on the lagging strand because', '["the lagging strand has no template", "helicase cuts the lagging strand into pieces", "DNA polymerase can only add to a 3′ end, so on that strand it must work away from the fork in short pieces", "the lagging strand is copied by RNA polymerase"]', 2, 'To keep synthesis 5′ to 3′, the enzyme jumps back to a new primer each time, making separate fragments later joined by ligase.', '{"0": "Both parental strands act as templates.", "1": "Helicase separates strands; it does not cut them.", "3": "DNA polymerase builds both strands."}'),
  ('BIO-0390', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Replication mechanism', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 128 (§19.3.3)', 'The region of DNA between a replication origin site and a termination site is called a', '["codon", "operon", "promoter", "replicon"]', 3, 'A replication unit from origin to termination site is a replicon; eukaryotic chromosomes have several.', '{"0": "A codon is a triplet of mRNA nucleotides.", "1": "This term is not used for it in the book.", "2": "A promoter is where RNA polymerase binds."}'),
  ('BIO-0391', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Replication mechanism', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 129 (§19.3.3)', 'Compared with prokaryotes, eukaryotic Okazaki fragments are', '["longer, about 1000–2000 nucleotides", "the same length", "shorter, about 100–200 nucleotides", "not formed at all"]', 2, 'Prokaryotic Okazaki fragments are 1000–2000 nucleotides; eukaryotic ones are 100–200.', '{"0": "1000–2000 is the prokaryotic length.", "1": "They differ in length.", "3": "Both have lagging strands with Okazaki fragments."}'),
  ('BIO-0392', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Replication mechanism', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 129 (§19.3.3)', 'Eukaryotes have at least how many DNA polymerases, according to the book?', '["3", "14", "1", "20"]', 1, 'Prokaryotes use DNA polymerase I, II and III; eukaryotes have at least 14, such as alpha, delta and epsilon.', '{"0": "Three are named for prokaryotes.", "2": "More than one is needed.", "3": "20 is the number of amino acids."}'),
  ('BIO-0393', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Stability and variability', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 129 (§19.3.3)', 'The "stability" of DNA during replication is maintained mainly by', '["mutations caused by radiation", "crossing over in meiosis", "random insertion of transposons", "the proofreading ability of DNA polymerases"]', 3, 'Proofreading keeps the nucleotide sequence accurate, preserving the organism''s traits.', '{"0": "Mutations are the source of variability, not stability.", "1": "Crossing over creates variation.", "2": "Transposons can cause mutations."}'),
  ('BIO-0394', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Central dogma', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 130 (§19.4)', 'The central dogma, a term used by Francis Crick, describes the flow of genetic information as', '["DNA → RNA → protein", "protein → RNA → DNA", "RNA → DNA → protein", "DNA → protein → RNA"]', 0, 'Information passes from DNA to an RNA copy by transcription, and from RNA to protein by translation; it does not go back from protein.', '{"1": "Information never flows back from protein.", "2": "The book''s dogma starts from DNA.", "3": "RNA is made before protein."}'),
  ('BIO-0395', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Genetic code', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 131 (§19.4.1)', 'Crick reasoned that codons must be three nucleotides long because', '["four nucleotides cannot be arranged in triplets", "there are exactly 64 amino acids", "single nucleotides give 20 combinations", "two-nucleotide codons give only 16 combinations, too few for 20 amino acids"]', 3, 'With four bases, triplets give 4³ = 64 combinations, more than enough for 20 amino acids.', '{"0": "Triplets give 64 combinations.", "1": "There are 20 amino acids.", "2": "Single nucleotides give only 4."}'),
  ('BIO-0396', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Genetic code', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 131 (§19.4.1)', 'A DNA codon TAC would be transcribed into which mRNA codon?', '["AUG", "ATG", "UAC", "TUG"]', 0, 'mRNA is complementary to the template: T→A, A→U, C→G, giving AUG.', '{"1": "RNA has U instead of T.", "2": "This is the same sequence with U, not the complement.", "3": "This is not complementary."}'),
  ('BIO-0397', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Genetic code', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 131 (§19.4.1)', 'Nirenberg added a poly-U mRNA to bacterial cytoplasm and obtained a chain of', '["methionine", "glycine", "phenylalanine", "valine"]', 2, 'The polypeptide made was all phenylalanine, showing that UUU codes for phenylalanine.', '{"0": "AUG codes for methionine.", "1": "Glycine is coded by GG_ codons.", "3": "Valine is not coded by UUU."}'),
  ('BIO-0398', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Genetic code', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 131 (§19.4.1)', 'Which of these is a stop (nonsense) codon?', '["AUG", "UAG", "UUU", "GGC"]', 1, 'UAA, UAG and UGA are stop codons and do not specify amino acids.', '{"0": "AUG is the start codon (methionine).", "2": "UUU codes for phenylalanine.", "3": "GGC codes for glycine."}'),
  ('BIO-0399', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Genetic code', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 131 (§19.4.1)', 'Har Gobind Khorana, who studied at Government College Lahore, determined the amino acids specified by', '["the remaining 17 codons", "all 64 codons alone", "only the 3 stop codons", "47 codons with Philip Leder"]', 0, 'Nirenberg and Leder worked out 47 codons; Khorana determined the remaining 17.', '{"1": "The work was shared.", "2": "He did not only study stop codons.", "3": "That was the work of Nirenberg and Leder."}'),
  ('BIO-0400', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Genetic code', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 132 (§19.4.2)', 'The genetic code in mitochondrial DNA is slightly different from that of chromosomal DNA. This is an exception to the code being', '["triplet", "non-overlapping", "universal", "without punctuation"]', 2, 'The code is the same in almost all organisms; mitochondria, some ciliates and some viruses are exceptions.', '{"0": "Mitochondria still use triplet codons.", "1": "This is not the property affected.", "3": "This is not the property affected."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-0401', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Genetic code', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 132 (§19.4.2)', 'The sequence AUGAGCGCA is read only as AUG / AGC / GCA. This shows that the genetic code is', '["degenerate", "non-overlapping", "universal", "ambiguous"]', 1, 'Each triplet is read independently of the next, so bases are not shared between codons.', '{"0": "Degeneracy means several codons for one amino acid.", "2": "Universality means the same code in almost all organisms.", "3": "The code is not ambiguous."}'),
  ('BIO-0402', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Genetic code', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 132 (§19.4.2)', 'Because the genetic code is universal,', '["every organism has the same genes", "each codon codes for several amino acids", "mitochondria have no DNA", "genes can be transferred from one organism to another and still be read correctly"]', 3, 'A codon such as AGA means arginine in bacteria and humans alike, so transferred genes are understood.', '{"0": "Organisms share the code, not the same genes.", "1": "Each codon codes for one amino acid.", "2": "Mitochondria have their own DNA."}'),
  ('BIO-0403', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Transcription', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 133 (§19.5)', 'In transcription, the coding (sense) strand of DNA is the strand that', '["is transcribed into mRNA", "is complementary to the mRNA", "is not transcribed and has the same sequence as the mRNA, with T in place of U", "is removed during splicing"]', 2, 'The template (antisense) strand is transcribed; the coding strand matches the mRNA except that DNA has T where RNA has U.', '{"0": "The template strand is the one transcribed.", "1": "The template strand is complementary to the mRNA.", "3": "Splicing acts on mRNA, not on DNA strands."}'),
  ('BIO-0404', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Transcription', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 133 (§19.5)', 'Unlike DNA replication, transcription', '["does not need a primer", "needs an RNA primer", "uses DNA polymerase III", "copies both DNA strands"]', 0, 'RNA polymerase binds the promoter and starts the RNA chain without a primer.', '{"1": "No primer is needed in transcription.", "2": "RNA polymerase is used.", "3": "Only the template strand is transcribed."}'),
  ('BIO-0405', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Transcription', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 133 (§19.5)', 'In prokaryotes, the promoter sequence TATAAT located 10 nucleotides before the start point is called the', '["TATA box (–25 sequence)", "CAAT box (–70 sequence)", "–35 sequence", "–10 sequence"]', 3, 'Prokaryotic promoters are TTGACA (–35) and TATAAT (–10); eukaryotic ones include the TATA box (–25) and CAAT box (–70).', '{"0": "The TATA box (TATAAA) is eukaryotic.", "1": "The CAAT box is eukaryotic.", "2": "The –35 sequence is TTGACA."}'),
  ('BIO-0406', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Transcription', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 134 (§19.5)', 'In eukaryotes, mRNA is synthesized by', '["RNA polymerase I", "RNA polymerase II", "RNA polymerase III", "DNA polymerase III"]', 1, 'RNA polymerase I makes rRNA (in the nucleolus), II makes mRNA and III makes tRNA; prokaryotes have only one RNA polymerase.', '{"0": "RNA polymerase I makes rRNA.", "2": "RNA polymerase III makes tRNA.", "3": "DNA polymerase III replicates DNA."}'),
  ('BIO-0407', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Transcription', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 134 (§19.5)', 'In the elongation phase of transcription, the RNA chain grows in the', '["3′ to 5′ direction, using deoxyribonucleotides", "5′ to 3′ direction, using amino acids", "3′ to 5′ direction, using primers", "5′ to 3′ direction, using ribonucleoside triphosphates"]', 3, 'RNA polymerase adds rNTPs to the 3′ end; two phosphates are released with each addition.', '{"0": "Synthesis is 5′ to 3′ with ribonucleotides.", "1": "Amino acids are used in translation.", "2": "No primer is used and the direction is 5′ to 3′."}'),
  ('BIO-0408', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Transcription', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 135 (§19.5)', 'Transcription in prokaryotes stops when the mRNA forms a GC hairpin followed by a run of U nucleotides because', '["a release factor binds to the stop codon on the ribosome", "the hairpin makes RNA polymerase pause and the weak U–A pairing cannot hold the RNA on the DNA", "the sigma subunit cuts the RNA", "DNA ligase seals the transcription bubble"]', 1, 'During the pause, the weak U–A RNA–DNA pairs let the RNA dissociate from the template, ending transcription.', '{"0": "Release factors act in translation.", "2": "The sigma subunit recognizes promoters.", "3": "Ligase is a replication enzyme."}'),
  ('BIO-0409', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'mRNA processing', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 136 (§19.5)', 'The 5′ cap added to eukaryotic mRNA is a', '["chain of about 250 A nucleotides", "stop codon", "methylated GTP that protects the mRNA from nucleases and phosphatases", "piece of intron"]', 2, 'A methylated GTP attached to the 5′ end protects the mRNA on its journey to the cytoplasm.', '{"0": "That is the 3′ poly-A tail.", "1": "A stop codon is part of the coding sequence.", "3": "Introns are removed by splicing."}'),
  ('BIO-0410', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'mRNA processing', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 136 (§19.5)', 'Eukaryotic mRNA becomes shorter before translation mainly because', '["introns are removed by splicing and the exons are joined", "the 5′ cap is removed", "exons are removed and introns joined", "the poly-A tail is doubled"]', 0, 'Immature mRNA contains non-coding introns; splicing removes them, leaving only coding exons.', '{"1": "The cap is added, not removed.", "2": "Exons are kept; introns are removed.", "3": "A tail is added, which would lengthen the mRNA."}'),
  ('BIO-0411', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Translation', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 137 (§19.6)', 'Each amino acid is attached to its own tRNA by', '["peptidyl transferase", "RNA polymerase", "release factor", "a specific aminoacyl-tRNA synthetase"]', 3, 'There are 20 kinds of aminoacyl-tRNA synthetase; each binds one amino acid and the tRNAs carrying its anticodons.', '{"0": "Peptidyl transferase forms peptide bonds on the ribosome.", "1": "RNA polymerase makes RNA.", "2": "Release factor ends translation."}'),
  ('BIO-0412', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Translation', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 137 (§19.6)', 'The first amino acid of a polypeptide in prokaryotes is', '["unmodified methionine", "phenylalanine", "N-formyl methionine", "valine"]', 2, 'Prokaryotes start with modified N-formyl methionine; eukaryotes with unmodified methionine.', '{"0": "Unmodified methionine starts eukaryotic polypeptides.", "1": "Phenylalanine is coded by UUU, not the start codon.", "3": "Valine is not the start amino acid."}'),
  ('BIO-0413', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Translation', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 138 (§19.6)', 'On the ribosome, the aminoacyl site (A site) is where', '["empty tRNAs leave the ribosome", "the next aminoacyl-tRNA binds", "the mRNA is spliced", "the release factor makes the 5′ cap"]', 1, 'The P site holds the tRNA with the growing chain, the A site receives the next aa-tRNA and the E site releases empty tRNAs.', '{"0": "That is the E (exit) site.", "2": "Splicing happens in the nucleus.", "3": "Release factors end translation; caps are added in the nucleus."}'),
  ('BIO-0414', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Translation', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 138 (§19.6)', 'Relative to the mRNA, the three ribosomal sites are arranged from 5′ to 3′ as', '["E – P – A", "A – P – E", "P – A – E", "E – A – P"]', 0, 'The ribosome moves toward the 3′ end, so new tRNAs enter at A, move to P and leave from E.', '{"1": "This is the reverse.", "2": "The E site is on the 5′ side.", "3": "P lies between E and A."}'),
  ('BIO-0415', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Translation', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 138 (§19.6)', 'The movement of the ribosome three nucleotides along the mRNA during elongation is called', '["transformation", "transposition", "transcription", "translocation"]', 3, 'After each peptide bond forms, the ribosome moves one codon toward the 3′ end, shifting tRNAs from A to P and P to E.', '{"0": "Transformation is uptake of genetic material by bacteria.", "1": "Transposition is movement of jumping genes.", "2": "Transcription makes RNA."}'),
  ('BIO-0416', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Translation', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 140 (§19.6)', 'Translation ends when a stop codon reaches the A site because', '["a special tRNA brings a stop amino acid", "no tRNA has an anticodon for it, and a release factor frees the polypeptide", "the ribosome cuts the mRNA at the stop codon", "RNA polymerase falls off"]', 1, 'Stop codons have no matching tRNA; a release factor recognizes them and releases the chain; the subunits separate.', '{"0": "There is no amino acid for stop codons.", "2": "The mRNA is not cut by the ribosome.", "3": "RNA polymerase acts in transcription."}'),
  ('BIO-0417', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Translation', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 140 (§19.6.1)', 'Which is true of translation in eukaryotes but not prokaryotes?', '["Ribosomes are 70S", "The first amino acid is N-formyl methionine", "mRNA is capped, tailed and spliced before translation", "Translation begins while mRNA is still being made, with no modification"]', 2, 'Eukaryotic mRNA is processed in the nucleus; ribosomes are 80S; translation occurs in cytoplasm and on rough ER.', '{"0": "70S ribosomes are prokaryotic.", "1": "N-formyl methionine is prokaryotic.", "3": "Immediate, unmodified translation is prokaryotic."}'),
  ('BIO-0418', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Gene regulation', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 140 (§19.7)', 'All cell types of an organism have the same genome, yet they differ because', '["different genes are switched on in different cell types", "each cell type has different chromosomes", "some cells have lost most of their DNA", "only liver cells express genes"]', 0, 'Regulation of gene expression is the basis of cellular differentiation.', '{"1": "They have the same chromosomes.", "2": "The genome is the same in all cell types.", "3": "All cells express some genes."}'),
  ('BIO-0419', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Gene regulation', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 141 (§19.7)', 'In negative gene regulation, repressor proteins', '["help RNA polymerase bind to the promoter", "remove introns from mRNA", "bind to operators near the promoter and block RNA polymerase", "add a poly-A tail to mRNA"]', 2, 'Repressors stop or reduce transcription by blocking RNA polymerase.', '{"0": "That is positive regulation by activators.", "1": "That is splicing.", "3": "That is post-transcriptional modification."}'),
  ('BIO-0420', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Gene regulation', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 142 (§19.7)', 'Liver cells express genes for alcohol dehydrogenase while brain neurons keep the same genes switched off. This illustrates', '["cell-specific regulation of gene expression", "mutation in brain neurons", "loss of genes from neurons", "crossing over"]', 0, 'Both cell types carry the gene, but only liver cells express it.', '{"1": "No mutation is involved.", "2": "Neurons still have these genes.", "3": "Crossing over happens in meiosis."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-0421', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Gene regulation', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 142 (§19.7)', 'Alternative splicing of mRNA allows', '["a gene to be copied into DNA", "two genes to share one promoter only", "proteins to be converted back into mRNA", "more than one polypeptide to be made from a single gene"]', 3, 'Regulatory proteins in different cell types remove pieces of mRNA in different ways, giving mRNAs of different lengths.', '{"0": "Splicing acts on mRNA, not DNA copying.", "1": "This is not what alternative splicing means.", "2": "Information does not flow back from protein."}'),
  ('BIO-0422', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Mutation', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 143 (§19.8)', 'An agent that causes mutation is a', '["mutant", "mutagen", "transposon", "codon"]', 1, 'Mutagens cause mutations; the organism or cell carrying a mutation is the mutant.', '{"0": "A mutant is the organism or cell with the mutation.", "2": "A transposon is a jumping gene.", "3": "A codon is a nucleotide triplet."}'),
  ('BIO-0423', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Mutation', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 143 (§19.8.1)', 'Ionizing radiation such as X-rays and gamma rays damages DNA by', '["forming free radicals that can break phosphodiester bonds", "joining adjacent pyrimidines with covalent bonds", "removing amino groups from adenine", "inserting transposons into genes"]', 0, 'Ionizing radiation converts atoms into free radicals, which can break the DNA backbone.', '{"1": "That is the effect of UV radiation.", "2": "That is the action of some mutagen chemicals.", "3": "Transposons insert themselves; radiation does not insert them."}'),
  ('BIO-0424', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Mutation', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 144 (§19.8.1)', 'A change from GGC to GGU that still codes for glycine is an example of a', '["missense mutation", "silent mutation", "nonsense mutation", "frameshift deletion"]', 1, 'Because of the degeneracy of the code, both codons give glycine, so the protein is unchanged.', '{"0": "A missense mutation changes one amino acid.", "2": "A nonsense mutation creates a premature stop codon.", "3": "No nucleotide is lost."}'),
  ('BIO-0425', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Mutation', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 144 (§19.8.1)', 'A mutation that creates a premature stop codon in the middle of a gene, as in some cases of Duchenne muscular dystrophy, is a', '["silent mutation", "missense mutation", "chromosomal translocation", "nonsense mutation"]', 3, 'A premature stop codon gives a short, non-functional protein.', '{"0": "A silent mutation does not change the protein.", "1": "A missense mutation swaps one amino acid.", "2": "This is a point mutation, not a translocation."}'),
  ('BIO-0426', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Mutation', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 144 (§19.8.1)', 'The common cause of cystic fibrosis named in the book is', '["trisomy of chromosome 21", "substitution of glutamic acid by valine", "deletion of three nucleotides, so one amino acid is missing", "non-disjunction of the X chromosome"]', 2, 'Losing three nucleotides leaves out one amino acid, so the protein folds wrongly, affecting lungs and digestion.', '{"0": "Trisomy 21 causes Down syndrome.", "1": "Glu → Val causes sickle cell anaemia.", "3": "X non-disjunction causes Klinefelter or Turner syndrome."}'),
  ('BIO-0427', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Point mutation disorders', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 145 (§19.8.1)', 'The gene for the β chain of haemoglobin, mutated in sickle cell anaemia, is located on', '["chromosome 12", "chromosome 21", "chromosome 11", "the X chromosome"]', 2, 'Sickle cell anaemia is due to a recessive allele of the β-globin gene on chromosome 11.', '{"0": "Chromosome 12 carries the PAH gene (phenylketonuria).", "1": "Chromosome 21 is involved in Down syndrome.", "3": "Sickle cell anaemia is autosomal."}'),
  ('BIO-0428', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Point mutation disorders', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 145 (§19.8.1)', 'In sickle cell anaemia, the abnormal β chain has', '["valine in place of glutamic acid", "glutamic acid in place of valine", "phenylalanine in place of tyrosine", "an extra chromosome 11"]', 0, 'A single base substitution changes the codon for glutamic acid into one for valine.', '{"1": "This reverses the change.", "2": "This is not the change in sickle cell anaemia.", "3": "It is a point mutation, not an extra chromosome."}'),
  ('BIO-0429', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Point mutation disorders', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 145 (§19.8.1)', 'Which treatment can provide a long-term cure for sickle cell anaemia?', '["A low-phenylalanine diet", "Estrogen replacement therapy", "Growth hormone injections", "Bone marrow transplant"]', 3, 'Bone marrow transplants can cure it; pain relief, transfusions, vaccines and antibiotics manage it.', '{"0": "A low-phenylalanine diet is for PKU.", "1": "Estrogen therapy is used in Turner syndrome.", "2": "Growth hormone is used in Turner syndrome."}'),
  ('BIO-0430', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Point mutation disorders', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 145 (§19.8.1)', 'In phenylketonuria (PKU), the defective liver enzyme normally converts', '["tyrosine into phenylalanine", "phenylalanine into tyrosine", "glucose into glycogen", "ammonia into urea"]', 1, 'Phenylalanine hydroxylase (gene on chromosome 12) converts phenylalanine to tyrosine; without it phenylalanine builds up.', '{"0": "The direction is reversed.", "2": "That is glycogenesis.", "3": "That is the urea cycle."}'),
  ('BIO-0431', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Point mutation disorders', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 146 (§19.8.1)', 'A person with PKU should avoid foods that are high in', '["vitamin C", "carbohydrates only", "iodine", "phenylalanine, such as meat, dairy and eggs"]', 3, 'A low-phenylalanine diet with regular blood monitoring keeps phenylalanine below toxic levels.', '{"0": "Vitamin C is not the problem.", "1": "The issue is an amino acid.", "2": "Iodine is linked to thyroid function."}'),
  ('BIO-0432', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Chromosomal mutations', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 146 (§19.8.2)', 'A chromosomal mutation in which a segment of one chromosome becomes part of another chromosome is a', '["inversion", "deletion", "translocation", "substitution"]', 2, 'In translocation a segment moves to another chromosome; in inversion a segment''s orientation is reversed.', '{"0": "Inversion reverses a segment within a chromosome.", "1": "Deletion removes a segment.", "3": "Substitution is a point mutation."}'),
  ('BIO-0433', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Chromosomal mutations', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 147 (§19.8.2)', 'Insertional inactivation occurs when', '["a transposon (jumping gene) inserts into a gene and inactivates it", "two chromosomes fail to separate", "a gene is copied twice", "a stop codon is removed"]', 0, 'Transposons move at random; inserted into a gene (e.g. clotting factor IX), they can add a stop codon and inactivate it.', '{"1": "That is non-disjunction.", "2": "That is duplication.", "3": "Inactivation adds, not removes, a stop."}'),
  ('BIO-0434', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Chromosomal mutations', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 147 (§19.8.2)', 'The risk of non-disjunction is greater in eggs than in sperm because', '["sperms are made only once in life", "all eggs are arrested in prophase I from before birth, so they are as old as the mother", "eggs never undergo meiosis", "sperms have no sex chromosomes"]', 1, 'Men make new sperm daily, but a woman''s oocytes have waited in prophase I since before her birth.', '{"0": "Sperms are produced daily.", "2": "Eggs do undergo meiosis.", "3": "Sperms carry X or Y."}'),
  ('BIO-0435', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Chromosomal mutations', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 148 (§19.8.2)', 'Down syndrome is caused by non-disjunction of chromosome pair', '["13, giving 45 chromosomes", "21, giving 47 chromosomes", "X, giving 45 chromosomes", "18, giving 46 chromosomes"]', 1, 'Trisomy 21 (2n + 1 = 47) usually comes from an egg with 24 chromosomes.', '{"0": "Trisomy 13 is another condition, and trisomy gives 47.", "2": "Loss of an X gives Turner syndrome.", "3": "A trisomy gives 47, not 46."}'),
  ('BIO-0436', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Chromosomal mutations', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 148 (§19.8.2)', 'Klinefelter syndrome is caused by the sex chromosome complement', '["XO, in a female with 45 chromosomes", "XXX, in a female with 47 chromosomes", "XYY, in a female with 47 chromosomes", "XXY, in a male with 47 chromosomes"]', 3, 'XXY individuals are sterile males with some female body features.', '{"0": "XO is Turner syndrome.", "1": "XXX is triple X syndrome.", "2": "This is not Klinefelter syndrome."}'),
  ('BIO-0437', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Chromosomal mutations', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 149 (§19.8.2)', 'A zygote with OY sex chromosomes', '["fails to develop, because humans cannot survive without genes on the X", "develops into a Turner female", "develops into a Klinefelter male", "develops into a normal male"]', 0, 'An egg lacking an X fertilized by a Y sperm gives OY, which does not develop.', '{"1": "Turner syndrome is XO.", "2": "Klinefelter syndrome is XXY.", "3": "A normal male is XY."}'),
  ('BIO-0438', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Chromosomal mutations', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 149 (§19.8.2)', 'Short stature, webbed neck and immature sex organs in a sterile female with 45 chromosomes indicate', '["Klinefelter syndrome (XXY)", "Down syndrome (trisomy 21)", "Turner syndrome (XO)", "triple X syndrome (XXX)"]', 2, 'Turner syndrome is monosomy of the X; growth hormone and estrogen therapy are used.', '{"0": "Klinefelter individuals are males with 47 chromosomes.", "1": "Down syndrome gives 47 chromosomes.", "3": "Triple X females are fertile with 47 chromosomes."}'),
  ('BIO-0439', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Chromosomal mutations', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 148 (§19.8.2)', 'Triple X syndrome (XXX) differs from Turner syndrome in that affected females', '["have 45 chromosomes", "are always sterile", "are fertile and enter puberty at the normal age", "have a webbed neck"]', 2, 'XXX females have 47 chromosomes, may show learning difficulties and tall stature, but are fertile.', '{"0": "They have 47 chromosomes.", "1": "They are fertile.", "3": "A webbed neck is a feature of Turner syndrome."}'),
  ('BIO-0440', 'Biotechnology', 'Biotechnology', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 155 (chapter introduction)', 'Traditional biotechnology, used for thousands of years to make bread, cheese and beer, is based on', '["recombinant DNA technology", "polymerase chain reaction", "fermentation", "gene guns"]', 2, 'Fermentation is the old form of biotechnology; modern biotechnology arose in the 20th century after DNA''s structure was found.', '{"0": "Recombinant DNA is modern biotechnology.", "1": "PCR was developed in 1983.", "3": "Gene guns are a modern gene-transfer method."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-0441', 'Biotechnology', 'Genetic engineering', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 155 (§20.1)', 'Genetic engineering is best defined as', '["breeding two varieties of plant by cross-pollination", "altering an organism''s DNA to change its traits or capabilities", "using microbes to ferment food", "selecting the best animals for breeding"]', 1, 'It involves inserting, deleting or modifying genes, sometimes moving them between species.', '{"0": "Cross-pollination is traditional breeding, not direct DNA alteration.", "2": "Fermentation is traditional biotechnology.", "3": "Selective breeding does not directly alter DNA."}'),
  ('BIO-0442', 'Biotechnology', 'Tools', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 156 (§20.2.1)', 'Making a gene of interest by copying its mRNA into DNA requires the enzyme', '["restriction endonuclease", "DNA ligase", "RNA polymerase", "reverse transcriptase"]', 3, 'A gene of interest can be made by direct synthesis, by reverse transcription of its mRNA, or by cutting it out of a chromosome.', '{"0": "Restriction enzymes cut DNA out of chromosomes.", "1": "Ligase joins DNA fragments.", "2": "RNA polymerase makes RNA from DNA, the opposite direction."}'),
  ('BIO-0443', 'Biotechnology', 'Tools', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 156 (§20.2.1)', 'Restriction endonucleases are called "molecular scissors" because they', '["cut DNA at specific nucleotide sequences", "join two DNA fragments", "copy DNA many times", "carry genes into host cells"]', 0, 'Each kind recognizes a specific restriction site and cuts the DNA there.', '{"1": "Joining is done by DNA ligase (\"molecular glue\").", "2": "Copying is done by DNA polymerase in PCR.", "3": "Carrying genes is the role of vectors."}'),
  ('BIO-0444', 'Biotechnology', 'Tools', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 156 (§20.2.1)', 'Restriction sites are palindromic, which means that', '["the site is always one nucleotide long", "both strands read the same sequence in opposite directions at the site", "the site contains only A and T", "the site is found only in plasmids"]', 1, 'Palindromic sites of 4–8 nucleotides read the same on both strands, 5′ to 3′.', '{"0": "Sites are 4–8 nucleotides long.", "2": "Sites can contain any bases.", "3": "Restriction sites occur in all kinds of DNA."}'),
  ('BIO-0445', 'Biotechnology', 'Tools', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 156 (§20.2.1)', 'Sticky ends produced by a restriction endonuclease are', '["short single-stranded ends that are complementary to each other", "double-stranded blunt ends with no overhang", "ends covered with proteins", "ends that can never be joined again"]', 0, 'Because both strands are cut, the fragments have short complementary single-stranded overhangs.', '{"1": "Sticky ends have single-stranded overhangs.", "2": "They are bare DNA ends.", "3": "Fragments cut by the same enzyme can be joined."}'),
  ('BIO-0446', 'Biotechnology', 'Tools', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 156 (§20.2.1)', 'Restriction endonucleases are so useful in recombinant DNA work because', '["they work only on bacterial DNA", "they copy DNA without a template", "any two DNA fragments cut by the same enzyme can be joined together", "they make bacteria resistant to antibiotics"]', 2, 'Fragments cut by the same enzyme have compatible sticky ends.', '{"0": "They cut DNA from any source.", "1": "They cut, they do not copy.", "3": "Resistance comes from a plasmid gene."}'),
  ('BIO-0447', 'Biotechnology', 'Vectors', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 157 (§20.2.1)', 'The most commonly used vectors in recombinant DNA technology are', '["restriction enzymes", "ribosomes", "Okazaki fragments", "bacterial plasmids"]', 3, 'Vectors carry the gene of interest into host cells; plasmids are most common, with phages, cosmids, BACs and YACs also used.', '{"0": "Restriction enzymes cut DNA.", "1": "Ribosomes make proteins.", "2": "Okazaki fragments are pieces of lagging DNA."}'),
  ('BIO-0448', 'Biotechnology', 'Vectors', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 157 (§20.2.1)', 'A cosmid is a vector that is', '["an artificial chromosome made from yeast", "a human cold virus", "a heat-stable DNA polymerase", "a plasmid containing a small region of bacteriophage DNA called the cos sequence"]', 3, 'Cosmids combine plasmid features with the phage cos sequence.', '{"0": "That is a YAC.", "1": "Animal viruses such as the cold virus are another vector type.", "2": "That is Taq polymerase."}'),
  ('BIO-0449', 'Biotechnology', 'Tools', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 157 (§20.2.1)', 'DNA ligase is called "molecular glue" because it', '["cuts DNA at palindromic sites", "forms phosphodiester linkages between adjacent nucleotides of two DNA fragments", "separates DNA strands at 98 °C", "detects colonies with a radioactive probe"]', 1, 'Ligase joins the gene of interest to the cut vector, making recombinant DNA.', '{"0": "Cutting is done by restriction enzymes.", "2": "Heat separates strands in PCR.", "3": "Probes are used in hybridization."}'),
  ('BIO-0450', 'Biotechnology', 'Tools', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 157 (§20.2.1)', 'In recombinant DNA technology, the "expression system" is', '["a host organism that takes up the recombinant DNA and expresses the gene of interest", "the restriction enzyme used to cut DNA", "the antibiotic used to select bacteria", "the gel used to separate fragments"]', 0, 'Bacteria are ideal hosts because of their short generation time and simple genetic system.', '{"1": "That is a tool for cutting.", "2": "Antibiotics are used in screening.", "3": "This is not an expression system."}'),
  ('BIO-0451', 'Biotechnology', 'Steps', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 157 (§20.2.2)', 'To produce compatible sticky ends on both the gene of interest and the plasmid,', '["each is cut with a different ligase", "both are heated to 98 °C", "both are cut with the same restriction endonuclease", "only the plasmid is cut"]', 2, 'Using the same enzyme gives complementary sticky ends, which ligase then joins.', '{"0": "Ligase joins; it does not cut.", "1": "Heating separates DNA strands; it does not make sticky ends.", "3": "The gene of interest must also be cut out."}'),
  ('BIO-0452', 'Biotechnology', 'Screening', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 159 (§20.2.2)', 'When bacteria are grown on a medium containing ampicillin after transformation,', '["only bacteria that took up a plasmid with the resistance gene survive", "only bacteria without plasmids survive", "all bacteria turn blue", "all bacteria die"]', 0, 'The plasmid carries an antibiotic resistance gene, so plasmid-free cells are killed.', '{"1": "Cells without plasmids lack resistance.", "2": "Colour comes from blue–white screening.", "3": "Cells with the plasmid survive."}'),
  ('BIO-0453', 'Biotechnology', 'Screening', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 159 (§20.2.2)', 'In blue–white screening, a white bacterial colony indicates that', '["the plasmid has no insert", "the bacteria have no plasmid", "the bacteria are resistant to all antibiotics", "the gene of interest has been inserted, breaking the lacZ′ gene"]', 3, 'A plain plasmid keeps lacZ′ working and gives blue colonies; an insert disrupts lacZ′, so recombinant colonies are white.', '{"0": "Plasmids without inserts give blue colonies.", "1": "Bacteria without plasmids die on the antibiotic medium.", "2": "Colour is not a measure of resistance."}'),
  ('BIO-0454', 'Biotechnology', 'Screening', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 159 (§20.2.2)', 'In DNA hybridization screening, the colonies with the gene of interest are found using', '["a blue dye made by lacZ′", "a radioactive DNA probe complementary to the gene, located by autoradiography", "Taq polymerase", "an electrical current to make pores"]', 1, 'The probe hybridizes only to colonies containing the gene, and photographic film locates them on the master plate.', '{"0": "The blue colour distinguishes plain from recombinant plasmids.", "2": "Taq polymerase is used in PCR.", "3": "Electrical pores are used in electroporation."}'),
  ('BIO-0455', 'Biotechnology', 'PCR', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 160 (§20.3)', 'The polymerase chain reaction (PCR) was developed in 1983 by', '["Har Gobind Khorana", "Marshall Nirenberg", "Kary Mullis", "Thomas Hunt Morgan"]', 2, 'Kary Mullis developed PCR and won the 1993 Nobel Prize in Chemistry.', '{"0": "Khorana worked on the genetic code.", "1": "Nirenberg worked on the genetic code.", "3": "Morgan worked on Drosophila genetics."}'),
  ('BIO-0456', 'Biotechnology', 'PCR', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 160 (§20.3.1)', 'In the first step of PCR, the DNA is heated to about 98 °C in order to', '["activate the primers", "join the new strands together", "destroy the Taq polymerase", "separate the double-stranded DNA into single strands"]', 3, 'Denaturation makes each strand a template for in vitro synthesis.', '{"0": "Primers anneal on cooling, around 60 °C.", "1": "Strands are not joined at this step.", "2": "Taq polymerase survives heating."}'),
  ('BIO-0457', 'Biotechnology', 'PCR', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 160 (§20.3.1)', 'During the annealing step of PCR, the mixture is cooled to about 60 °C so that', '["primers bind to complementary regions of the single-stranded DNA", "the DNA strands separate", "Taq polymerase adds nucleotides", "the DNA is cut into fragments"]', 0, 'Forward and backward primers hybridize to their complementary sites as the solution cools.', '{"1": "Strands separate at about 98 °C.", "2": "Extension happens at about 72 °C.", "3": "No cutting takes place in PCR."}'),
  ('BIO-0458', 'Biotechnology', 'PCR', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 160 (§20.3.1)', 'Primers used in PCR are about', '["2 to 3 nucleotides long", "20 to 30 nucleotides long", "200 to 300 nucleotides long", "2000 to 3000 nucleotides long"]', 1, 'An excess of primers 20–30 nucleotides long is mixed with the DNA.', '{"0": "Too short to bind specifically.", "2": "Much longer than the book states.", "3": "Far too long."}'),
  ('BIO-0459', 'Biotechnology', 'PCR', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 161 (§20.3.1)', 'Taq polymerase is used in PCR because it', '["works only at 37 °C", "cuts DNA at restriction sites", "is heat-stable, coming from Thermus aquaticus of hot springs", "needs to be added fresh after every cycle"]', 2, 'Because heating does not destroy it, no more enzyme is needed after each cycle.', '{"0": "It works at high temperatures; extension is at about 72 °C.", "1": "It copies DNA; it does not cut it.", "3": "It survives the heating steps."}'),
  ('BIO-0460', 'Biotechnology', 'PCR', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 161 (§20.3.1)', 'Each PCR cycle doubles the DNA. Starting from one fragment, how many copies are there after 20 cycles?', '["20", "More than one million (2²⁰)", "400", "About one thousand"]', 1, '2²⁰ = 1,048,576, so one fragment gives over a million copies.', '{"0": "Copies double each cycle; they do not just add one.", "2": "20² is not the doubling result.", "3": "About 1000 copies come after only 10 cycles."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-0461', 'Biotechnology', 'PCR', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 162 (§20.3.1)', 'In criminal investigations, PCR is used to', '["produce insulin for diabetics", "transfer genes into plant cells with a gene gun", "make DNA fingerprints from tiny samples such as dried blood or a hair root", "make bacteria resistant to ampicillin"]', 2, 'PCR amplifies tiny amounts of DNA for fingerprinting, diagnosis, embryo testing and study of extinct species.', '{"0": "Insulin is made by transgenic bacteria.", "1": "That is biolistic transformation.", "3": "That is a plasmid resistance gene."}'),
  ('BIO-0462', 'Biotechnology', 'GMOs', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 162 (§20.4)', 'Transgenic organisms are a subset of GMOs that', '["have received DNA from a different, unrelated species", "have had no change in their genome", "are produced only by natural mating", "contain only their own genes in new places"]', 0, 'A GMO is any organism with a changed genome; a transgenic one carries DNA from another species.', '{"1": "A GMO has a changed genome.", "2": "Transgenics are made by gene transfer.", "3": "Transgenics carry foreign genes."}'),
  ('BIO-0463', 'Biotechnology', 'Gene transfer', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 162 (§20.4)', 'In electroporation, genes enter a target cell through', '["a fine needle into the nucleus", "heavy-metal particles fired by a gene gun", "a virus that infects the cell", "temporary pores made by an electrical current"]', 3, 'An electric pulse opens pores in the cell wall or membrane; they close when the current stops.', '{"0": "That is microinjection.", "1": "That is biolistic transformation.", "2": "That is virus-mediated gene transfer."}'),
  ('BIO-0464', 'Biotechnology', 'Gene transfer', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 162 (§20.4)', 'Coating the gene of interest with heavy metal and firing it into cells with a gene gun is called', '["electroporation", "microinjection", "biolistic transformation", "protoplast-mediated transfer"]', 2, 'Biolistic transformation uses mechanical force to drive the coated DNA into cells.', '{"0": "Electroporation uses electric pores.", "1": "Microinjection uses a fine needle.", "3": "Protoplast transfer removes the cell wall first."}'),
  ('BIO-0465', 'Biotechnology', 'Transgenic bacteria', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 163 (§20.4.1)', 'Which product made by transgenic bacteria is used to dissolve blood clots?', '["Erythropoietin", "Tissue plasminogen activator", "Interferon", "Chymosin"]', 1, 'Transgenic bacteria make insulin, clotting factors, vaccines, growth hormone, interferon, erythropoietin and tissue plasminogen activator.', '{"0": "Erythropoietin treats anaemia.", "2": "Interferon is used against some cancers.", "3": "Chymosin clots milk protein for cheese."}'),
  ('BIO-0466', 'Biotechnology', 'Transgenic bacteria', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 163 (§20.4.1)', 'Chymosin produced by transgenic bacteria is used in', '["cheese making, because it clots milk protein", "fruit juice clarification", "converting starch to sugars", "treating haemophilia"]', 0, 'Food enzymes from transgenic bacteria include alpha-amylase, chymosin and pectin-esterase.', '{"1": "Pectin-esterase improves juice clarity.", "2": "Alpha-amylase converts starch to sugars.", "3": "Clotting factors treat haemophilia."}'),
  ('BIO-0467', 'Biotechnology', 'Transgenic bacteria', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 163 (§20.4.1)', 'Besides making medicines and food enzymes, transgenic bacteria are used to', '["produce spider silk proteins in milk", "kill insect pests inside cotton plants", "make milk like human breast milk", "leach copper from ore and clean up mercury pollution"]', 3, 'They can degrade toxins, leach copper, clean up mercury and detect arsenic in drinking water.', '{"0": "Silk proteins are made by transgenic goats.", "1": "Pest-killing proteins come from Bt genes in transgenic plants.", "2": "This is done by transgenic cows."}'),
  ('BIO-0468', 'Biotechnology', 'Transgenic animals', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 163 (§20.4.2)', 'Transgenic goats have been made to produce', '["human insulin in their blood", "strong spider-web-like silk proteins in their milk", "Bt toxin in their skin", "chymosin in their saliva"]', 1, 'Transgenic goats produce spider silk proteins and the human enzyme lysozyme in their milk.', '{"0": "Insulin is made in bacteria.", "2": "Bt toxin genes are put into plants.", "3": "Chymosin is made by bacteria."}'),
  ('BIO-0469', 'Biotechnology', 'Transgenic animals', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 163 (§20.4.2)', 'Most research on inserting genes into humans has focused on somatic cells rather than germline cells because', '["somatic cells have no DNA", "germline cells cannot take up genes", "germline changes are inheritable and carry risks and ethical issues", "somatic changes pass to all offspring"]', 2, 'Genes put into germline cells pass to future generations, raising risk and ethical concerns.', '{"0": "Somatic cells do contain DNA.", "1": "Germline cells can take up genes in theory.", "3": "Somatic changes are not inherited."}'),
  ('BIO-0470', 'Biotechnology', 'Transgenic animals', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 164 (§20.4.2)', 'Malaria-resistant mosquitoes have been developed in the laboratory by', '["removing all their genes for blood feeding", "feeding them Bt toxin", "giving them human lysozyme", "inserting a gene that reduces the development of Plasmodium in them"]', 3, 'The inserted gene limits Plasmodium development inside the mosquito.', '{"0": "This is not how they were made.", "1": "Bt genes are used in plants against insect pests.", "2": "Lysozyme is produced by transgenic goats."}'),
  ('BIO-0471', 'Biotechnology', 'Transgenic plants', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 164 (§20.4.3)', 'Transgenic plants can be grown from a single engineered cell because many plant cells are', '["totipotent", "haploid", "without cell walls", "unable to divide"]', 0, 'A totipotent cell can form a whole new plant.', '{"1": "Being haploid is not the reason given.", "2": "Plant cells have walls.", "3": "They must divide to form a plant."}'),
  ('BIO-0472', 'Biotechnology', 'Transgenic plants', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 164 (§20.4.3)', 'Genes from Bacillus thuringiensis are inserted into crops such as cotton to', '["increase iron content in the grain", "make the plants resistant to glyphosate", "make proteins that kill insect pests but not other useful insects", "produce human insulin"]', 2, 'Bt genes code for insecticidal proteins; crops like cotton, maize and brinjal carry them.', '{"0": "Iron genes came from beans, Aspergillus and wild rice.", "1": "Glyphosate resistance is a different modification.", "3": "Insulin is made in bacteria."}'),
  ('BIO-0473', 'Biotechnology', 'Transgenic plants', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 165 (§20.4.3)', 'In transgenic rice, the genes for making beta-carotene (a precursor of vitamin A) were taken from', '["daffodil", "beans", "Aspergillus fungus", "Bacillus thuringiensis"]', 0, 'Beta-carotene genes came from daffodil; iron genes came from beans, Aspergillus and wild rice.', '{"1": "Beans were a source of iron genes.", "2": "Aspergillus was a source of iron genes.", "3": "Bt genes give insect resistance."}'),
  ('BIO-0474', 'Biotechnology', 'Insulin', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 165 (§20.5)', 'In making human insulin with bacteria, scientists', '["put the whole pancreas gene into one yeast cell", "make chains A and B in separate E. coli cultures and join them by disulphide bonds", "extract insulin directly from transgenic goats'' milk", "make insulin by PCR without any living cells"]', 1, 'Each chain''s DNA goes into its own set of plasmids and bacterial culture; the purified chains are linked by S–S bridges.', '{"0": "The book describes two separate genes in E. coli.", "2": "Goats are used for other products.", "3": "PCR copies DNA; it does not make protein."}'),
  ('BIO-0475', 'Biotechnology', 'Insulin', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 165 (§20.5)', 'Why is each insulin chain gene often placed next to a bacterial gene such as lacZ in the plasmid?', '["To make the insulin chains blue", "To join chain A to chain B inside the bacterium", "To kill bacteria that lack the plasmid", "To help the bacteria recognize and express the foreign DNA"]', 3, 'Attaching the insulin gene to a bacterial gene helps the bacterium read and express it.', '{"0": "The book gives expression, not colour, as the reason here.", "1": "The chains are joined chemically after extraction.", "2": "Antibiotic resistance genes are used for that."}'),
  ('BIO-0476', 'Biotechnology', 'Insulin', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 165 (§20.5)', 'The two insulin chains produced by bacteria are linked into functional insulin by', '["peptide bonds between their ends", "hydrogen bonds only", "disulphide (sulphur–sulphur) bonds", "phosphodiester bonds"]', 2, 'After purification, chains A and B are mixed and joined by disulphide bridges between specific amino acids.', '{"0": "They are not joined end to end.", "1": "Disulphide bonds join the chains.", "3": "Phosphodiester bonds link nucleotides, not amino acids."}'),
  ('BIO-0477', 'Immunity', 'First line of defence', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 170 (§21.1)', 'The first line of defence against infection consists of', '["macrophages, neutrophils and natural killer cells", "complement proteins and interferons", "T cells and B cells", "skin, the digestive tract and the respiratory tract"]', 3, 'The first line keeps microbes out at the body surfaces: skin, digestive tract and respiratory tract.', '{"0": "These germ-killer cells belong to the second line of defence.", "1": "Protective proteins belong to the second line of defence.", "2": "Lymphocytes make up the third (specific) line of defence."}'),
  ('BIO-0478', 'Immunity', 'First line of defence', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 170 (§21.1.1)', 'The outer layer of the epidermis, whose cells are continuously shed and replaced, is called the', '["stratum corneum", "dermis", "hypodermis", "basement membrane"]', 0, 'Cells of the stratum corneum are continually damaged and shed; new cells form deeper in the epidermis and move upward.', '{"1": "The dermis lies below the epidermis.", "2": "The hypodermis is the layer beneath the dermis.", "3": "The basement membrane lies under the epithelium; it is not its outer layer."}'),
  ('BIO-0479', 'Immunity', 'First line of defence', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 170 (§21.1.1)', 'Skin is tough and impenetrable to microbes mainly because its epidermal cells form the protein', '["collagen", "lysozyme", "keratin", "perforin"]', 2, 'Cells migrating up through the epidermis form keratin, which makes the skin tough.', '{"0": "Collagen is a connective-tissue fibre protein, not the epidermal barrier protein.", "1": "Lysozyme is an enzyme in sweat, not a structural protein.", "3": "Perforins are made by natural killer cells."}'),
  ('BIO-0480', 'Immunity', 'First line of defence', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 170 (§21.1.1)', 'The human epidermis is about how many cells thick?', '["1 to 3", "10 to 30", "100 to 300", "500 to 1000"]', 1, 'The book gives the epidermis a thickness of 10 to 30 cells.', '{"0": "Too thin; that would be a simple epithelium.", "2": "Too thick for the epidermis.", "3": "Far too thick."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-0481', 'Immunity', 'First line of defence', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 171 (§21.1.1)', 'Oil and sweat glands help the skin resist microbes by lowering the surface pH to about', '["3 to 5", "7 to 8", "8 to 10", "1 to 2"]', 0, 'A surface pH of 3–5 is acidic enough to inhibit the growth of many microbes.', '{"1": "pH 7–8 is neutral to slightly alkaline.", "2": "pH 8–10 is alkaline.", "3": "pH 1–2 is the level of stomach acid, not skin."}'),
  ('BIO-0482', 'Immunity', 'First line of defence', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 171 (§21.1.1)', 'The enzyme found in both sweat and saliva that digests the cell walls of many bacteria is', '["amylase", "lysozyme", "pepsin", "lipase"]', 1, 'Lysozyme attacks bacterial cell walls; it occurs in sweat and in saliva.', '{"0": "Salivary amylase digests starch, not bacterial walls.", "2": "Pepsin is a stomach protease.", "3": "Lipase digests fats."}'),
  ('BIO-0483', 'Immunity', 'First line of defence', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 171 (§21.1.2)', 'In the respiratory tract, microbes trapped in mucus of the bronchi and bronchioles are removed when', '["macrophages carry the mucus into the lungs", "the mucus is absorbed into the blood", "alveoli secrete acid that dissolves it", "cilia sweep the mucus upward so it can be swallowed"]', 3, 'Cilia continually sweep the mucus upward, away from the warm, moist lungs, where it is swallowed.', '{"0": "The aim is to keep microbes out of the lungs, not carry them in.", "1": "Mucus is not absorbed into blood.", "2": "Alveoli do not secrete acid."}'),
  ('BIO-0484', 'Immunity', 'First line of defence', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 171 (§21.1.2)', 'Most bacteria swallowed with food are killed in the stomach by', '["bile salts", "interferons", "strong digestive acid", "antibodies made by plasma cells"]', 2, 'The strong acid of the stomach (with intestinal proteases) kills bacteria in food.', '{"0": "Bile is released into the small intestine and emulsifies fat.", "1": "Interferons act against viruses inside the body.", "3": "Antibodies are third-line defence, not stomach defence."}'),
  ('BIO-0485', 'Immunity', 'Second line of defence', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 172 (§21.2)', 'Which statement about the second line of defence is correct?', '["Like the first line, it is non-specific", "It targets one particular microbe only", "It is carried out mainly by B cells", "It develops only after vaccination"]', 0, 'Both first and second lines are non-specific; specificity belongs to the third line.', '{"1": "Targeting a particular microbe is specific (third-line) defence.", "2": "B cells are part of the specific immune system.", "3": "The non-specific defences are present at birth."}'),
  ('BIO-0486', 'Immunity', 'Second line of defence', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 172 (§21.2)', 'Which is NOT one of the four main components of the second line of defence?', '["germ-killer cells of blood", "plasma cells secreting antibodies", "the inflammatory response", "the temperature response"]', 1, 'The four are germ-killer cells, antimicrobial proteins, inflammation and the temperature response.', '{"0": "Germ-killer cells are one of the four.", "2": "Inflammation is one of the four.", "3": "Fever is one of the four."}'),
  ('BIO-0487', 'Immunity', 'Germ-killer cells', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 172 (§21.2.1)', 'Macrophages develop from', '["neutrophils", "platelets", "monocytes", "plasma cells"]', 2, 'Monocytes, a type of white blood cell, give rise to macrophages.', '{"0": "Neutrophils are a separate type of phagocyte.", "1": "Platelets are cell fragments for clotting.", "3": "Plasma cells come from B cells and make antibodies."}'),
  ('BIO-0488', 'Immunity', 'Germ-killer cells', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 172 (§21.2.1)', 'A macrophage kills a bacterium it has ingested by endocytosis using', '["perforins", "a network of DNA fibres", "membrane-attack complexes", "lysozyme enzymes"]', 3, 'After endocytosis, the macrophage digests the bacterium with lysozyme enzymes.', '{"0": "Perforins are used by natural killer and cytotoxic T cells.", "1": "DNA fibre networks are a neutrophil method.", "2": "MACs are formed by complement proteins."}'),
  ('BIO-0489', 'Immunity', 'Germ-killer cells', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 172 (§21.2.1)', 'Fixed macrophages are found within organs such as the', '["kidneys, bladder and ureters", "lungs, liver, spleen and brain", "stomach, oesophagus and colon", "skin, hair and nails"]', 1, 'Some macrophages are fixed in the lungs, liver, spleen and brain; most patrol blood, lymph and interstitial fluid.', '{"0": "The book does not list urinary organs.", "2": "The book does not list gut organs.", "3": "These are not the listed sites."}'),
  ('BIO-0490', 'Immunity', 'Germ-killer cells', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 172 (§21.2.1)', 'Which method of killing pathogens is used by neutrophils but not by macrophages, as described in the book?', '["ingesting bacteria by phagocytosis", "being derived from monocytes", "patrolling the blood", "releasing a network of DNA fibres that binds and kills bacteria"]', 3, 'Neutrophils use phagocytosis, soluble antimicrobial chemicals, and released DNA-fibre networks.', '{"0": "Both cell types are phagocytic.", "1": "Macrophages, not neutrophils, come from monocytes.", "2": "Both patrol the blood."}'),
  ('BIO-0491', 'Immunity', 'Germ-killer cells', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 173 (§21.2.1)', 'Natural killer cells destroy virus-infected body cells by', '["inserting perforins that form pores so the cell swells and bursts", "engulfing the whole cell by phagocytosis", "secreting antibodies against it", "releasing histamine around it"]', 0, 'Perforins make a pore in the target membrane; water rushes in and the cell bursts.', '{"1": "NK cells do not phagocytose their targets.", "2": "Antibodies are made by plasma cells.", "3": "Histamine is released by mast cells and injured cells."}'),
  ('BIO-0492', 'Immunity', 'Germ-killer cells', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 173 (§21.2.1)', 'Besides virus-infected cells, natural killer cells can also detect and kill', '["red blood cells that are aging", "free bacteria in the plasma", "cancer cells before they form a tumour", "pollen grains in the nose"]', 2, 'NK cells kill cancer cells before they can develop into a tumour; they do not kill invading microbes themselves.', '{"0": "Old red cells are removed by the spleen and liver, not NK cells.", "1": "The book says NK cells do not kill invading microbes.", "3": "Pollen is an allergen dealt with differently."}'),
  ('BIO-0493', 'Immunity', 'Germ-killer cells', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 173 (§21.2.1)', 'Patrolling germ-killer cells do not attack the body''s own normal cells because these cells carry', '["a layer of keratin", "interferon receptors only", "self-proteins on their surface", "a cell wall"]', 2, 'Self-proteins mark normal cells; germ-killer cells attack only cells lacking them.', '{"0": "Keratin is in skin cells only.", "1": "Interferon receptors do not mark self.", "3": "Human cells have no cell wall."}'),
  ('BIO-0494', 'Immunity', 'Protective proteins', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 173 (§21.2.2)', 'Proteins of the complement system are produced in the', '["liver", "thymus", "bone marrow", "lymph nodes"]', 0, 'More than a dozen complement proteins are made in the liver and circulate inactive.', '{"1": "The thymus matures T cells.", "2": "Bone marrow makes blood cells.", "3": "Lymph nodes house lymphocytes."}'),
  ('BIO-0495', 'Immunity', 'Protective proteins', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 173 (§21.2.2)', 'In the complement system, the membrane-attack complex is formed by', '["the first protein to be activated", "the final five proteins of the activation sequence", "antibodies of the E type", "interferons released by infected cells"]', 1, 'Proteins activate one another in sequence; the last five form the MAC that punctures the microbe''s membrane.', '{"0": "The first protein only starts the cascade.", "2": "E-antibodies are involved in allergy.", "3": "Interferons are a separate protective protein group."}'),
  ('BIO-0496', 'Immunity', 'Protective proteins', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 173 (§21.2.2)', 'A microbe attacked by the membrane-attack complex dies because', '["its DNA is cut by the complex", "it is engulfed by the complement proteins", "its ribosomes are blocked", "fluids and salts enter through the puncture and it swells and bursts"]', 3, 'The MAC punctures the plasma membrane; water and salts move in and the microbe bursts.', '{"0": "The MAC acts on the membrane, not DNA.", "1": "Complement proteins do not engulf anything.", "2": "The MAC does not act on ribosomes."}'),
  ('BIO-0497', 'Immunity', 'Protective proteins', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 173 (§21.2.2)', 'Interferons are released by', '["mast cells in connective tissue", "virus-infected cells", "liver cells into the blood", "plasma cells in lymph nodes"]', 1, 'Infected cells release interferons that make nearby cells strengthen their antiviral defences.', '{"0": "Mast cells release histamine.", "2": "The liver makes complement proteins.", "3": "Plasma cells secrete antibodies."}'),
  ('BIO-0498', 'Immunity', 'Protective proteins', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 174 (§21.2.2)', 'Interferons belong to a large class of cell-signalling proteins called', '["cytokines", "immunoglobulins", "perforins", "pyrogens"]', 0, 'Cytokines carry signals between cells to trigger immune defences; interferons are cytokines.', '{"1": "Immunoglobulins are antibodies.", "2": "Perforins are pore-forming proteins of NK cells.", "3": "Pyrogens are fever-causing chemicals from macrophages."}'),
  ('BIO-0499', 'Immunity', 'Inflammatory response', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 174 (§21.2.3)', 'Besides making the site unfavourable for microbial growth, the local heat of inflammation', '["stops blood flow to the injured area", "destroys the self-proteins of nearby cells", "promotes healing and raises the mobility of white blood cells", "converts neutrophils into plasma cells"]', 2, 'Heat at the inflamed site hinders microbes, promotes healing and makes white blood cells more mobile.', '{"0": "Blood flow to the site increases in inflammation.", "1": "Self-proteins are not destroyed by heat.", "3": "Plasma cells come from B cells, not neutrophils."}'),
  ('BIO-0500', 'Immunity', 'Inflammatory response', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 174 (§21.2.3)', 'The redness, swelling and warmth of inflammation are caused by', '["constriction of local arterioles", "clotting that blocks capillaries", "shedding of the stratum corneum", "local dilation of blood vessels and more permeable capillaries"]', 3, 'Increased capillary blood flow and leakiness give redness, swelling and warmth.', '{"0": "Constriction would reduce blood flow.", "1": "Clotting is not what produces the signs.", "2": "Shedding of skin cells is a first-line process."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-0501', 'Immunity', 'Inflammatory response', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 174 (§21.2.3)', 'Pus formed at some infection sites is mainly a mixture of', '["excess antibodies and complement proteins", "dead neutrophils, broken tissue cells and dead pathogens", "red blood cells and platelets", "mucus from the bronchioles"]', 1, 'Pus is dead or dying neutrophils, broken-down tissue cells and dead pathogens.', '{"0": "Pus is cellular debris, not mainly free proteins.", "2": "Red cells and platelets are not its main content.", "3": "Bronchial mucus is a first-line secretion."}'),
  ('BIO-0502', 'Immunity', 'Inflammatory response', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 174 (§21.2.3)', 'Arthritis is given in the book as an example of', '["inflammation without infection, a misdirected immune response", "a successful first-line defence", "passive acquired immunity", "a fever caused by pyrogens"]', 0, 'In arthritis, inflammation occurs without infection or injury.', '{"1": "Arthritis is a disorder, not a defence.", "2": "Passive immunity means receiving antibodies.", "3": "Arthritis is not described as fever."}'),
  ('BIO-0503', 'Immunity', 'Temperature response', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 175 (§21.2.4)', 'Fever begins when pyrogens released by macrophages act on the', '["medulla oblongata", "cerebellum", "thymus", "hypothalamus"]', 3, 'The hypothalamus is the body''s thermostat; pyrogens make it raise core temperature above 37 °C.', '{"0": "The medulla controls breathing and heartbeat.", "1": "The cerebellum coordinates movement.", "2": "The thymus matures T cells."}'),
  ('BIO-0504', 'Immunity', 'Temperature response', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 175 (§21.2.4)', 'According to the book, body temperatures above about which value are often fatal?', '["37 °C (98.6 °F)", "38 °C (100.4 °F)", "40.5 °C (105 °F)", "39.4 °C (103 °F)"]', 2, 'Above 39.4 °C is dangerous; above 40.5 °C is often fatal.', '{"0": "37 °C is normal.", "1": "38 °C is a mild fever.", "3": "39.4 °C is the level called dangerous, not often fatal."}'),
  ('BIO-0505', 'Immunity', 'Temperature response', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 175 (§21.2.4)', 'Which is a way that fever helps the body fight infection?', '["it speeds up the actions of neutrophils and macrophages", "it makes bacteria reproduce faster so they exhaust nutrients", "it shuts down interferon release", "it stops antibody production"]', 0, 'Fever hinders microbial reproduction, kills temperature-sensitive microbes, speeds phagocytes and raises cytokine release.', '{"1": "Fever hinders microbial reproduction.", "2": "More interferons are released during fever.", "3": "Fever does not stop antibody production."}'),
  ('BIO-0506', 'Immunity', 'Third line of defence', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 175 (§21.3)', 'The third line of defence differs from the first two because it', '["is present at birth in full", "consists only of skin and mucus", "acts equally against all microbes", "targets specific microbes and remembers them"]', 3, 'The immune system is specific and can respond faster on re-exposure.', '{"0": "Inborn defence is non-specific.", "1": "Skin and mucus are first-line.", "2": "Equal action against all microbes is non-specific."}'),
  ('BIO-0507', 'Immunity', 'Types of immunity', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 175 (§21.3)', 'A person who recovers from measles and becomes immune has', '["artificial active immunity", "natural passive immunity", "natural active immunity", "innate immunity"]', 2, 'The body made its own antibodies after natural exposure; the book calls this the most effective form.', '{"0": "Artificial active immunity comes from vaccination.", "1": "Passive immunity means receiving ready-made antibodies.", "3": "Innate immunity is non-specific and present at birth."}'),
  ('BIO-0508', 'Immunity', 'Types of immunity', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 175 (§21.3)', 'Passive immunity is short-lived because', '["it depends on memory B cells", "the person is given antibodies but does not make them", "it is produced by vaccination", "it involves the body''s own T cells"]', 1, 'In passive immunity ready-made antibodies are supplied; no memory cells form.', '{"0": "Memory cells belong to active immunity.", "2": "Vaccination produces active immunity.", "3": "Passive immunity does not use the body''s own lymphocytes."}'),
  ('BIO-0509', 'Immunity', 'Components of immune system', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 176 (§21.3.1)', 'T cells mature and learn to recognize antigens in the', '["bone marrow, where they stay throughout", "spleen, below the diaphragm", "liver, along with complement proteins", "thymus, a small gland just above the heart"]', 3, 'T cells arise from bone-marrow stem cells but migrate to the thymus to mature.', '{"0": "B cells, not T cells, complete maturation in the bone marrow.", "1": "The spleen is not where T cells mature.", "2": "The liver is not where T cells mature."}'),
  ('BIO-0510', 'Immunity', 'Components of immune system', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 176 (§21.3.1)', 'Which kind of T cell supervises the development of T cells in the thymus?', '["inducer T cells", "helper T cells", "cytotoxic T cells", "suppressor T cells"]', 0, 'The four kinds: helper (initiate), cytotoxic (kill infected cells), inducer (supervise development), suppressor (terminate).', '{"1": "Helper T cells initiate the immune response.", "2": "Cytotoxic T cells break infected cells.", "3": "Suppressor T cells end the response."}'),
  ('BIO-0511', 'Immunity', 'Components of immune system', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 177 (§21.3.1)', 'When a B cell meets its antigen, it divides to form', '["macrophages that engulf the antigen", "plasma cells that secrete antibodies", "inducer T cells", "mast cells that release histamine"]', 1, 'Each plasma cell produces an antibody that sticks to the antigen and marks it for destruction.', '{"0": "Macrophages come from monocytes.", "2": "Inducer T cells are a T-cell type.", "3": "Mast cells are not made from B cells."}'),
  ('BIO-0512', 'Immunity', 'Cell-mediated immunity', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 177 (§21.3.2)', 'Both cell-mediated and antibody-mediated responses are started by', '["suppressor T cells", "memory B cells", "activated helper T cells", "natural killer cells"]', 2, 'A macrophage that engulfs a microbe makes proteins that activate helper T cells, which start both responses.', '{"0": "Suppressor T cells end responses.", "1": "Memory B cells act on later exposure.", "3": "NK cells are non-specific defence."}'),
  ('BIO-0513', 'Immunity', 'Cell-mediated immunity', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 177 (§21.3.2)', 'In cell-mediated immunity, T cell growth factor released by activated helper T cells causes', '["proliferation of large clones of T cells", "attraction of macrophages to the site", "shutdown of the cytotoxic response", "secretion of antibodies"]', 0, 'The proliferation step: T cell growth factor makes all types of T cells multiply.', '{"1": "Attracting macrophages is the activation step, by a second factor.", "2": "Shutdown is the suppression step.", "3": "Antibodies are made by plasma cells."}'),
  ('BIO-0514', 'Immunity', 'Cell-mediated immunity', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 177 (§21.3.2)', 'Suppressor T cells cannot stop the cytotoxic attack at first because', '["they are made only in the liver", "they cannot recognize antigens", "they multiply more slowly and start in low numbers", "they are destroyed by perforins"]', 2, 'Their numbers rise enough to shut the response down only after 1 to 2 weeks.', '{"0": "T cells are not made in the liver.", "1": "This is not the reason the book gives.", "3": "Perforins are not described as destroying them."}'),
  ('BIO-0515', 'Immunity', 'Antibody-mediated immunity', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 178 (§21.3.2)', 'Antibodies make up about what proportion, by weight, of the total protein in blood plasma?', '["2%", "50%", "80%", "20%"]', 3, 'The book states antibodies are about 20% by weight of plasma protein.', '{"0": "Too low.", "1": "Too high; albumin is the major plasma protein.", "2": "Far too high."}'),
  ('BIO-0516', 'Immunity', 'Antibody-mediated immunity', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 179 (§21.3.2)', 'Which is NOT one of the three ways antibodies mark an antigen for destruction?', '["activating complement proteins", "antibodies digest the antigen directly with enzymes", "being recognized by macrophages", "being recognized by killer cells"]', 1, 'Antibodies do not destroy antigens themselves; they mark them for complement, macrophages or killer cells.', '{"0": "Complement activation is one mechanism.", "2": "Macrophage ingestion is one mechanism.", "3": "Killer cells with antibody receptors are one mechanism."}'),
  ('BIO-0517', 'Immunity', 'Antibody-mediated immunity', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 179 (§21.3.2)', 'A rapid secondary response on a later meeting with the same antigen is due to', '["memory B cells that persist from the plasma-cell clone", "complement proteins made in the liver", "interferons from infected cells", "histamine from mast cells"]', 0, 'Some members of the clone persist as memory B cells instead of making antibodies.', '{"1": "Complement is non-specific and has no memory.", "2": "Interferons are non-specific.", "3": "Histamine drives inflammation and allergy."}'),
  ('BIO-0518', 'Immunity', 'Antibody structure', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 181 (§21.3.2, Structure of antibody)', 'An antibody molecule is made of', '["one heavy and three light chains joined by hydrogen bonds", "four identical chains joined by peptide bonds", "two heavy and two light polypeptide chains joined by disulphide bridges", "two heavy chains only, folded into a Y"]', 2, 'The Y-shaped antibody has two identical heavy and two light chains held by disulphide bridges.', '{"0": "Wrong numbers and wrong bonds.", "1": "The chains are not all identical.", "3": "Light chains are also present."}'),
  ('BIO-0519', 'Immunity', 'Antibody structure', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 181 (§21.3.2, Structure of antibody)', 'The specific antigen-binding site on each arm of an antibody is formed by', '["the constant regions of both heavy chains", "the variable regions of one heavy and one light chain", "the disulphide bridges at the hinge", "the stem of the Y"]', 1, 'Variable regions differ between antibodies, which is why each antibody is specific.', '{"0": "Constant regions are similar within a class and do not bind antigen.", "2": "Disulphide bridges hold chains together.", "3": "The stem is made of constant regions."}'),
  ('BIO-0520', 'Immunity', 'Monoclonal antibodies', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 181 (§21.3.2, Monoclonal antibodies)', 'The small portion of an antigen that fits into an antibody''s binding site is called the', '["hybridoma", "self-protein", "constant region", "determinant"]', 3, 'Only the determinant fits the binding site; monoclonal antibodies attach to just one determinant.', '{"0": "A hybridoma is a fused cell.", "1": "Self-proteins mark body cells.", "2": "The constant region is part of the antibody."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-0521', 'Immunity', 'Monoclonal antibodies', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 181 (§21.3.2, Monoclonal antibodies)', 'Hybridoma cells used to make monoclonal antibodies are formed by fusing', '["T cells with macrophages", "antibody-producing plasma cells with myeloma cancer cells", "neutrophils with red blood cells", "mast cells with B cells"]', 1, 'The hybridoma uses plasma-cell genes to make antibody and myeloma genes to keep dividing.', '{"0": "T cells and macrophages do not make antibodies.", "2": "Neither makes antibodies.", "3": "This is not the Milstein–Köhler method."}'),
  ('BIO-0522', 'Immunity', 'Monoclonal antibodies', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 182 (§21.3.2, Monoclonal antibodies)', 'A use of monoclonal antibodies mentioned in the book is', '["replacing skin in burn patients", "producing insulin in bacteria", "purifying a specific molecule such as interferon from a complex mixture", "raising body temperature during infection"]', 2, 'They bind one determinant, so they can pull out one molecule; interferon was purified this way.', '{"0": "Not described as a use.", "1": "Insulin is made by recombinant DNA, not monoclonals.", "3": "Fever is caused by pyrogens."}'),
  ('BIO-0523', 'Immunity', 'Gene therapy', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 179 (§21.3.2, Immune system and gene therapy)', 'In the proposed gene therapy for malignant melanoma, tumour-infiltrating lymphocytes are given a gene to make', '["tumour necrosis factor", "interferon", "insulin", "lysozyme"]', 0, 'Engineered TILs would return to the tumour and make TNF, which kills tumour cells.', '{"1": "Interferon is not the protein named.", "2": "Insulin is unrelated.", "3": "Lysozyme digests bacterial walls."}'),
  ('BIO-0524', 'Immunity', 'Allergies', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 182 (§21.3.3)', 'In an allergic reaction, E-antibodies bind to mast cells, which then release', '["perforins", "pyrogens", "complement proteins", "histamines"]', 3, 'Histamines start an inflammatory response: dilated vessels, leaking fluid, sneezing, itching.', '{"0": "Perforins are made by NK and cytotoxic T cells.", "1": "Pyrogens come from macrophages and cause fever.", "2": "Complement proteins come from the liver."}'),
  ('BIO-0525', 'Immunity', 'Allergies', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 182 (§21.3.3)', 'Desensitization treats allergies by injecting allergen extracts so that the body produces', '["more E-antibodies on mast cells", "antihistamines of its own", "G-antibodies that remove the allergen before it reaches E-antibodies", "more mast cells in connective tissue"]', 2, 'G-antibodies kill the allergen before it meets E-antibodies, so mast cells are not triggered.', '{"0": "More E-antibodies would worsen the allergy.", "1": "Antihistamines are drugs given from outside.", "3": "More mast cells would worsen the allergy."}'),
  ('BIO-0526', 'Immunity', 'Allergies', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 182 (§21.3.3)', 'Anaphylactic shock is dangerous mainly because', '["swelling makes breathing difficult", "body temperature falls below 30 °C", "all antibodies are destroyed", "the skin stops making keratin"]', 0, 'Histamines can cause life-threatening swelling that blocks breathing.', '{"1": "Not described.", "2": "Not described.", "3": "Unrelated to allergy."}'),
  ('BIO-0527', 'Immunity', 'Autoimmune diseases', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 183 (§21.3.3)', 'In myasthenia gravis, muscles fail to respond to nerve impulses because antibodies bind to', '["insulin-producing cells of the pancreas", "cartilage of the joints", "connective tissue of major organs", "acetylcholine receptors on skeletal muscle cells"]', 3, 'Antibodies block the ACh receptors, so the impulse cannot reach the muscle.', '{"0": "This is Type I diabetes.", "1": "This is rheumatoid arthritis.", "2": "This is SLE."}'),
  ('BIO-0528', 'Immunity', 'Autoimmune diseases', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 183 (§21.3.3)', 'Type I (juvenile) diabetes is classed as an autoimmune disease because', '["the body makes too much insulin", "antibodies destroy the insulin-producing cells of the pancreas", "the liver stops making glucose", "mast cells release histamine in the pancreas"]', 1, 'The immune system attacks the body''s own beta cells.', '{"0": "Type I diabetes means too little insulin.", "2": "Not the cause described.", "3": "That describes allergy, not autoimmunity."}'),
  ('BIO-0529', 'Immunity', 'Transplant rejection', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 183 (§21.3.3)', 'The best donor for an organ transplant, in the book''s order of preference, is', '["a parent", "an unrelated donor", "a grandparent", "an identical twin"]', 3, 'Identical twins match self-proteins 100%; order is identical twin > sibling > parent > unrelated.', '{"0": "Parents come after siblings.", "1": "Unrelated donors match about 1 in 100,000–200,000.", "2": "Not in the order given; less close than a twin."}'),
  ('BIO-0530', 'Immunity', 'Transplant rejection', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 183 (§21.3.3)', 'Which is one approach described for preventing transplant rejection?', '["injecting allergen extracts to make G-antibodies", "using monoclonal antibodies to destroy the T cells that attack the transplant", "raising body temperature with pyrogens", "giving antihistamine drugs"]', 1, 'The other approach uses stem cells to build a new immune system that treats the organ as self.', '{"0": "That is desensitization for allergy.", "2": "Fever does not prevent rejection.", "3": "Antihistamines treat allergy symptoms."}'),
  ('BIO-0531', 'Biostatistics', 'Biostatistics', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 186 (§22.1)', 'The main advantage of biostatistical analysis described in the book is that it allows', '["decisions about whole populations using data from a sample", "every member of a population to be tested cheaply", "experiments to be done without any data", "diseases to be cured directly"]', 0, 'Testing entire populations is often impractical or costly; a representative sample is analysed instead.', '{"1": "The point is to avoid testing everyone.", "2": "Statistics always needs data.", "3": "Statistics informs decisions; it does not cure disease."}'),
  ('BIO-0532', 'Biostatistics', 'Components', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 186 (§22.1.1)', 'Randomization, controlling confounding factors and calculating an adequate sample size are part of which component of biostatistics?', '["data management and processing", "presentation and communication", "study design and experimental planning", "interpretation of results"]', 2, 'Study design covers the research question, objectives, population, variables, randomization and sample size.', '{"0": "Data management is cleaning, coding and storing data.", "1": "Presentation is charts and clear reporting.", "3": "Interpretation turns findings into conclusions."}'),
  ('BIO-0533', 'Biostatistics', 'Components', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 187 (§22.1.1)', 'Data cleaning, coding and storage, while keeping data confidential, belong to the component called', '["data collection", "study design", "data analysis", "data management and processing"]', 3, 'Collected data is organized and checked for accuracy before analysis.', '{"0": "Collection is gathering primary or secondary data.", "1": "Study design is planning before data exist.", "2": "Analysis applies statistical techniques to the cleaned data."}'),
  ('BIO-0534', 'Biostatistics', 'Components', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 187 (§22.1.1)', 'Which of these is an example of inferential rather than descriptive statistics?', '["calculating the mean", "hypothesis testing", "finding the mode", "calculating the standard deviation"]', 1, 'Inferential statistics (hypothesis testing, regression, modelling) draw conclusions about populations from samples.', '{"0": "The mean only describes the data.", "2": "The mode only describes the data.", "3": "The standard deviation only describes the data."}'),
  ('BIO-0535', 'Biostatistics', 'Applications', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 188 (§22.1.2)', 'Biostatistics used to tailor treatment to an individual''s genetic profile (personalized medicine) is an application in', '["environmental health", "healthcare quality control", "genetics and genomics", "identifying barriers to care"]', 2, 'Genetics and genomics uses biostatistics to link genes with disease and support personalized medicine.', '{"0": "Environmental health studies pollution and toxins.", "1": "Quality control tracks test accuracy and outcomes.", "3": "Barriers to care concern access to health services."}'),
  ('BIO-0536', 'Biostatistics', 'Applications', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 187 (§22.1.2)', 'Studying the distribution and determinants of diseases within populations is called', '["bioinformatics", "pharmacology", "demography", "epidemiology"]', 3, 'Epidemiology uses biostatistics to see how diseases spread and to identify risk factors.', '{"0": "Bioinformatics analyses large genomic and proteomic datasets.", "1": "Pharmacology is the study of drugs.", "2": "Demography studies births, deaths and population trends."}'),
  ('BIO-0537', 'Biostatistics', 'Mean', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 189 (§22.2.1)', 'The lengths (cm) of five leaves are 12, 15, 18, 21 and 24. Their mean length is', '["15 cm", "18 cm", "21 cm", "90 cm"]', 1, 'Mean = Σx/n = 90/5 = 18 cm.', '{"0": "15 is the second value, not the mean.", "2": "21 is the fourth value.", "3": "90 is the sum, not divided by n."}'),
  ('BIO-0538', 'Biostatistics', 'Mean', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 190 (§22.2.1)', 'Grouped data: class 0–10 has frequency 2, class 10–20 has frequency 5 and class 20–30 has frequency 3. The mean is', '["16", "15", "20", "160"]', 0, 'Midpoints 5, 15, 25; Σfx = 10 + 75 + 75 = 160; Σf = 10; mean = 160/10 = 16.', '{"1": "15 is the middle midpoint, ignoring frequencies.", "2": "Not the result of Σfx/Σf.", "3": "160 is Σfx before dividing by Σf."}'),
  ('BIO-0539', 'Biostatistics', 'Mean', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 190 (§22.2.1)', 'For grouped data, the value xi used for a class interval of 30–40 when calculating the mean is', '["30", "40", "10", "35"]', 3, 'xi is the midpoint: (lower limit + upper limit)/2 = (30 + 40)/2 = 35.', '{"0": "30 is the lower limit.", "1": "40 is the upper limit.", "2": "10 is the class width."}'),
  ('BIO-0540', 'Biostatistics', 'Median', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 191 (§22.2.2)', 'The median of 7, 3, 9, 5 and 11 is', '["9", "7", "5", "35"]', 1, 'Ordered: 3, 5, 7, 9, 11; with n = 5 (odd), the median is the 3rd value, 7.', '{"0": "9 is the 4th value in order.", "2": "5 is the 2nd value in order.", "3": "35 is the sum."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-0541', 'Biostatistics', 'Median', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 191 (§22.2.2)', 'The median of 12, 20, 16, 30, 24 and 18 is', '["19", "18", "20", "16"]', 0, 'Ordered: 12, 16, 18, 20, 24, 30; n = 6 (even), so median = (18 + 20)/2 = 19.', '{"1": "18 is only the 3rd value.", "2": "20 is only the 4th value.", "3": "16 is the 3rd value of the unordered list."}'),
  ('BIO-0542', 'Biostatistics', 'Median', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 191 (§22.2.2)', 'According to the book, the median gives a better picture of central tendency than other measures when data', '["have only one value", "are perfectly symmetrical", "are not normally distributed, such as disease prevalence", "are arranged in classes only"]', 2, 'For skewed biological data (population size, prevalence, body measurements), the median represents the centre better.', '{"0": "A single value needs no measure of centre.", "1": "For symmetrical data mean and median agree.", "3": "Grouping is not the reason given."}'),
  ('BIO-0543', 'Biostatistics', 'Mode', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 192 (§22.2.3)', 'Numbers of seeds per pod: 4, 6, 6, 7, 8, 8, 8, 9. The mode is', '["6", "7", "7.5", "8"]', 3, '8 appears three times, more than any other value.', '{"0": "6 appears only twice.", "1": "7 appears once.", "2": "7.5 is the median of these values."}'),
  ('BIO-0544', 'Biostatistics', 'Mode', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 192 (§22.2.3)', 'The dataset 3, 3, 5, 7, 7, 9 has', '["one mode, 5", "one mode, 7 only", "two modes, 3 and 7", "a mode of 5.5"]', 2, 'The mode is the value or values that appear most frequently; 3 and 7 each appear twice.', '{"0": "5 appears only once.", "1": "3 appears as often as 7.", "3": "A mode must be a value that occurs in the data."}'),
  ('BIO-0545', 'Biostatistics', 'Mode', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 193 (§22.2.3)', 'Grouped data: 0–10 (f = 4), 10–20 (f = 10), 20–30 (f = 16), 30–40 (f = 8). Using mode = l + [(fm − f1)/((fm − f1) + (fm − f2))] × h, the mode is about', '["25.00", "24.29", "20.00", "26.67"]', 1, 'Modal class 20–30: l = 20, fm = 16, f1 = 10, f2 = 8, h = 10; mode = 20 + (6/14) × 10 = 24.29.', '{"0": "25 is just the class midpoint.", "2": "20 is only the lower boundary.", "3": "This uses the wrong frequencies in the formula."}'),
  ('BIO-0546', 'Biostatistics', 'Standard deviation', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 194 (§22.2.4)', 'Values 2, 4, 4, 4, 5, 5, 7, 9 have a mean of 5. Their standard deviation (dividing by N) is', '["2", "4", "32", "5"]', 0, 'Squared deviations sum to 32; variance = 32/8 = 4; SD = √4 = 2.', '{"1": "4 is the variance, before the square root.", "2": "32 is the sum of squared deviations.", "3": "5 is the mean."}'),
  ('BIO-0547', 'Biostatistics', 'Standard deviation', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 194 (§22.2.4)', 'Two plant samples have the same mean height. Sample X has a standard deviation of 1 cm and sample Y of 8 cm. This shows that', '["heights in X are clustered more tightly around the mean", "plants in X are taller on average", "Y has fewer plants", "X has a larger range"]', 0, 'A smaller SD means data points lie closer to the mean.', '{"1": "The means are the same.", "2": "SD does not show sample size.", "3": "Tight clustering usually means a smaller range."}'),
  ('BIO-0548', 'Biostatistics', 'Standard deviation', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 194 (§22.2.4)', 'In calculating standard deviation, the deviations from the mean are squared in order to', '["convert the data to percentages", "remove negative signs and emphasize larger deviations", "find the median", "make all deviations equal"]', 1, 'Squaring removes negatives and weights larger deviations more; the square root is taken at the end.', '{"0": "Percentages are not involved.", "2": "The median is a different measure.", "3": "Squaring does not equalize deviations."}'),
  ('BIO-0549', 'Biostatistics', 'Standard deviation', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 195 (§22.2.4)', 'The standard deviation is related to the variance in that it is', '["the variance multiplied by N", "half the variance", "the square of the variance", "the square root of the variance"]', 3, 'Variance is the average of squared deviations; SD = √variance (e.g. √87.11 ≈ 9.33).', '{"0": "Multiplying by N gives the sum of squares.", "1": "Halving has no meaning here.", "2": "Squaring goes the wrong way."}'),
  ('BIO-0550', 'Biostatistics', 'Range', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 195 (§22.2.5)', 'Body temperatures (°C) of five patients are 37.2, 38.5, 36.9, 39.1 and 37.8. The range is', '["1.3 °C", "0.6 °C", "2.2 °C", "37.9 °C"]', 2, 'Range = maximum − minimum = 39.1 − 36.9 = 2.2 °C.', '{"0": "39.1 − 37.8; 37.8 is not the minimum.", "1": "37.8 − 37.2; neither is an extreme.", "3": "37.9 is the mean, not the range."}'),
  ('BIO-0551', 'Biostatistics', 'Range', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 195 (§22.2.5)', 'A limitation of the range as a measure of spread is that it', '["uses every value in its calculation", "depends only on the two extreme values", "cannot be calculated for small datasets", "is always zero for biological data"]', 1, 'Range = Xmax − Xmin; it is the simplest measure of variability, so one extreme value changes it.', '{"0": "Standard deviation, not range, uses every value.", "2": "It can be found for any dataset of two or more values.", "3": "It is zero only when all values are equal."}'),
  ('BIO-0552', 'Biostatistics', 'Percentile', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 196 (§22.2.6)', 'The 75th percentile of a dataset is also called the', '["first quartile (Q1)", "median", "mode", "third quartile (Q3)"]', 3, '25th = Q1, 50th = median, 75th = Q3.', '{"0": "Q1 is the 25th percentile.", "1": "The median is the 50th percentile.", "2": "The mode is the most frequent value."}'),
  ('BIO-0553', 'Biostatistics', 'Percentile', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 196 (§22.2.6)', 'Using R = (P/100)(n + 1), the rank of the 25th percentile in an ordered set of 19 observations is', '["5", "4.75", "25", "20"]', 0, 'R = 0.25 × 20 = 5, so the 25th percentile is the 5th value.', '{"1": "4.75 comes from using n instead of n + 1.", "2": "25 is P, not R.", "3": "20 is n + 1."}'),
  ('BIO-0554', 'Biostatistics', 'Percentile', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 196 (§22.2.6)', 'Ordered data: 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12, 14, 16. Using R = (P/100)(n + 1) and interpolation, the 30th percentile is', '["4", "6", "5.4", "2.7"]', 2, 'R = 0.3 × 9 = 2.7; value = 4 + 0.7 × (6 − 4) = 5.4.', '{"0": "4 is the 2nd value without interpolation.", "1": "6 is the 3rd value.", "3": "2.7 is the rank, not the value."}'),
  ('BIO-0555', 'Biostatistics', 'Bar chart', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 197 (§22.3.1)', 'In a bar chart comparing counts of five insect species, the species names are placed on the', '["x-axis, with the counts on the y-axis", "y-axis, with the counts on the x-axis", "legend only, with no axes", "circle, as sectors"]', 0, 'Categories go on the horizontal x-axis; values or frequencies go on the vertical y-axis.', '{"1": "This reverses the standard arrangement described.", "2": "A bar chart has axes.", "3": "Sectors are used in pie charts."}'),
  ('BIO-0556', 'Biostatistics', 'Bar chart', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 197 (§22.3.1)', 'When drawing a bar chart, the bars should be', '["wider for larger values", "of equal width and evenly spaced", "drawn touching in a circle", "of random width"]', 1, 'Only the height (or length) of each bar shows its value.', '{"0": "Width must not carry information.", "2": "That is not a bar chart.", "3": "Random widths would mislead."}'),
  ('BIO-0557', 'Biostatistics', 'Bar chart', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 198 (§22.3.1)', 'The key on a chart that explains what each colour or pattern represents is the', '["axis title", "error bar", "legend", "class interval"]', 2, 'A legend identifies what the symbols, colours or patterns stand for.', '{"0": "An axis title names an axis.", "1": "Error bars show variability.", "3": "A class interval groups data."}'),
  ('BIO-0558', 'Biostatistics', 'Pie chart', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 199 (§22.3.2)', 'In a survey of 240 students, 60 have blood group B. On a pie chart, the sector angle for group B is', '["60°", "25°", "144°", "90°"]', 3, 'Sector angle = (60/240) × 360° = 90°.', '{"0": "60 is the frequency, not the angle.", "1": "25 is the percentage, not the angle.", "2": "144° would be 40% of the circle."}'),
  ('BIO-0559', 'Biostatistics', 'Pie chart', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 199 (§22.3.2)', 'A sector with an angle of 54° on a pie chart represents what percentage of the whole?', '["54%", "15%", "5.4%", "30%"]', 1, '54/360 × 100 = 15%.', '{"0": "The angle is not the percentage.", "2": "Wrong by a factor of 10.", "3": "30% would need 108°."}'),
  ('BIO-0560', 'Biostatistics', 'Choosing a chart', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 199 (§22.3.2)', 'To show how a whole population of cells is divided among four cell types as parts of 100%, the most suitable chart is a', '["x-axis data figure with a line", "bar chart with error bars", "pie chart", "scatter of individual measurements"]', 2, 'Pie charts show categorical data as portions of a whole.', '{"0": "Line figures show trends against an independent variable such as time.", "1": "Error bars show variability around means.", "3": "Not suited to parts of a whole."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-0561', 'Biostatistics', 'X-axis data figure', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 200 (§22.3.2, X-axis data figure)', 'In a graph of enzyme activity measured at several temperatures, temperature is placed on the x-axis because it is the', '["dependent variable", "control variable", "legend", "independent variable"]', 3, 'The x-axis usually shows the independent variable; enzyme activity, the measured result, goes on the y-axis.', '{"0": "Enzyme activity is the dependent variable.", "1": "Control variables are kept constant, not plotted.", "2": "The legend is a key, not a variable."}'),
  ('BIO-0562', 'Biostatistics', 'Error bars', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 201 (§22.3.2, Error bars)', 'Range error bars on a bar chart extend', '["from the minimum to the maximum value of each group", "one standard deviation above and below the mean", "from zero to the mean", "from the mode to the median"]', 0, 'Range error bars show the full spread of data in each group.', '{"1": "That describes SD error bars.", "2": "That is the bar itself.", "3": "Not a type of error bar."}'),
  ('BIO-0563', 'Biostatistics', 'Error bars', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 202 (§22.3.2, Error bars)', 'A group of plants has a mean height of 15 cm and a standard deviation of 2 cm. A ±1 SD error bar on its bar would run from', '["14 cm to 16 cm", "15 cm to 17 cm", "11 cm to 19 cm", "13 cm to 17 cm"]', 3, 'Mean ± 1 SD = 15 ± 2.', '{"0": "This uses ±1 cm.", "1": "This extends only upward.", "2": "This is ±2 SD."}'),
  ('BIO-0564', 'Biostatistics', 'Error bars', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 203 (§22.3.2, Error bars)', 'Heights in one group are 8, 12 and 10 cm. Using range error = range/2, as in the book''s table, the range error is', '["±4 cm", "±10 cm", "±2 cm", "±1 cm"]', 2, 'Range = 12 − 8 = 4; range error = 4/2 = ±2 cm.', '{"0": "4 is the whole range.", "1": "10 is the mean.", "3": "Half of the range is 2, not 1."}'),
  ('BIO-0565', 'Biostatistics', 'Experimental design', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 204 (§22.3 (Experimental design))', 'Students test whether fertilizer concentration affects the number of leaves on bean plants. The dependent variable is', '["the fertilizer concentration", "the number of leaves", "the type of pot used for all plants", "the plants given no fertilizer"]', 1, 'The dependent variable is what is measured and changes in response to the independent variable.', '{"0": "Fertilizer concentration is the independent variable.", "2": "Pot type is a control variable kept the same.", "3": "Plants given no fertilizer form the control group."}'),
  ('BIO-0566', 'Biostatistics', 'Experimental design', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 205 (§22.3 (Experimental design))', 'In the book''s experiment on a high-fat diet and weight gain in mice, which is a control variable?', '["the age of the mice", "the type of diet", "the weight gained", "the mice fed the high-fat diet"]', 0, 'Control variables (age, strain, sex, cage, light, temperature, feeding schedule) are kept the same for a fair test.', '{"1": "Diet type is the independent variable.", "2": "Weight gain is the dependent variable.", "3": "These mice are the experimental group."}'),
  ('BIO-0567', 'Biostatistics', 'Experimental design', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 205 (§22.3 (Experimental design))', 'In the mouse diet experiment, the mice are randomly assigned to the two groups so that', '["the groups are comparable and bias is reduced", "the high-fat group always gains more weight", "no control group is needed", "the dependent variable stays constant"]', 0, 'Random assignment avoids systematic differences between groups apart from the diet.', '{"1": "Randomization does not fix the outcome.", "2": "A control group is still needed.", "3": "The dependent variable is what is measured."}'),
  ('BIO-0568', 'Biostatistics', 'Experimental design', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 204 (§22.3 (Experimental design))', 'In the book''s steps for designing an experiment, which step comes immediately after stating the question?', '["drawing conclusions", "analysing results", "setting up control and experimental groups", "formulating a hypothesis"]', 3, 'Order: question, hypothesis, identify variables, set up groups, plan procedure, analyse, conclude.', '{"0": "Conclusions come last.", "1": "Analysis comes near the end.", "2": "Groups are set up after variables are identified."}'),
  ('BIO-0569', 'Biostatistics', 'Experimental design', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 205 (§22.3 (Experimental design))', 'If the high-fat and normal-diet mice show no significant difference in weight gain, the hypothesis that a high-fat diet causes greater weight gain is', '["supported", "proved true", "rejected", "unaffected by the results"]', 2, 'A hypothesis is accepted or rejected on the basis of the results.', '{"0": "Support requires a significant increase in the high-fat group.", "1": "No significant difference cannot prove it.", "3": "The results are what decide it."}'),
  ('BIO-0570', 'Pharmacology', 'Pharmacology', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 23 Pharmacology, p. 208 (chapter introduction)', 'Medicines prepared from plants and used in traditional systems of treatment are called', '["herbal medicines, or phytotherapy", "antibiotics", "monoclonal antibodies", "antiretrovirals"]', 0, 'Drugs come from plants, animals, microbes or laboratory synthesis; plant medicines are herbal medicines (phytotherapy).', '{"1": "Antibiotics act against bacteria and are not defined by plant origin.", "2": "Monoclonals are laboratory-made antibodies.", "3": "Antiretrovirals treat retroviral infections."}'),
  ('BIO-0571', 'Pharmacology', 'Drug discovery', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 23 Pharmacology, p. 209 (§23.1.1)', 'In drug discovery, the stage in which researchers confirm by experiment that acting on a chosen target produces a therapeutic effect is', '["target identification", "target validation", "lead optimization", "post-marketing surveillance"]', 1, 'Order: target identification, target validation, lead compound identification, lead optimization.', '{"0": "Identification only picks the molecule.", "2": "Optimization modifies lead compounds.", "3": "Surveillance happens after the drug is sold."}'),
  ('BIO-0572', 'Pharmacology', 'Drug discovery', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 23 Pharmacology, p. 209 (§23.1.1)', 'Promising compounds, found by screening thousands of substances, that interact effectively with the target molecule are called', '["target molecules", "placebos", "lead compounds", "hybridomas"]', 2, 'Screened natural or synthetic substances that interact well with the target are lead compounds.', '{"0": "The target is the disease molecule, such as an enzyme.", "1": "Placebos are inactive comparison treatments.", "3": "Hybridomas make monoclonal antibodies."}'),
  ('BIO-0573', 'Pharmacology', 'Drug discovery', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 23 Pharmacology, p. 209 (§23.1.1)', 'Lead optimization involves', '["testing the drug in thousands of patients", "selling the drug and monitoring rare side effects", "choosing a disease-causing protein to target", "modifying lead compounds to improve effectiveness and reduce toxicity"]', 3, 'Lead compounds are chemically modified to improve effectiveness, reduce toxicity and enhance drug-like properties.', '{"0": "That is Phase 3 of clinical trials.", "1": "That is post-marketing surveillance.", "2": "That is target identification."}'),
  ('BIO-0574', 'Pharmacology', 'Drug development', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 23 Pharmacology, p. 209 (§23.1.2)', 'Testing a compound on cell cultures and in animals before any human trial is called', '["Phase 1 clinical trial", "FDA drug review", "preclinical research", "lead compound identification"]', 2, 'Preclinical research uses in vitro (cell culture) and in vivo (animal) testing for safety, toxicity and efficacy.', '{"0": "Phase 1 is the first test in humans.", "1": "Drug review examines trial data.", "3": "Lead identification is a discovery step."}'),
  ('BIO-0575', 'Pharmacology', 'Drug development', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 23 Pharmacology, p. 209 (§23.1.2)', 'Phase 1 clinical trials mainly determine safety and dosage in', '["1,000–3,000 patients", "a small group of 20–100 healthy volunteers", "laboratory animals only", "the general public after marketing"]', 1, 'Phase 1 is small and uses healthy volunteers; later phases use patients.', '{"0": "That is Phase 3.", "2": "Animal testing is preclinical.", "3": "That is post-marketing surveillance."}'),
  ('BIO-0576', 'Pharmacology', 'Drug development', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 23 Pharmacology, p. 209 (§23.1.2)', 'Large-scale trials in about 1,000–3,000 patients that confirm efficacy and monitor adverse reactions are', '["Phase 3 trials", "Phase 1 trials", "preclinical trials", "lead optimization"]', 0, 'Phase 1: 20–100 healthy volunteers; Phase 2: 100–300 patients; Phase 3: 1,000–3,000 patients.', '{"1": "Phase 1 is small.", "2": "Preclinical work is not in humans.", "3": "Optimization happens before testing."}'),
  ('BIO-0577', 'Pharmacology', 'Drug development', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 23 Pharmacology, p. 209 (§23.1.2)', 'Rare and long-term side effects of a newly released drug are detected mainly during', '["target validation", "Phase 1 trials", "preclinical research", "post-marketing surveillance"]', 3, 'After release the drug is monitored continuously in the whole population.', '{"0": "Validation is before any compound exists.", "1": "Phase 1 groups are too small to reveal rare effects.", "2": "Preclinical work is before human use."}'),
  ('BIO-0578', 'Pharmacology', 'Penicillin', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 23 Pharmacology, p. 210 (§23.2.1)', 'Penicillin was discovered in 1928 by', '["Gerhard Domagk", "George Lesher", "Cesar Milstein", "Alexander Fleming"]', 3, 'Penicillin comes from Penicillium fungi; Fleming discovered it in 1928.', '{"0": "Domagk is linked with sulfonamides (1932).", "1": "Lesher discovered fluoroquinolones (1962).", "2": "Milstein shared the Nobel for monoclonal antibodies."}'),
  ('BIO-0579', 'Pharmacology', 'Penicillin', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 23 Pharmacology, p. 210 (§23.2.1)', 'Penicillin kills bacteria by', '["binding the 30S ribosomal subunit", "blocking cross-linking of peptidoglycan so the cell wall fails and the cell bursts", "inhibiting DNA gyrase", "blocking folate synthesis"]', 1, 'Without new cross-linked murein, the wall loses integrity and the cell lyses.', '{"0": "That is tetracycline.", "2": "That is a fluoroquinolone.", "3": "That is a sulfonamide."}'),
  ('BIO-0580', 'Pharmacology', 'Penicillin', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 23 Pharmacology, p. 210 (§23.2.1)', 'Penicillin does not harm human cells because', '["animal cells lack the cell walls it targets", "human cells make beta-lactamase", "human ribosomes are 30S", "human cells cannot absorb it"]', 0, 'Its target, peptidoglycan cell-wall synthesis, exists only in bacteria.', '{"1": "Beta-lactamases are bacterial resistance enzymes.", "2": "Human cytoplasmic ribosomes are 80S.", "3": "Absorption is not the reason given."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-0581', 'Pharmacology', 'Penicillin', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 23 Pharmacology, p. 210 (§23.2.1)', 'Some bacteria resist penicillin by producing', '["reverse transcriptase", "DNA gyrase", "beta-lactamase enzymes", "lysozyme"]', 2, 'Beta-lactamases are the resistance mechanism named in the book.', '{"0": "Reverse transcriptase is a retroviral enzyme.", "1": "Gyrase is the target of fluoroquinolones.", "3": "Lysozyme attacks bacterial walls."}'),
  ('BIO-0582', 'Pharmacology', 'Tetracycline', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 23 Pharmacology, p. 211 (§23.2.2)', 'Tetracycline inhibits bacterial protein synthesis by', '["binding the 30S subunit so aminoacyl-tRNA cannot enter the acceptor site", "breaking bacterial DNA strands", "blocking cell-wall cross-links", "inhibiting folic acid synthesis"]', 0, 'It binds reversibly to the 30S ribosomal subunit and blocks translation.', '{"1": "DNA breaks are caused by fluoroquinolones.", "2": "Cell-wall inhibition is penicillin.", "3": "Folate inhibition is sulfonamides."}'),
  ('BIO-0583', 'Pharmacology', 'Tetracycline', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 23 Pharmacology, p. 211 (§23.2.2)', 'Which statement about tetracycline is correct?', '["it is bactericidal and acts only on Gram-positive bacteria", "it should be taken with dairy products to increase absorption", "it is made from the bark of the Cinchona tree", "it is bacteriostatic and acts on both Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria"]', 3, 'It stops bacterial growth rather than killing outright, and should not be taken with dairy or iron.', '{"0": "It is bacteriostatic, not bactericidal.", "1": "The book says not to take it with dairy or iron.", "2": "It comes from Streptomyces; Cinchona gives quinine."}'),
  ('BIO-0584', 'Pharmacology', 'Tetracycline', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 23 Pharmacology, p. 211 (§23.2.2)', 'A side effect listed for tetracycline but not for penicillin is', '["nausea", "sensitivity to sunlight", "diarrhoea", "vomiting"]', 1, 'Tetracycline side effects include nausea, vomiting, diarrhoea and sun sensitivity.', '{"0": "Listed for both.", "2": "Listed for both.", "3": "Listed for both."}'),
  ('BIO-0585', 'Pharmacology', 'Fluoroquinolones', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 23 Pharmacology, p. 211 (§23.2.3)', 'Fluoroquinolones kill bacteria by', '["blocking peptidoglycan cross-links", "binding the 30S ribosomal subunit", "trapping type II topoisomerases so DNA strands break", "competing with PABA in folate synthesis"]', 2, 'The trapped enzyme complex causes double-strand DNA breaks, so they are bactericidal.', '{"0": "That is penicillin.", "1": "That is tetracycline.", "3": "That is sulfonamides."}'),
  ('BIO-0586', 'Pharmacology', 'Fluoroquinolones', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 23 Pharmacology, p. 211 (§23.2.3)', 'In Gram-negative bacteria, fluoroquinolones mainly target', '["DNA gyrase, stopping DNA uncoiling during replication", "topoisomerase IV, stopping separation of replicated DNA", "the 50S ribosomal subunit", "the outer membrane lipids"]', 0, 'Gyrase is the main target in Gram-negative bacteria; topoisomerase IV in Gram-positive bacteria.', '{"1": "Topoisomerase IV is the main target in Gram-positive bacteria.", "2": "Ribosomes are not their target.", "3": "Membrane lipids are not their target."}'),
  ('BIO-0587', 'Pharmacology', 'Fluoroquinolones', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 23 Pharmacology, p. 211 (§23.2.3)', 'A rare but serious adverse effect associated with fluoroquinolones is', '["hair loss", "night blindness", "tendon rupture", "tooth decay"]', 2, 'Rare effects include tendon rupture, peripheral neuropathy and cardiac problems.', '{"0": "Not listed.", "1": "Not listed.", "3": "Not listed."}'),
  ('BIO-0588', 'Pharmacology', 'Sulfonamides', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 23 Pharmacology, p. 212 (§23.2.4)', 'Sulfonamides are selectively toxic to bacteria because', '["human cells lack ribosomes", "bacteria must make folate themselves while humans absorb it from food", "only bacteria have DNA", "human cells have cell walls that block the drug"]', 1, 'Blocking folate synthesis starves bacteria but spares human cells that take folate from the diet.', '{"0": "Human cells have ribosomes.", "2": "Human cells also have DNA.", "3": "Human cells have no cell walls."}'),
  ('BIO-0589', 'Pharmacology', 'Sulfonamides', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 23 Pharmacology, p. 212 (§23.2.4)', 'Sulfonamides are often combined with trimethoprim in order to', '["make them act on viruses", "reduce their spectrum to Gram-positive bacteria only", "convert them into penicillin", "produce a stronger, bactericidal effect"]', 3, 'Alone they are bacteriostatic; combined therapy kills bacteria.', '{"0": "Neither drug is antiviral.", "1": "Sulfonamides are broad-spectrum.", "2": "No conversion occurs."}'),
  ('BIO-0590', 'Pharmacology', 'Antibiotic classes', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 23 Pharmacology, p. 210 (§23.2)', 'Which pair of antibiotic classes is described in the book as bactericidal?', '["tetracycline and sulfonamides", "penicillin and tetracycline", "sulfonamides and fluoroquinolones", "penicillin and fluoroquinolones"]', 3, 'Penicillin lyses bacteria and fluoroquinolones break their DNA; tetracycline and sulfonamides alone are bacteriostatic.', '{"0": "Both are bacteriostatic.", "1": "Tetracycline is bacteriostatic.", "2": "Sulfonamides alone are bacteriostatic."}'),
  ('BIO-0591', 'Pharmacology', 'Antivirals', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 23 Pharmacology, p. 212 (§23.3)', 'Which statement about antiviral and antiretroviral drugs is correct?', '["all antivirals are antiretrovirals", "all antiretrovirals are antivirals, but not all antivirals are antiretrovirals", "antiretrovirals treat bacterial infections", "antivirals work by blocking cell-wall synthesis"]', 1, 'Antiretrovirals target retroviruses such as HIV, which are one group of viruses.', '{"0": "Antivirals also treat hepatitis, influenza and herpes viruses.", "2": "They treat retroviral infections.", "3": "Viruses have no cell walls."}'),
  ('BIO-0592', 'Pharmacology', 'Antivirals', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 23 Pharmacology, p. 212 (§23.3)', 'Retroviruses such as HIV are defined by', '["having a peptidoglycan cell wall", "needing folate from food", "having RNA as genetic material and replicating with reverse transcriptase", "having 30S ribosomes"]', 2, 'Antiretroviral drugs act against these RNA viruses that use reverse transcriptase.', '{"0": "Peptidoglycan walls belong to bacteria.", "1": "Not a feature of viruses.", "3": "Viruses have no ribosomes."}'),
  ('BIO-0593', 'Pharmacology', 'Monoclonal antibodies', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 23 Pharmacology, p. 213 (§23.4.2)', 'In cancer treatment, an advantage of monoclonal antibodies over conventional chemotherapy is that they', '["target cancer cells and reduce damage to healthy tissue", "kill all dividing cells equally", "act against every antigen at once", "cure all bacterial infections"]', 0, 'They are highly specific, giving targeted therapy with fewer side effects and lower toxicity.', '{"1": "Conventional chemotherapy affects dividing cells broadly.", "2": "Each binds a single target.", "3": "They are not general antibacterials."}'),
  ('BIO-0594', 'Evolution', 'Creationism', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 215 (chapter introduction)', 'The view that every type of organism was created individually in its present form and has not changed is called', '["spontaneous generation", "Lamarckism", "Neo-Darwinism", "creationism"]', 3, 'Creationism (intelligent design) holds that organisms were created as they exist today.', '{"0": "Spontaneous generation is life arising from non-living matter.", "1": "Lamarckism is an evolutionary theory.", "2": "Neo-Darwinism is the modern evolutionary synthesis."}'),
  ('BIO-0595', 'Evolution', 'Concept of evolution', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 215 (§24.1)', 'Experiments that disproved spontaneous generation did NOT', '["show that life comes only from life", "reject the idea that life arises from non-living matter", "disprove creationism", "advance scientific thinking"]', 2, 'The book notes that such experiments disproved spontaneous generation but could not negate creationism.', '{"0": "They did support the idea that life comes from life.", "1": "That is exactly what they did.", "3": "They were a step in the progress of science."}'),
  ('BIO-0596', 'Evolution', 'Concept of evolution', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 215 (§24.1)', 'In modern terms, evolution is best defined as', '["the growth of an individual organism during its lifetime", "a change in allele frequencies of a population over generations", "the creation of each species separately", "the origin of life from non-living matter"]', 1, 'Evolution is a gradual change in the genetic makeup of a population, which may lead to new species.', '{"0": "Individuals develop; populations evolve.", "2": "That is creationism.", "3": "That is spontaneous generation."}'),
  ('BIO-0597', 'Evolution', 'Darwinism', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 216 (§24.2)', 'Darwin''s five-year voyage around the world as a naturalist, beginning in 1831, was aboard', '["HMS Beagle", "HMS Galápagos", "HMS Endeavour", "HMS Victoria"]', 0, 'The Beagle expedition took Darwin to rainforests, fossil sites and the Galápagos Islands.', '{"1": "Galápagos is the island group, not the ship.", "2": "Not the ship named in the book.", "3": "Not the ship named in the book."}'),
  ('BIO-0598', 'Evolution', 'Darwinism', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 216 (§24.2)', 'Darwin found that giant tortoises on different Galápagos islands differed in having', '["different numbers of legs", "saddle-shaped shells on one island and dome-shaped shells on another", "wings on some islands", "gills on smaller islands"]', 1, 'Island-to-island differences in shell shape helped him think about adaptation.', '{"0": "Not observed.", "2": "Not observed.", "3": "Not observed."}'),
  ('BIO-0599', 'Evolution', 'Darwinism', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 216 (§24.2)', 'Darwin concluded that the different beak sizes and shapes of Galápagos finches were', '["adaptations for feeding on different kinds of food", "caused by stretching beaks during life", "identical on every island", "unrelated to the environment"]', 0, 'He linked the origin of new species with gradual accumulation of adaptations to different environments.', '{"1": "That is a Lamarckian explanation.", "2": "The beaks differed between islands.", "3": "He related them to food sources."}'),
  ('BIO-0600', 'Evolution', 'Darwinism', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 216 (§24.2)', 'Darwin noticed that many Galápagos plants and animals most resembled those of', '["Africa", "Australia", "Europe", "South America"]', 3, 'The resemblance to the nearest mainland suggested descent from colonists that later changed.', '{"0": "Not the mainland described.", "1": "Not the mainland described.", "2": "Not the mainland described."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-0601', 'Evolution', 'Natural selection', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 217 (§24.2.1)', 'Darwin published "On the Origin of Species" in', '["1831", "1809", "1859", "1908"]', 2, 'Darwin (1809–1882) proposed evolution by natural selection in 1859.', '{"0": "1831 is when the Beagle voyage began.", "1": "1809 is Darwin''s birth year and Lamarck''s book year.", "3": "1908 is the Hardy-Weinberg theorem."}'),
  ('BIO-0602', 'Evolution', 'Natural selection', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 217 (§24.2.1)', 'The example of a sea star releasing about a million eggs each season illustrates which point of Darwin''s theory?', '["overproduction", "inheritance of acquired characters", "gene flow", "mutational equilibrium"]', 0, 'Organisms have far greater reproductive potential than is ever realized.', '{"1": "That is Lamarck''s idea.", "2": "Gene flow is migration between populations.", "3": "That is a mutation balance concept."}'),
  ('BIO-0603', 'Evolution', 'Natural selection', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 218 (§24.2.1)', 'In Darwin''s theory, variations that help individuals survive and reproduce are called', '["acquired characters", "adaptive traits", "vestigial structures", "analogous organs"]', 1, 'Individuals with advantageous variations reproduce successfully; these variations are adaptive traits.', '{"0": "Acquired characters are Lamarck''s term.", "2": "Vestigial structures have lost their use.", "3": "Analogous organs share function but not structure."}'),
  ('BIO-0604', 'Evolution', 'Natural selection', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 218 (§24.2.1)', 'Why do disadvantageous traits eventually disappear from a population under natural selection?', '["the organisms stop using those organs", "mutations stop producing them", "individuals carrying them are less likely to reproduce", "the environment removes them from DNA directly"]', 2, 'Lower reproduction makes such traits less frequent each generation until they are eliminated.', '{"0": "Disuse is a Lamarckian idea.", "1": "Mutations keep occurring.", "3": "The environment selects; it does not edit DNA."}'),
  ('BIO-0605', 'Evolution', 'Neo-Darwinism', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 218 (§24.2.2)', 'Neo-Darwinism arose in the early 20th century by combining Darwinism with', '["the endosymbiotic theory", "creationism", "the idea of spontaneous generation", "modern genetics"]', 3, 'It defines evolution as a change in allele frequencies in the gene pool, based on the Hardy-Weinberg theorem.', '{"0": "Endosymbiosis explains eukaryote origins.", "1": "Creationism denies evolution.", "2": "Spontaneous generation had been disproved."}'),
  ('BIO-0606', 'Evolution', 'Hardy-Weinberg', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 218 (§24.2.2)', 'The Hardy-Weinberg theorem (1908) was proposed by', '["two English naturalists on the Beagle", "a French naturalist and his students", "an American biologist who studied cells", "an English mathematician and a German physician"]', 3, 'Godfrey Hardy (mathematician, England) and Wilhelm Weinberg (physician, Germany).', '{"0": "That describes no one in this theorem.", "1": "That suggests Lamarck.", "2": "That suggests Lynn Margulis."}'),
  ('BIO-0607', 'Evolution', 'Hardy-Weinberg', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 218 (§24.2.2)', 'According to the Hardy-Weinberg theorem, allele and genotype frequencies remain constant when', '["a large, randomly mating population has no evolutionary forces acting", "a small population is isolated on an island", "individuals choose mates of the same colour", "many new mutations arise each generation"]', 0, 'Large size, random mating, no migration, no net mutation and equal survival keep frequencies constant.', '{"1": "Small populations undergo genetic drift.", "2": "Non-random mating breaks the equilibrium.", "3": "Mutation changes allele frequencies."}'),
  ('BIO-0608', 'Evolution', 'Hardy-Weinberg', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 218 (§24.2.2)', 'In a plant population, 300 of 1200 flower-colour genes are for white. The allele frequency of white is', '["0.30", "0.25", "0.75", "0.40"]', 1, 'Allele frequency = 300/1200 = 0.25 (25%).', '{"0": "Uses 300 as a percentage of 1000.", "2": "0.75 is the frequency of the other allele.", "3": "Not obtainable from these numbers."}'),
  ('BIO-0609', 'Evolution', 'Hardy-Weinberg', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 218 (§24.2.2)', 'Of 250 plants, 50 have genotype rr. The genotype frequency of rr is', '["0.5", "0.25", "0.2", "0.05"]', 2, 'Genotype frequency = number of individuals with the genotype / total = 50/250 = 0.2.', '{"0": "Wrong division.", "1": "Wrong division.", "3": "Wrong by a factor of 4."}'),
  ('BIO-0610', 'Evolution', 'Hardy-Weinberg', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 218 (§24.2.2)', 'The total of all the genes for a trait in a population is called its', '["genotype frequency", "allele frequency", "gene pool", "genome"]', 2, 'Example: a population with 1000 genes for flower colour has a gene pool of 1000 such genes.', '{"0": "Genotype frequency is the proportion of individuals with a genotype.", "1": "Allele frequency is the proportion of one allele.", "3": "A genome is all the DNA of one organism."}'),
  ('BIO-0611', 'Evolution', 'Genetic drift', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 219 (§24.2.2)', 'Genetic drift differs from natural selection because the changes it causes', '["happen by chance rather than because of fitness", "always increase genetic diversity", "are most obvious in very large populations", "result from migration"]', 0, 'Drift is often called neutral selection; it is strongest in small populations and reduces diversity.', '{"1": "Drift usually lowers diversity.", "2": "It is most obvious in small populations.", "3": "Migration causes gene flow."}'),
  ('BIO-0612', 'Evolution', 'Gene flow', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 219 (§24.2.2)', 'Gene flow can change the gene frequencies of a population almost instantly because', '["it causes mutations in resident individuals", "it prevents random mating", "it reduces the population size to a few individuals", "immigrants can bring in alleles the population did not have"]', 3, 'Entry of individuals from another population introduces new alleles.', '{"0": "Gene flow moves existing alleles; it does not mutate residents.", "1": "Not the mechanism described.", "2": "That describes a bottleneck and drift."}'),
  ('BIO-0613', 'Evolution', 'Mutation', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 219 (§24.2.2)', 'Mutational equilibrium exists when', '["no organism in the population has any mutation", "mutation from wild-type to mutant is balanced by mutation back to wild-type", "all mutations are harmful", "mutation pressure is at its maximum"]', 1, 'Balanced forward and back mutation leaves allele frequency unchanged, as if no mutation occurred; it rarely exists.', '{"0": "Equilibrium is about balance, not absence.", "2": "Mutations can also be neutral or helpful.", "3": "Mutation pressure measures the tendency to change."}'),
  ('BIO-0614', 'Evolution', 'Mutation', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 219 (§24.2.2)', 'Mutations are important in evolution mainly because they', '["are always beneficial", "reduce variation in populations", "are the origin of all new genes and a source of variation", "happen only in small populations"]', 2, 'Most mutations are harmful, but they supply all new alleles on which selection acts.', '{"0": "Most are deleterious.", "1": "They add variation.", "3": "They occur in all populations."}'),
  ('BIO-0615', 'Evolution', 'Biogeography', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 219 (§24.3.1)', 'The African lion and the American mountain lion are similar yet clearly different. Biogeography explains this as', '["convergent evolution from unrelated ancestors", "common ancestry followed by millions of years of independent evolution", "the result of Lamarckian use and disuse", "recent migration between continents"]', 1, 'Similarities suggest a shared ancestor; differences reflect long separate evolution.', '{"0": "The book presents them as having a common ancestry.", "2": "Lamarckism is not the explanation given.", "3": "They are separated by vast barriers."}'),
  ('BIO-0616', 'Evolution', 'Biogeography', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 220 (§24.3.1)', 'Islands often have relatively few but unique species because of', '["colonization by a few organisms followed by separate evolution", "more frequent creation of species there", "constant gene flow from the mainland", "the absence of any mutations"]', 0, 'Island colonization and subsequent evolutionary events produce distinctive island species.', '{"1": "Not a scientific explanation.", "2": "Isolation, not constant gene flow, makes them unique.", "3": "Mutations occur everywhere."}'),
  ('BIO-0617', 'Evolution', 'Comparative anatomy', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 220 (§24.3.2)', 'Homologous structures, similar in structure but different in function, are evidence of', '["convergent evolution from different ancestors", "inheritance of acquired characters", "spontaneous generation", "divergent evolution from a common ancestor"]', 3, 'Vertebrate limbs share a bone plan but are used for flying, swimming, running or climbing.', '{"0": "Convergence produces analogous structures.", "1": "Not an accepted mechanism.", "2": "Not related."}'),
  ('BIO-0618', 'Evolution', 'Comparative anatomy', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 221 (§24.3.2)', 'The wing of a bird and the wing of an insect are analogous because they', '["serve the same function but evolved from different ancestors", "have the same bones arranged differently", "are both vestigial", "came from a common winged ancestor"]', 0, 'Both need a broad, flat surface for flight, which explains the similarity; they show convergent evolution.', '{"1": "Insect wings have no bones.", "2": "Both are functional.", "3": "They have different ancestors."}'),
  ('BIO-0619', 'Evolution', 'Vestigial organs', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 221 (§24.3.2)', 'Tiny pelvic bones in boa constrictors and pythons are an example of', '["analogous structures", "acquired characters", "vestigial structures from limbed reptilian ancestors", "homologous biochemicals"]', 2, 'Remnants of once-useful structures are clear indications of evolutionary change.', '{"0": "Analogous structures share function, not ancestry.", "1": "Acquired characters are Lamarck''s concept.", "3": "That term refers to molecules such as DNA and proteins."}'),
  ('BIO-0620', 'Evolution', 'Vestigial organs', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 221 (§24.3.2)', 'In humans, the vermiform appendix is considered a vestige of the', '["tail", "caecum", "third molars", "ear muscles"]', 1, 'Other human vestiges: coccyx (tail), wisdom teeth (third molars), ear muscles.', '{"0": "The coccyx is the remnant of the tail.", "2": "Wisdom teeth are the vestigial third molars.", "3": "Ear muscles are vestigial themselves."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-0621', 'Evolution', 'Molecular biology', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 221 (§24.3.3)', 'Scientists estimate the time since two species shared a common ancestor by', '["counting their vestigial organs", "comparing their body sizes", "measuring how much they use each organ", "comparing differences in their DNA or proteins and assuming constant mutation rates"]', 3, 'Related organisms have similar DNA and proteins; the number of differences reflects time since divergence.', '{"0": "Vestigial organs are anatomical, not timing, evidence.", "1": "Size is not a molecular clock.", "2": "Use and disuse is Lamarckian."}'),
  ('BIO-0622', 'Evolution', 'Molecular biology', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 221 (§24.3.3)', 'Bacteria become resistant to an antibiotic over generations because', '["each bacterium learns to avoid the drug and passes it on", "a resistance gene arising by mutation is passed on through natural selection", "the antibiotic directly creates resistance genes", "the antibiotic stops all mutations"]', 1, 'Mutated resistance genes give survival under the drug and spread to later generations.', '{"0": "That would be a Lamarckian explanation.", "2": "Antibiotics select; they do not design genes.", "3": "Mutations continue to occur."}'),
  ('BIO-0623', 'Evolution', 'Endosymbiotic theory', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 222 (§24.4)', 'The endosymbiotic theory of eukaryote origin was proposed by', '["Charles Darwin", "Jean-Baptiste Lamarck", "Lynn Margulis", "Alfred Wallace"]', 2, 'Large prokaryotes engulfed small ones that lived inside them and became organelles.', '{"0": "Darwin proposed natural selection.", "1": "Lamarck proposed inheritance of acquired characters.", "3": "Wallace co-developed natural selection."}'),
  ('BIO-0624', 'Evolution', 'Endosymbiotic theory', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 222 (§24.4)', 'According to the book, prokaryotes first appeared about ___ years ago and eukaryotes evolved about ___ years ago.', '["2 billion; 3.5 billion", "3.5 million; 2 million", "1 billion; 500 million", "3.5 billion; 2 billion"]', 3, 'Prokaryotes arose about 3.5 billion years ago; eukaryotes about 2 billion years ago.', '{"0": "This reverses the order.", "1": "These are millions, not billions.", "2": "Not the figures given."}'),
  ('BIO-0625', 'Evolution', 'Endosymbiotic theory', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 222 (§24.4)', 'In the endosymbiotic theory, small engulfed prokaryotes that could carry out aerobic respiration became', '["mitochondria", "chloroplasts", "the nucleus", "the endoplasmic reticulum"]', 0, 'Aerobic prokaryotes became mitochondria; photosynthetic ones became chloroplasts.', '{"1": "Chloroplasts came from photosynthetic prokaryotes.", "2": "The nucleus is explained by membrane invagination.", "3": "ER is explained by membrane invagination."}'),
  ('BIO-0626', 'Evolution', 'Endosymbiotic theory', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 222 (§24.4)', 'Which feature of mitochondria supports the endosymbiotic theory?', '["they divide by mitosis like eukaryotic cells", "they are much larger than bacteria", "they divide by binary fission like prokaryotes", "their membranes match the eukaryotic plasma membrane"]', 2, 'Mitochondria are bacteria-sized, have their own circular DNA, divide by binary fission and have prokaryote-like membranes.', '{"0": "Eukaryotic cells divide by mitosis; mitochondria do not.", "1": "They are about the size of prokaryotes.", "3": "Their membranes resemble prokaryotic ones."}'),
  ('BIO-0627', 'Evolution', 'Endosymbiotic theory', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 222 (§24.4)', 'The membrane invagination theory explains the origin of', '["mitochondria from engulfed aerobic bacteria", "the nucleus and endoplasmic reticulum from infolded cell membrane", "chloroplasts from engulfed photosynthetic bacteria", "flagella from engulfed spirochete-like bacteria"]', 1, 'Infolding of the prokaryotic cell membrane produced internal membranous structures.', '{"0": "That is endosymbiosis.", "2": "That is endosymbiosis.", "3": "That is also proposed under endosymbiosis."}'),
  ('BIO-0628', 'Evolution', 'Lamarckism', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 223 (§24.5)', 'Lamarck presented his ideas on evolution in 1809 in the book', '["On the Origin of Species", "Principles of Geology", "Systema Naturae", "Philosophie Zoologique"]', 3, 'Lamarck (1744–1829) proposed use and disuse and inheritance of acquired characters.', '{"0": "That is Darwin''s book (1859).", "1": "Not Lamarck''s book.", "2": "Not Lamarck''s book."}'),
  ('BIO-0629', 'Evolution', 'Lamarckism', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 223 (§24.5)', 'In Lamarck''s theory, a body part that grows stronger because it is used often during an organism''s life is an', '["acquired character", "adaptive trait selected by nature", "homologous structure", "example of gene flow"]', 0, 'Changes due to long-term use or disuse were called acquired characters.', '{"1": "Adaptive traits are Darwin''s idea and are inherited genetic variations.", "2": "Homology is an anatomical comparison between species.", "3": "Gene flow is migration of alleles."}'),
  ('BIO-0630', 'Evolution', 'Lamarckism', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 224 (§24.5)', 'In the Lamarckian explanation of the giraffe''s long neck, the key step is that', '["giraffes born with longer necks by mutation survived better", "short-necked giraffes migrated away", "neck length changed only by chance", "necks stretched during life were passed on as longer necks to offspring"]', 3, 'Lamarck held that the acquired character (a stretched neck) was inherited.', '{"0": "That is the Darwinian explanation.", "1": "Migration is not part of his account.", "2": "Chance changes describe genetic drift."}'),
  ('BIO-0631', 'Evolution', 'Lamarckism', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 224 (§24.5.1)', 'Lamarckism was later rejected mainly because', '["it said evolution does not happen", "acquired characters have no genetic basis and cannot be inherited", "it relied on genetic drift", "it was based on molecular evidence"]', 1, 'Also, organs are not modified by the wish or need of an organism.', '{"0": "Lamarck supported evolution.", "2": "Drift was not part of his theory.", "3": "Molecular evidence came much later."}'),
  ('BIO-0632', 'Evolution', 'Lamarckism', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 224 (§24.5.1)', 'According to the book, in his own time Lamarck was criticized more for', '["advocating the idea of evolutionary change than for his proposed mechanism", "his mechanism than for the idea of evolution", "his work on the endosymbiotic theory", "his mathematical errors"]', 0, 'At that time there was no evidence explaining a mechanism, so the idea of change itself drew criticism.', '{"1": "The book says the opposite.", "2": "Endosymbiosis was proposed by Margulis much later.", "3": "Not mentioned."}'),
  ('BIO-0633', 'Ecology', 'Levels of organization', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 228 (§25.1)', 'A group of organisms that can interbreed to produce fertile offspring is a', '["community", "biome", "ecosystem", "species"]', 3, 'The species is the basic unit of classification; the Indus River dolphin is the book''s example.', '{"0": "A community contains many species.", "1": "A biome is a large climatic region.", "2": "An ecosystem includes abiotic factors too."}'),
  ('BIO-0634', 'Ecology', 'Levels of organization', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 228 (§25.1)', 'All the markhors living in Chitral Gol National Park make up a', '["species", "community", "population", "biome"]', 2, 'A population is one species in one area at one time; markhors across all of Pakistan would be the species.', '{"0": "The species includes markhors everywhere.", "1": "A community includes other species too.", "3": "A biome is a large climatic region."}'),
  ('BIO-0635', 'Ecology', 'Levels of organization', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 229 (§25.1)', 'The Thar Desert, considered together with its sand dunes, heat and rare rainfall as well as its snakes, blackbucks and shrubs, is an example of', '["an ecosystem", "a population", "a community", "a species"]', 0, 'An ecosystem combines the biotic community with abiotic factors.', '{"1": "A population is one species only.", "2": "A community is only the living part.", "3": "A species is one type of organism."}'),
  ('BIO-0636', 'Ecology', 'Levels of organization', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 229 (§25.1)', 'In an ecosystem, which statement correctly contrasts energy and nutrients?', '["nutrients flow one way while energy cycles", "energy flows one way while nutrients cycle", "both energy and nutrients cycle", "neither energy nor nutrients move"]', 1, 'Energy enters as sunlight and is lost as heat; elements are recycled between living and non-living parts.', '{"0": "This reverses the two.", "2": "Energy is not recycled.", "3": "Both move through the ecosystem."}'),
  ('BIO-0637', 'Ecology', 'Levels of organization', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 229 (§25.1)', 'The highest and most inclusive level of ecological organization, the global sum of all biomes, is the', '["biome", "community", "biosphere", "ecosystem"]', 2, 'The biosphere is every part of Earth where life exists, sometimes called the global ecosystem.', '{"0": "A biome is one large region.", "1": "A community is in one area.", "3": "An ecosystem is local, not global."}'),
  ('BIO-0638', 'Ecology', 'Levels of organization', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 229 (§25.1)', 'Murree and Swat, with cold winters and pine trees, are given as examples of which biome in Pakistan?', '["coniferous forest biome", "desert biome", "freshwater biome", "mangrove biome"]', 0, 'High-altitude cold areas dominated by pines form the coniferous forest biome.', '{"1": "Thal and Thar are the desert biome.", "2": "The Indus River system is a freshwater biome.", "3": "Mangroves are coastal."}'),
  ('BIO-0639', 'Ecology', 'Biogeochemical cycles', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 230 (§25.2)', 'Biogeochemical cycles are classified as gaseous or sedimentary on the basis of their', '["speed of cycling", "number of organisms involved", "trophic level", "primary reservoir"]', 3, 'Gaseous cycles have air or oceans as reservoir; sedimentary cycles have Earth''s crust.', '{"0": "Speed is not the basis given.", "1": "Not the basis given.", "2": "Trophic levels relate to energy flow."}'),
  ('BIO-0640', 'Ecology', 'Biogeochemical cycles', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 230 (§25.2)', 'Which of these is a sedimentary cycle?', '["the nitrogen cycle", "the phosphorus cycle", "the water cycle", "the carbon cycle"]', 1, 'Sedimentary cycles (phosphorus, sulphur, calcium) have Earth''s crust as reservoir.', '{"0": "Nitrogen is a gaseous cycle.", "2": "Water is a gaseous cycle.", "3": "Carbon is a gaseous cycle."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-0641', 'Ecology', 'Water cycle', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 231 (§25.2.1)', 'The energy that drives the water cycle comes from', '["the sun", "the Earth''s core", "lightning", "decomposers"]', 0, 'Solar radiation heats surface water so it evaporates.', '{"1": "Not the source described.", "2": "Lightning fixes nitrogen.", "3": "Decomposers release small amounts of water only."}'),
  ('BIO-0642', 'Ecology', 'Water cycle', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 231 (§25.2.1)', 'About what share of global evaporation takes place from the oceans?', '["14%", "50%", "86%", "99%"]', 2, 'The oceans supply most water vapour; for Pakistan the Arabian Sea is the main source.', '{"0": "Too low.", "1": "Too low.", "3": "Too high."}'),
  ('BIO-0643', 'Ecology', 'Water cycle', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 231 (§25.2.1)', 'Water that seeps into the soil and moves down to the water table, recharging groundwater, is undergoing', '["surface runoff", "transpiration", "condensation", "infiltration"]', 3, 'Infiltration (percolation) stores water underground; runoff flows over land into rivers.', '{"0": "Runoff flows over the surface.", "1": "Transpiration releases vapour from leaves.", "2": "Condensation forms clouds."}'),
  ('BIO-0644', 'Ecology', 'Water cycle', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 231 (§25.2.1)', 'The major route by which water leaves the biotic component and returns to the atmosphere is', '["excretion in urine", "transpiration through leaf stomata", "egestion in faeces", "decomposition of dead bodies"]', 1, 'The book calls transpiration the major exit point for water.', '{"0": "Urine is a smaller route.", "2": "Faeces are a smaller route.", "3": "Decomposition is a smaller route."}'),
  ('BIO-0645', 'Ecology', 'Water cycle', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 231 (§25.2.1)', 'Water produced by cellular respiration and exhaled as vapour is called', '["groundwater", "surface runoff", "metabolic water", "brackish water"]', 2, 'Respiration in plants and animals produces water as a by-product.', '{"0": "Groundwater is stored below the water table.", "1": "Runoff flows over land.", "3": "Brackish water mixes fresh and sea water."}'),
  ('BIO-0646', 'Ecology', 'Nitrogen cycle', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 232 (§25.2.2)', 'Plants cannot use atmospheric N2 directly because', '["its two atoms are held by a strong triple bond", "it is only 1% of the atmosphere", "it is a solid at room temperature", "it is toxic to plant cells"]', 0, 'Nitrogen fixation is needed to convert N2 into usable compounds such as ammonia.', '{"1": "N2 is about 78% of the atmosphere.", "2": "N2 is a gas.", "3": "N2 is not toxic."}'),
  ('BIO-0647', 'Ecology', 'Nitrogen cycle', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 232 (§25.2.2)', 'More than 90% of all nitrogen fixation is carried out by', '["lightning", "soil microorganisms", "industrial fertilizer plants", "animals"]', 1, 'Biological fixation by free-living and symbiotic microbes dominates.', '{"0": "Lightning fixes only about 5–10%.", "2": "Industrial fixation is not the major share stated.", "3": "Animals cannot fix nitrogen."}'),
  ('BIO-0648', 'Ecology', 'Nitrogen cycle', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 232 (§25.2.2)', 'Which nitrogen fixer lives symbiotically in the root nodules of legumes such as chickpea?', '["Azotobacter", "Clostridium", "Nitrobacter", "Rhizobium"]', 3, 'Rhizobium is mutualistic; Azotobacter, Clostridium and cyanobacteria are free-living fixers.', '{"0": "Azotobacter is free-living.", "1": "Clostridium is free-living.", "2": "Nitrobacter is a nitrifying bacterium."}'),
  ('BIO-0649', 'Ecology', 'Nitrogen cycle', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 232 (§25.2.2)', 'Atmospheric nitrogen fixation happens when', '["bacteria in root nodules make ammonia", "lightning supplies energy to combine nitrogen with oxygen", "nitrogen and hydrogen are combined in factories", "decomposers break down proteins"]', 1, 'N2 becomes nitrogen oxides and then nitrates; this fixes only 5–10% of nitrogen.', '{"0": "That is biological fixation.", "2": "That is industrial fixation.", "3": "That is ammonification."}'),
  ('BIO-0650', 'Ecology', 'Nitrogen cycle', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 233 (§25.2.2)', 'In nitrification, Nitrobacter converts', '["nitrite to nitrate", "ammonia to nitrite", "nitrate to nitrogen gas", "nitrogen gas to ammonia"]', 0, 'Nitrosomonas makes nitrite from ammonia; Nitrobacter makes nitrate from nitrite.', '{"1": "That step is done by Nitrosomonas.", "2": "That is denitrification.", "3": "That is nitrogen fixation."}'),
  ('BIO-0651', 'Ecology', 'Nitrogen cycle', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 233 (§25.2.2)', 'Decomposers converting the organic nitrogen of dead bodies and wastes back into ammonia is called', '["assimilation", "nitrification", "denitrification", "ammonification"]', 3, 'Fungi and bacteria break down proteins and release ammonia.', '{"0": "Assimilation is uptake of nitrates into living things.", "1": "Nitrification oxidizes ammonia to nitrate.", "2": "Denitrification releases N2."}'),
  ('BIO-0652', 'Ecology', 'Nitrogen cycle', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 233 (§25.2.2)', 'Plants absorbing nitrates from the soil to make proteins, DNA and chlorophyll is the step called', '["ammonification", "nitrogen fixation", "assimilation", "denitrification"]', 2, 'Animals assimilate nitrogen by eating plants and other animals.', '{"0": "Ammonification returns organic N to ammonia.", "1": "Fixation converts N2 to ammonia.", "3": "Denitrification returns N2 to air."}'),
  ('BIO-0653', 'Ecology', 'Nitrogen cycle', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 234 (§25.2.2)', 'Denitrification, returning nitrogen gas to the air, occurs mainly in', '["well-aerated sandy soils", "the root nodules of legumes", "waterlogged soils", "clouds during lightning"]', 2, 'In waterlogged (oxygen-poor) soils, denitrifying bacteria turn nitrates into N2.', '{"0": "Not the condition described.", "1": "Root nodules fix nitrogen.", "3": "Lightning fixes nitrogen."}'),
  ('BIO-0654', 'Ecology', 'Trophic levels', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 234 (§25.3.1)', 'Herbivores such as rabbits and many insects occupy', '["trophic level 1", "trophic level 3", "trophic level 4", "trophic level 2"]', 3, 'Level 1 producers; level 2 primary consumers; level 3 secondary consumers; level 4 tertiary consumers.', '{"0": "Level 1 is producers.", "1": "Level 3 is carnivores such as frogs and snakes.", "2": "Level 4 is animals that eat carnivores."}'),
  ('BIO-0655', 'Ecology', 'Trophic levels', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 234 (§25.3.1)', 'Decomposers and detritivores are placed in a separate trophic level because they', '["carry out photosynthesis", "feed on dead material from all trophic levels", "eat only herbivores", "are eaten only by tertiary consumers"]', 1, 'They consume detritus and faecal matter from every level.', '{"0": "Producers photosynthesize.", "2": "That describes secondary consumers.", "3": "Not the reason given."}'),
  ('BIO-0656', 'Ecology', 'Energy flow', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 234 (§25.3.2)', 'Of the solar energy falling on producers, the share converted into the bond energy of organic matter is about', '["1–2%", "10%", "50%", "90%"]', 0, 'Only 1–2% of sunlight is fixed by producers; about 10% then passes between later levels.', '{"1": "10% is the transfer between trophic levels.", "2": "Far too high.", "3": "Far too high."}'),
  ('BIO-0657', 'Ecology', 'Energy flow', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 235 (§25.3.2)', 'If producers fix 50,000 kcal, roughly how much reaches tertiary consumers, using the 10% transfer rule?', '["500 kcal", "50 kcal", "5,000 kcal", "5 kcal"]', 1, '50,000 → 5,000 (primary) → 500 (secondary) → 50 (tertiary).', '{"0": "500 kcal reaches secondary consumers.", "2": "5,000 kcal reaches primary consumers.", "3": "5 kcal would be a fifth level."}'),
  ('BIO-0658', 'Ecology', 'Energy flow', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 235 (§25.3.2)', 'Short food chains are more energy-efficient than long ones because', '["energy is lost as heat at every transfer, so fewer steps waste less", "energy is recycled in short chains", "producers fix more energy in short chains", "decomposers are absent in short chains"]', 0, 'Each trophic step loses most energy as heat.', '{"1": "Energy is never recycled.", "2": "Chain length does not change what producers fix.", "3": "Decomposers are present in all ecosystems."}'),
  ('BIO-0659', 'Ecology', 'Population characteristics', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 235 (§25.4)', 'The statistical study of the characteristics of populations is called', '["biogeography", "ecology", "taxonomy", "demography"]', 3, 'Population size is described as its most important feature.', '{"0": "Biogeography studies distribution of organisms.", "1": "Ecology is broader.", "2": "Taxonomy is classification."}'),
  ('BIO-0660', 'Ecology', 'Survivorship', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 236 (§25.4)', 'Humans and many large mammals, where most individuals live to old age and then die rapidly, show', '["a type II (diagonal) curve", "a type III (concave) curve", "a type I (convex) survivorship curve", "an exponential growth curve"]', 2, 'Type I: low mortality until old age.', '{"0": "Type II is a constant death rate (birds, lizards, rodents).", "1": "Type III is high juvenile mortality (fishes).", "3": "That is a growth model, not survivorship."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-0661', 'Ecology', 'Survivorship', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 236 (§25.4)', 'A fish population in which most young die soon after hatching but adults survive well shows', '["a type I (convex) curve", "a type III (concave) survivorship curve", "a type II (diagonal) curve", "a logistic growth curve"]', 1, 'Type III has very high juvenile mortality, seen in fishes and many invertebrates.', '{"0": "Type I has low early mortality.", "2": "Type II has a constant death rate.", "3": "That is a growth model."}'),
  ('BIO-0662', 'Ecology', 'Population growth', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 236 (§25.4)', 'Logistic population growth gives an S-shaped curve because', '["the population grows without limit", "the population adds the same number each generation forever", "survivorship is always type I", "growth slows as resources run short and the population levels off at carrying capacity"]', 3, 'Shortage of space, light, water or nutrients stabilizes the population.', '{"0": "That is exponential growth.", "1": "Neither model is described that way.", "2": "Survivorship type is unrelated."}'),
  ('BIO-0663', 'Ecology', 'Population growth', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 236 (§25.4)', 'In the exponential growth model, a population increases by', '["a constant number of individuals each generation", "smaller amounts until it reaches carrying capacity", "the same ratio per unit time, without limits", "chance alone"]', 2, 'Exponential growth is proportional, not additive.', '{"0": "The book contrasts it with adding a constant number.", "1": "That is logistic growth.", "3": "Chance is not the model."}'),
  ('BIO-0664', 'Ecology', 'Population density', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 236 (§25.4)', 'The number of E. coli cells per millilitre in a test tube is a measure of', '["population density", "carrying capacity", "survivorship", "population dispersion"]', 0, 'Density is individuals per unit area or volume.', '{"1": "Carrying capacity is the maximum supportable number.", "2": "Survivorship is the share alive at an age.", "3": "Dispersion is spacing pattern."}'),
  ('BIO-0665', 'Ecology', 'Population dispersion', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 237 (§25.4)', 'Nesting penguins that defend territories are spaced', '["randomly", "uniformly", "in clumps", "exponentially"]', 1, 'Territorial behaviour, like plants inhibiting neighbours, gives uniform spacing.', '{"0": "Random spacing occurs when individuals do not interact strongly.", "2": "Clumping follows uneven resources or group living.", "3": "Not a dispersion pattern."}'),
  ('BIO-0666', 'Ecology', 'Population dispersion', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 237 (§25.4)', 'The most common dispersion pattern in nature is', '["random spacing", "uniform spacing", "linear spacing", "clumped spacing"]', 3, 'Organisms gather where resources are, and many animals live in groups.', '{"0": "Random spacing is described as not common.", "1": "Uniform spacing is limited to cases like territoriality.", "2": "Not one of the patterns described."}'),
  ('BIO-0667', 'Ecology', 'Carrying capacity', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 237 (§25.4)', 'The maximum number of individuals of a species that an environment can support sustainably is set by', '["limiting factors such as food, water, shelter, predators and disease", "the survivorship type alone", "the dispersion pattern alone", "the number of trophic levels alone"]', 0, 'Carrying capacity depends on the limiting factors of the environment.', '{"1": "Survivorship describes death patterns.", "2": "Dispersion describes spacing.", "3": "Trophic levels describe energy flow."}'),
  ('BIO-0668', 'Ecology', 'Minimum viable population', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 237 (§25.4)', 'A population of Himalayan brown bears that has fallen below its minimal viable size is at high risk mainly because', '["its carrying capacity becomes infinite", "it grows exponentially", "inbreeding weakens it and a single disaster could wipe it out", "it switches to type I survivorship"]', 2, 'Small populations suffer inbreeding, lower disease resistance and vulnerability to one flood or fire.', '{"0": "Carrying capacity is never infinite.", "1": "Tiny populations do not grow freely.", "3": "Survivorship type is not the issue."}'),
  ('BIO-0669', 'Ecology', 'Human population', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 238 (§25.4.1)', 'According to the 2023 census reported in the book, Pakistan''s population was about', '["8 billion", "24 million", "2.4 billion", "241 million"]', 3, 'The Pakistan Bureau of Statistics recorded 241.49 million, with growth about 2.55% a year.', '{"0": "8 billion is the world population.", "1": "Ten times too low.", "2": "Ten times too high."}'),
  ('BIO-0670', 'Ecology', 'Greenhouse effect', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 238 (§25.5)', 'Greenhouse gases warm the Earth because they', '["block all sunlight from reaching the surface", "let sunlight through but stop heat radiated from the surface escaping to space", "produce heat by burning in the atmosphere", "destroy the ozone layer only"]', 1, 'This natural effect keeps Earth warm enough for life; excess GHGs enhance it.', '{"0": "Sunlight passes through.", "2": "They trap heat, not produce it.", "3": "Ozone depletion is a separate issue."}'),
  ('BIO-0671', 'Ecology', 'Greenhouse effect', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 239 (§25.5)', 'Enteric fermentation in cattle is a major source of which greenhouse gas?', '["methane", "nitrous oxide", "chlorofluorocarbons", "carbon dioxide from fossil fuels"]', 0, 'Methane comes from livestock, agriculture and coal and gas production.', '{"1": "N2O comes from agriculture, industry and combustion.", "2": "CFCs are synthetic industrial gases.", "3": "Fossil fuel burning releases CO2, not the cattle source."}'),
  ('BIO-0672', 'Ecology', 'Greenhouse effect', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 239 (§25.5)', 'Which greenhouse gas group is entirely synthetic, released from old refrigerators and air conditioners?', '["methane", "nitrous oxide", "fluorinated gases such as chlorofluorocarbons", "carbon dioxide"]', 2, 'Fluorinated gases come only from industrial processes.', '{"0": "Methane also has natural sources.", "1": "N2O also has natural and agricultural sources.", "3": "CO2 has many natural sources."}'),
  ('BIO-0673', 'Ecology', 'Climate effects', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 240 (§25.5)', 'A sudden release of water from a glacial lake when its natural ice dam breaks is called', '["a cloudburst", "surface runoff", "a glacial lake outburst flood (GLOF)", "desertification"]', 2, 'Rising temperatures melt glaciers such as Baltoro and Batura and cause GLOFs.', '{"0": "A cloudburst is extremely heavy rain over a small area.", "1": "Runoff is normal flow over land.", "3": "Desertification is land drying into desert."}'),
  ('BIO-0674', 'Ecology', 'Climate effects', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 240 (§25.5)', 'Seawater intrusion in Thatta and Badin, turning farmland into salty marshes, results from', '["sea-level rise as polar ice melts and seawater expands", "glacial lake outburst floods", "heatwaves in Jacobabad", "denitrification in waterlogged soils"]', 0, 'Sea-level rise causes coastal erosion and salinization.', '{"1": "GLOFs affect mountain valleys.", "2": "Heatwaves are a different effect.", "3": "Denitrification is part of the nitrogen cycle."}'),
  ('BIO-0675', 'Ecology', 'Ecosystems of Pakistan', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 241 (§25.6.1)', 'Yew, blue pine and Himalayan cedar, with rhesus monkeys and black bears, are typical of Pakistan''s', '["tropical thorn forest", "temperate deciduous forest", "mangrove forest", "alpine meadows"]', 1, 'Found in Shogran, Neelam Valley, Murree and Kaghan with 750–1500 mm rain.', '{"0": "Thorn forest has Jhand, Peelu, Karir and Kikar.", "2": "Mangroves are dominated by Avicennia marina.", "3": "Alpine meadows have stunted junipers and birch."}'),
  ('BIO-0676', 'Ecology', 'Ecosystems of Pakistan', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 242 (§25.6.2)', 'The most widespread terrestrial ecosystem of Pakistan''s plains, with drought-resistant thorny plants, is the', '["temperate deciduous forest", "alpine meadow", "mangrove forest", "tropical thorn forest"]', 3, 'It covers much of the Indus plains with 200–750 mm rain; most of it has been cleared for settlement and canal irrigation.', '{"0": "That is in moist northern mountains.", "1": "Those are high-altitude cold grasslands.", "2": "Mangroves are coastal."}'),
  ('BIO-0677', 'Ecology', 'Ecosystems of Pakistan', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 243 (§25.6.3)', 'Large trees cannot grow in Pakistan''s alpine and sub-alpine meadows mainly because', '["the soil is too salty", "the climate is too cold and harsh", "rainfall is too heavy", "herbivores eat all seedlings"]', 1, 'These high-altitude "cold deserts" have mean annual temperatures of 1–10 °C and heavy snow.', '{"0": "Salty soils are a coastal feature.", "2": "Precipitation falls mostly as snow.", "3": "Not the reason given."}'),
  ('BIO-0678', 'Ecology', 'Ecosystems of Pakistan', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 244 (§25.6.4)', 'In Pakistan''s mangrove forests, about 95% of the trees are', '["Avicennia marina (Timmar)", "Vachellia nilotica (Kikar)", "Cedrus deodara (deodar)", "Betula utilis (birch)"]', 0, 'Mangroves grow in brackish water on oxygen-poor mudflats and have breathing roots.', '{"1": "Kikar is a thorn-forest tree.", "2": "Deodar is a temperate forest tree.", "3": "Birch grows in alpine areas."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

